window.DSH_DAILY_DATA = {"updatedAt":"2026-11-18 22:38 CST","dates":["2026-11-18"],"batches":{"2026-11-18":{"papers":[{"id":"2611.21997","title":"Fundamental physics with quantum sensors in space","summary":"Quantum sensors such as atomic clocks, atom interferometers, and atomic magnetometers can be used as sensitive probes of physics beyond the Standard Model. Space offers microgravity, long baselines, low environmental noise, access to varying gravitational potentials, and unique opportunities involving the Sun, Moon, planets, and asteroids. These proceedings of the Aspen Center for Physics workshop on \"Fundamental physics in space with quantum sensors,\" held February 22-27, 2026, summarize the scientific case, current capabilities, and space mission concepts for investigating dark matter, testing general relativity, and detecting gravitational waves in frequency bands inaccessible from the ground.","authors":"Jason Arakawa, Daniel J. Blumenthal, Oliver Buchmueller, Dmitry Budker, Luigi Cacciapuoti, Emily D. Caldwell, Andrei Derevianko, Benjamin P. Dix-Matthews, Michael A. Fedderke, Naceur Gaaloul, Susan Gardner, Jonah Glick, Paul Hamilton, Elena Jordan, Hidetoshi Katori, Peter Kurczynski, David R. Leibrandt, Anna L. Marchant, Indra Periwal, Igor Pikovski, Marianna S. Safronova, Christian Sanner, Sascha W. Schediwy, Volodymyr Takhistov, Yu-Cheng Tien, Yu-Dai Tsai, Jason R. Williams","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.IM","gr-qc","hep-ph","physics.atom-ph","quant-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle","sensing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21997"},{"id":"2611.22481","title":"Entanglement in restricted experimental setups","summary":"Entanglement is a necessary resource for steering and Bell nonlocality, yet whether a state is separable takes no account of what an experiment can prepare or measure. We define two notions of separability relative to an experiment, one on either side of separability. Inner separability confines the local states to the preparable sets. Outer separability lets the local factors be self-adjoint operators positive on the available effects but not necessarily positive semidefinite. We find exactly when each departs from separability and that outer separability keeps the operational content of separability for the available measurements: outer separable states, entangled or not, admit local hidden variable models for every behaviour these measurements generate and, when the steered party is unrestricted, local hidden state models for every assemblage the steering party's measurements generate. Such hidden state models do not imply outer separability in general, but we identify conditions under which they do.","authors":"Rafiul Nakib, Martin Pl\\'avala","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["foundations"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22481"},{"id":"2611.22029","title":"Exact Dynamic Scaling of Quantum Jump Statistics in Monitored Many-Body Systems","summary":"Quantum measurement has opened a new frontier in nonequilibrium many-body physics, revealing striking phenomena such as measurement-induced entanglement transitions, whose experimental observation typically requires costly postselection. Quantum jump statistics offer another route to studying such measurement-induced many-body phenomena without postselection, motivating the search for universality in measurement outcomes themselves. In this Letter, we discover that fluctuation of the number of quantum jumps within a subsystem exhibits novel dynamic scaling, characterized by diffusive time scale and anomalous super-Poissonian system-size scaling that depend on the subsystem size in monitored many-body systems. For free fermions under continuous monitoring, we exactly determine the scaling exponent and the scaling function by deriving an exact finite-size solution of the Liouvillian autocorrelation function. We also analytically show that the dynamic scaling with the same scaling exponent and the scaling function even holds for interacting fermions in the limit of large measurement strength. Our work provides a novel framework to classify measurement-induced nonequilibrium many-body universality through experimentally friendly, postselection-free quantities.","authors":"Kazuki Yamamoto, Taiki Ishiyama, Toranosuke Matsubara, Kazuya Fujimoto, Tomohiro Sasamoto, Ryusuke Hamazaki","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["cond-mat.stat-mech","cond-mat.quant-gas","cond-mat.str-el","quant-ph"],"topics":["foundations"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22029"},{"id":"2507.15453","title":"Entanglement preservation and Clauser-Horne nonlocality in electromagnetically induced transparency quantum memories","summary":"Understanding how decoherence-induced loss affects entanglement and nonlocal correlations in quantum memories is important for quantum information science. Here we develop a quantitative description of photonic quantum states retrieved from electromagnetically induced transparency (EIT) memories in a ground-state-decoherence-limited regime. Using the dark-state-polariton formalism, we derive the attenuation factor of the retrieved probe mode from the EIT dynamics. This attenuation depends on the coupling field and ground-state coherence decay and is not introduced as an independent phenomenological loss parameter. Within a specified quantum-limited pure-loss model, we use this attenuation to obtain the reduced photonic density operator. We show that, in the symmetric case, the complete retrieved state of an initially path-entangled single photon remains entangled for any nonzero storage efficiency, despite acquiring a vacuum component. In contrast, violation of the unconditional Clauser-Horne inequality under displaced on/off detection without post-selection requires an intrinsic efficiency above 89.7%. We further derive the retrieved N-qubit photonic density operator for multiple independently operating EIT memories. These results establish a quantitative connection between EIT memory dynamics, retrieved quantum states, and operational nonlocality benchmarks under explicit channel assumptions.","authors":"Po-Han Tseng, Yong-Fan Chen","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["foundations"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.15453"},{"id":"2510.20096","title":"All-Gaussian State Discrimination Beyond the Coherent Helstrom Bound","summary":"A core problem in communications is the optimal discrimination of binary-phase-shift-keyed (BPSK) signals. A longstanding goal has been to reach the fundamental quantum limit, known as the Helstrom bound, for BPSK signals encoded in coherent states. However, due to technical constraints, proposals for reaching the bound remain impractical. In this letter we take an alternative approach: using only Gaussian optics - displaced squeezed states and homodyne detection - we achieve discrimination of BPSK signals with error rates below what can be achieved using coherent states and any quantum measurement.","authors":"Angus Walsh, Lorcan Conlon, Biveen Shajilal, Ozlem Erkilic, Jiri Janousek, Syed Assad, Jie Zhao, Ping Koy Lam","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["foundations"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20096"},{"id":"2611.08151","title":"Machine learning quantum data to accelerate quantum Monte Carlo","summary":"Simulating strongly correlated electrons in molecules and materials remains a central goal for quantum computing. A promising strategy uses quantum processors to prepare trial wavefunctions that guide classical quantum Monte Carlo (QMC). However, the enormous computational cost of repeatedly processing quantum measurements has severely limited the practical scalability of this approach. Here, we overcome this bottleneck by compressing quantum measurement data into machine-learning models that efficiently provide the wavefunction overlaps needed by QMC. This accelerates quantum-classical QMC by orders of magnitude, enabling chemically accurate simulations on up to 40 qubits, more than doubling the scale of previous hardware demonstrations with comparable accuracy. We demonstrate our approach on hydrogen chains and strongly correlated iron-sulfur clusters using measurements from Google's Willow processor. By enabling efficient reuse of quantum-state information on the classical coprocessor, our framework addresses a fundamental bottleneck in hybrid quantum algorithms that will persist even into the fault-tolerant era.","authors":"Tong Jiang, Andrew Zhao, Shashwat Kumar, Jigyasa Nigam, Eliott Rosenberg, Ryan Babbush, Andrew D. Baczewski, William J. Huggins, Joonho Lee","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph","physics.chem-ph"],"topics":["foundations","computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08151"},{"id":"2510.17423","title":"Energy calibration of bulk events in the BULLKID detector","summary":"BULLKID is a cryogenic, solid-state detector designed for direct searches of particle Dark Matter candidates, with mass $\\lesssim 1$ GeV/c$^2$, and coherent neutrino-nucleus scattering. It is based on an array of dice carved in 5 mm thick silicon crystal, sensed by phonon-mediated Kinetic Inductance Detectors. In previous works, the array was calibrated with bursts of optical photons, which are absorbed in the first hundreds nanometers of the dice and give rise to surface events. In this work, we present the reconstruction of bulk events through the 59.5 keV $\\gamma$-ray generated by an $^{241}$Am source, which emulates more closely the interaction of Dark Matter and neutrinos. The peak resolution is $5\\%~(\\sigma)$ and its position is shifted by less than $10\\%$ with respect to the optical calibration. We observe that the resolution is further improved by a factor $2$ combining the signal from neighboring dice. These results confirm the performance of the detector in view of the physics goals of the BULLKID-DM experiment for dark matter search.","authors":"M. Folcarelli, D. Delicato, A. Acevedo-Renter\\'ia, L. E. Ardila-Perez, L. Bandiera, M. Calvo, M. Cappelli, R. Caravita, F. Carillo, U. Chowdhury, D. Crovo, A. Cruciani, A. D'Addabbo, M. De Lucia, G. Del Castello, M. del Gallo Roccagiovine, F. Ferraro, S. Fu, R. Gartmann, M. Grassi, V. Guidi, D. Helis, T. Lari, L. Malagutti, A. Mazzolari, A. Monfardini, T. Muscheid, D. Nicol\\`o, F. Paolucci, D. Pasciuto, L. Pesce, C. Puglia, D. Quaranta, C. M. A. Roda, S. Roddaro, M. Romagnoni, G. Signorelli, F. Simon, A. Tartari, E. V\\'azquez-J\\'auregui, M. Vignati, K. Zhao","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["physics.ins-det"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.17423"},{"id":"2611.21714","title":"21 cm Cosmology of Boosted Dark Matter","summary":"The formation history of low-mass halos is sensitive to the properties of dark matter. At low redshift, nonlinear evolution and baryonic processes can obscure this information. The 21 cm signal provides access to a high-redshift stage of structure formation, when the abundance and clustering of low-mass halos retain direct sensitivity to the mass and interactions of dark matter. Boosted dark matter (BDM) realizes this possibility through a two-component scenario in which the annihilation of the heavier component converts part of the mass difference into kinetic energy of the lighter component. Self-interactions redistribute this energy and generate pressure support, suppressing small-scale density perturbations even for dark matter masses in the MeV range. We calculate the global 21 cm brightness temperature and its power spectrum in BDM cosmology. The reduced abundance of low-mass halos delays the global absorption feature and shifts the characteristic redshift evolution of the power spectrum. We assess the projected sensitivity of SKA1-Low to these signatures. The sensitivity is strongest at intermediate light-component fractions, where both components remain sufficiently abundant for self-heating to affect structure formation. For $\\sigma_{\\rm self1}/m_1\\simeq0.1\\,{\\rm cm^2\\,g^{-1}}$, models with $0.2\\lesssim r_1\\lesssim0.7$ can be distinguished from the fiducial $\\Lambda$CDM prediction at the $95\\%$ confidence level under the optimistic forecast. We compare our results with existing direct-detection bounds from SENSEI and prospects for BDM searches at large-volume neutrino experiments, including Super-/Hyper-Kamiokande, JUNO, and DUNE. This comparison highlights the unique and complementary role of high-redshift surveys in testing BDM models.","authors":"Min Sub Lee, Jeong Han Kim, Kyoungchul Kong, Jong-Chul Park","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","astro-ph.HE","hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21714"},{"id":"2611.21719","title":"Feebly-Interacting Particle Searches","summary":"In the last several years, feebly-interacting particles (FIPs) have moved from \"exotica\" to the mainstream of particle physics. This development is fueled by three motivations: FIPs are one of two broad possibilities for new particles, FIPs are as well motivated by dark matter as WIMPs, and FIPs are connected to important problems in particle physics. I review these motivations and then briefly highlight recent progress in a few areas, including axion-like particles, dark photons and other gauge bosons, the X17 anomaly, quirks, and the Forward Physics Facility.","authors":"Jonathan L. Feng","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21719"},{"id":"2611.21728","title":"Plugging the Pion Chimney","summary":"Heavy QCD axions and other gluon-coupled axion-like particles (ALPs) with masses from MeV to GeV are well motivated and have been searched for extensively. However, a window around the neutral pion mass, the so-called pion chimney, has escaped every search. There the ALP mixes strongly with the $\\pi^0$ and therefore shares its experimental signatures. We show that the chimney can be closed with existing LHC data. The key point is that mixing changes the lifetimes of the two mass eigenstates but does not make them equal, and at LHC energies the states are boosted enough for the lifetime to decide whether they escape the detector or decay inside it. The ATLAS monojet search constrains the first case and the ATLAS inclusive photon measurement the second, and together they exclude the chimney up to the cutoff of the ALP effective theory. Above the cutoff, LHC searches for the colored states of the simplest ultraviolet completions exclude the rest. We also show that the bound from the $K^+$ width does not reach the chimney, because an ALP passes the same selection as a $\\pi^0$, and we derive an independent, purely kinematic constraint from the measured $\\pi^\\pm$-$\\pi^0$ mass splitting.","authors":"Nikita Blinov, Andrew M. Evans, Shirley Weishi Li","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21728"},{"id":"2611.21736","title":"Active Galactic Nuclei as Beyond the Standard Model Laboratories: a White Paper","summary":"Multi-messenger and multi-wavelength observations of Active Galactic Nuclei (AGN) can provide otherwise inaccessible knowledge about physics Beyond the Standard Model (BSM), and may even lead to its discovery. Realizing this potential hinges on two parallel efforts: advancing our understanding of AGN and of the dark matter around them, and identifying the BSM signatures that are at once promising and robust against the gaps in that understanding. These fields have attracted growing interest in recent years, also motivated by the IceCube association of neutrinos to individual AGN, yet we have only begun to explore their cross-fertilization. This white paper offers a new perspective on this interdisciplinary endeavor. It concisely reviews the state of the art in each field and at their interface, and identifies the key open questions that must be addressed to strengthen our prospects for learning about BSM physics with AGN.","authors":"Gonzalo Herrera, Filippo Sala, Piero Ullio, Misty C. Bentz, Carlos Blanco, Roberto Caiozzo, Marina Cerme\\~no, Matteo Cerruti, James M. Cline, Andrea Giovanni De Marchi, Pierfrancesco Di Cintio, Alessandro Granelli, R. Andrew Gustafson, Bradley J. Kavanagh, Cristina Lagunas Gualda, Kohta Murase, Takuya Okawa, Xavier Rodrigues, Lorenzo Ubaldi, Gabrijela Zaharijas","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","astro-ph.GA","astro-ph.HE","hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21736"},{"id":"2611.21737","title":"Thermal Composite Dark Matter at LZ","summary":"We show that stable fermionic resonances of Composite Higgs models provide a thermal inelastic Dark Matter interpretation of the high-energy nuclear-recoil event LZ230616. Their stability follows from an exact accidental parity of the strong sector. Annihilation channels from composite interactions link the thermal relic mass to the strong coupling, allowing masses well beyond the pure electroweak prediction. We identify three scenarios in which the neutral states form a split pseudo-Dirac pair, distinguished by the origin of the inelastic coupling to the $Z$ boson. In the pure electroweak regime, the pair consists of the neutral components of a complex Higgsino-like doublet. Its unsuppressed inelastic $Z$ coupling requires Dark Matter masses above 20 TeV. In the pure strong regime, the suppressed $Z$ coupling of neutral states with vanishing hypercharge arises from strong interactions alone, allowing for smaller splittings and TeV-scale masses. In the third regime, which interpolates between the previous two, the neutral components of multiplets with different hypercharge are mixed. This enhances their inelastic coupling, accommodating the LZ event for masses of a few TeV and splittings around 300 keV, with mixing angles of order $10^\\circ$. All three cases provide a thermal Dark Matter candidate able to explain the LZ event, consistently with the solar-capture bounds, the LZ high-energy sideband, and gamma-ray indirect searches.","authors":"Dario Buttazzo, Andrea Di Lecce, Roberto Franceschini, Giulio Marino, Paolo Panci","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21737"},{"id":"2611.21764","title":"$B\\to K a$ decays in QCD factorization and revised bounds on ALP couplings","summary":"Rare flavor-changing processes such as $B\\to K a$ offer sensitive probes of axion-like particles (ALPs) for masses below a few GeV. We study this process in the framework of QCD factorization and ALP effective theory, in which the ALP couples to the Standard Model (SM) fermions through shift-invariant derivative interactions. In contrast to previous studies, where flavor violation arises solely from flavor-changing ALP couplings, we also include the contributions proportional to flavor-conserving ALP couplings, in which the flavor change is mediated by the weak interactions of the SM. Based on this analysis, we derive upper bounds on both flavor-changing and flavor-conserving ALP couplings at low energies. Then we consider the UV scenario of a \"minimally flavor-violating ALP\", which features flavor-universal ALP-fermion couplings at very high energies, and evaluate the resulting constraints on the UV couplings.","authors":"Upalaparna Banerjee, Cristhian Calderon, Matthias Neubert","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21764"},{"id":"2511.10731","title":"Keeping It Renormalizable: Minimal Baryogenesis induced Asymmetric Dark Matter","summary":"Many asymmetric dark matter scenarios have been proposed to date. Among them, perhaps the most motivated ones are those in which the dark matter asymmetry is induced from the baryon/lepton asymmetries via chemical equilibration without any new sources of CP violation. However, most of the models put forward along these lines have been excluded by now and/or are based on complicated setups. In this letter, we present a new, simple, and viable scenario. It assumes only two new fields: a scalar singlet and an inert scalar doublet, and is based only on renormalizable interactions, that slowly generate the dark matter asymmetry from the Standard Model Higgs asymmetry. The successful asymmetry transfer requires small (but technically natural) couplings which in turn make the model CP conserving in practice. The model allows for the direct detection of dark matter in the upcoming generation of experiments, and the inert doublet is predicted to be light enough to be potentially produced and observed at the LHC and future colliders, $46\\,{\\rm GeV}<m_{H'}<580\\,{\\rm GeV}$. Quite remarkably, the mass and lifetime of the inert doublet may be inferred from direct detection experiments, indicating long-lived compressed electroweak states that can be searched for at the HL-LHC.","authors":"Miguel Escudero, Thomas Hambye, Chandan Hati","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","hep-ex","hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.10731"},{"id":"2605.29132","title":"Residual Nonextensivity from WIMP Freeze-Out: A Phenomenological Memory Channel","summary":"In this work we formulate the thermal decoupling, or freeze-out, of weakly interacting massive particle dark matter within a dynamical Tsallis framework. The parameter $q_\\chi$ is promoted to a function of $x=m_\\chi/T$, describing a relaxation toward extensivity that need not be completed before chemical decoupling. We adopt a sectorial approximation during freeze-out, with an extensive Standard Model bath, and specify the statistical weights and effective kinetic closure used for the dark matter abundance. The total derivatives of the equilibrium density contain the variation of $q_\\chi$, which changes the rate at which the abundance must evolve to follow its instantaneous reference state. The residual value $q_\\chi^{\\rm fo}$ then provides the input for a phenomenological memory channel into the electron-positron and neutrino sectors, while photons remain extensive. We derive the corresponding chain rule terms in the radiation energy and entropy balances and formulate the photon-reheating problem without assuming that comoving electromagnetic entropy is automatically conserved when $q$ varies. The resulting construction connects the freeze-out history with the neutrino-to-photon temperature ratio and $N_{\\rm eff}$. We discuss the physical motivation, closure assumptions, and conditions under which this description can be used, without presenting numerical results or observational fits.","authors":"Matias P. Gonzalez","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","cond-mat.stat-mech","hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.29132"},{"id":"2606.16204","title":"Dark Matter Attenuation inside the Earth: A Boltzmann Equation Approach","summary":"For strongly interacting or boosted dark matter, propagation through the Earth can involve sizable scattering and energy loss, reshaping the underground flux in energy, direction, and normalization. Scattered particles may still fall within the detector acceptance, so the detector-side signal depends on phase-space transport from the Earth's surface to the underground detector. In this work, we formulate this transport problem with the Boltzmann equation. Its integral solution organizes successive scattering effects as a deterministic expansion in scattering orders. We analyze the transport equation in flat-Earth and spherical-Earth geometries, and apply the method to Dirac dark matter with an isoscalar vector interaction. The iterative solution agrees well with the Monte Carlo spectra.","authors":"Chuan-Yang Xing, Chen Xia","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.16204"},{"id":"2606.30910","title":"Phenomenology of Long-Lived Dark Photons and Axion-Like Particles in a Mixed Portal Framework","summary":"We investigate the phenomenology of a dark photon \\(A'\\) and an axion-like particle (ALP) ,\\(a\\), connected through a mixed portal framework which simultaneously allows the conventional visible decay $(A'\\rightarrow f\\bar f)$ and the exotic cascade process $(A'\\rightarrow a\\gamma\\rightarrow3\\gamma)$. We derive the relevant decay widths, branching ratios, and Lorentz-boosted decay lengths, and introduce a dominance parameter \\(D=\\Gamma(A'\\to a\\gamma)/\\Gamma_{\\rm SM}\\) to distinguish Standard Model-dominated and cascade-dominated regions, with the transition occurring at $(D=1)$. A detailed analysis of both light $(0.1\\leq m_{A'}\\leq10~{\\rm GeV})$ and heavy $(10\\leq m_{A'}\\leq100~{\\rm GeV})$ dark-photon scenarios shows that the exotic channel can substantially modify the expected dark-photon signatures, transforming otherwise long-lived or detector-stable states into experimentally accessible displaced multi-photon events. In addition, the ALP sector itself may exhibit long-lived particle behavior, leading to distinct displaced diphoton signatures. Our results show that mixed dark-photon-ALP portals offer a rich LLP phenomenology that can be explored at future high-luminosity lepton colliders such as the FCC-ee.","authors":"Caglar Zorbilmez, Beyhan Tatar, Azmi Ali Altintas, Cihan Bayindir, Fatih Ozaydin","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.30910"},{"id":"2608.04990","title":"Thermal Alignment as a Pathway to Axion Dark Matter","summary":"We study the relic energy of an axion whose homogeneous displacement is damped by a thermal gauge plasma. Dissipation is accompanied by thermal fluctuations. To calculate the energy at decoupling, we retain the covariance between the axion field and its velocity. For linear Langevin dynamics, positivity of the noise covariance provides a finite-interval lower bound on the energy generated by the bath, independently of the initial state. We consider a renormalizable $SU(2)_h\\times SU(3)_\\ell$ theory in which a scalar transition separates the early topological susceptibility and Chern--Simons diffusion from the confining potential acting at late times. With the specified scalar history and a local plasma response, fluctuations generated during the final cooling interval carry energy comparable to the coherent-only dark matter benchmark. The relic-density integral extends over finite momenta and cannot be inferred from the homogeneous topological diffusion rate. We also include the chiral-condensate term in the scalar free energy. For the chirally broken benchmark, this term removes the stationary high-temperature solution of the polynomial potential. Thus, a consistent microscopic realization requires both an assessment of thermal axion production and scalar phases that remain stationary in the full free energy.","authors":"Imtiaz Khan, G. Mustafa, Chengxun Yuan, Farkhod Botirov, Ahmadjon Abdujabbarov, Farruh Atamurotov","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.04990"},{"id":"2611.21780","title":"Cross-correlating the SPHEREx extragalactic background light with CMB lensing II: theoretical predictions confronted with lightcone simulations","summary":"Cross-correlating intensity maps with the lensing of the cosmic microwave background (CMB) rejects the foregrounds and noise that do not trace the dark matter. In the near infrared, the remaining signal combines the integrated galaxy light (IGL), the intrahalo light (IHL) and several emission lines. Accurate modeling of all three is essential to forecast detectability and interpret measurements. We present simulated SPHEREx intensity maps of all three on a lightcone, painted on halos from the Hidden Valley $N$-body simulation, and the CMB-lensing convergence of the same lightcone, over $100\\,{\\rm deg}^2$ to $z \\approx 6$. We assign each halo its lines and continuum by stellar-population synthesis, in two models whose metallicities are fixed or follow its stellar mass. Halo-model predictions from the simulation's luminosity-halo mass relations and measured clustering, without free parameters, match the auto-spectra of the IGL and lines, and their cross-spectra with lensing, across the band: at $2.2\\,\\mu$m, to within $5\\%$ above $\\ell \\approx 400$ and $7\\%$ above $\\ell = 200$, respectively. The agreement requires nonlinear clustering: linear theory describes the cross-spectrum of a line up to $\\ell \\approx 1000$-$1600$ at $2.2\\,\\mu$m, but fails for the IGL and the convergence at multipoles of a few hundred. With the noise of a SPHEREx deep field and of Simons Observatory lensing, the unfiltered cross-spectra, each component taken alone, reach signal-to-noise ratios of $9$-$21$ for the lines, $20$ for the IGL and $15$ for the IHL. A filter that removes radial modes longer than about $600$ Mpc keeps $62\\%$ of the IGL's signal-to-noise and at most $20\\%$ of the lines', with fitted amplitudes within about $1\\sigma$ of the predictions before and after filtering. The emission model changes a channel's intensity by up to a factor of three, but the line detectability only by tens of percent.","authors":"Azadeh Moradinezhad Dizgah, Zucheng Gao","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO"],"topics":["dark"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21780"},{"id":"2611.22387","title":"Probing Velocity-Dependent WIMP Dark Matter Annihilation in Large-Scale Structure","summary":"The unresolved gamma-ray background (UGRB) provides a promising avenue for probing the cosmological annihilation of weakly interacting massive particle (WIMP) dark matter through its cross-correlation with large-scale structure (LSS). While most previous studies have assumed a velocity-independent (s-wave) annihilation cross section, many well-motivated particle physics models predict annihilation rates that depend on the relative velocity of dark matter particles. In this work, we develop a generalized halo-model framework to investigate the cosmological signatures of velocity-dependent WIMP annihilation. Parameterizing the velocity-weighted annihilation cross section as $\\sigma v_{\\rm rel}\\propto v_{\\rm rel}^{n}$, we consider four representative cases corresponding to Sommerfeld-like (n=-1), s-wave (n=0), p-wave (n=2), and d-wave (n=4) annihilation. We show that velocity dependence modifies not only the overall annihilation emission but also the halo population dominating the signal, leading to distinct effective halo biases, redshift distributions, and angular clustering properties. As a result, different annihilation mechanisms leave characteristic signatures in the UGRB-LSS cross-correlation that cannot be captured by the overall gamma-ray intensity alone. As an illustration of the formalism, we perform a statistical forecast for the cross-correlation between the Fermi Large Area Telescope (LAT) UGRB and the Two Micron All Sky Survey Redshift Survey (2MRS) galaxy catalog and derive the projected sensitivities to the velocity-independent annihilation coefficient for these four annihilation scenarios. Our results demonstrate that cosmological cross-correlations provide a powerful and complementary probe of velocity-dependent dark matter annihilation, offering a new avenue for constraining the particle physics of dark matter through its imprints on large-scale structure.","authors":"Wen-Qing Guo","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","astro-ph.HE"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22387"},{"id":"2611.22498","title":"Using the 0i Einstein equation to break dark-sector degeneracies","summary":"Over the past decade, cosmological observations have started to challenge the $\\Lambda$CDM model. If these tensions persist, they immediately raise one fundamental question: what new physics could be responsible for them? More specifically, do these deviations arise from a modification of gravity, from an evolving or clustering dark energy component, or from dark matter properties beyond the cold dark matter paradigm? In this paper we show that, with current methods, any observed deviation can always be attributed to more than one sector, preventing us from determining if the tension is due to gravity, dark energy or dark matter. The main reason is that current tests rely on unobservable quantities. These quantities are then either related to observables by assuming the validity of specific equations, or compared with theoretical predictions computed within a given class of theories. Both approaches inevitably break the model independence of the tests, leading to degeneracies between gravity, dark energy, and dark matter. To overcome this limitation, we construct a set of three tests that rely only on observable quantities and that, when combined, can distinguish between the three sectors. We show that the $0i$ Einstein equation -- which is never tested in standard analyses -- adds crucial information to that provided by the gravitational slip equation and the Euler equation.","authors":"Camille Bonvin","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","gr-qc"],"topics":["dark"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22498"},{"id":"2504.14456","title":"Background dynamics and cosmological tests of interacting, viscous $H^2+H^{-2}$ dark energy","summary":"We study dark-sector energy exchange and bulk viscosity in the HHDE model, whose density combines terms proportional to the square and inverse square of the Hubble parameter. Five viscosity cases and seven interactions give 35 fluid realizations governed by a common autonomous system. When the intrinsic dark-energy pressure is reconstructed from the continuity equation, dark-energy viscosity leaves the expansion history unchanged. Interaction and dark-matter viscosity enter through their net contribution, producing further exact background degeneracies. Each realization admits stable de Sitter attractors in physical parameter domains, either dominated by dark energy or containing residual dark matter sustained by exchange and viscous stress. Representative posterior trajectories connect the inferred present states to these endpoints. Using supernovae, cosmic chronometers, DESI baryon acoustic oscillations and a compressed Planck distance prior, the density-product interaction yields a Hubble constant of $71.15^{+1.53}_{-1.64}$ km s$^{-1}$ Mpc$^{-1}$. Conditional on the applicability of that prior, the preferred transfer from dark energy to dark matter lowers the minimum chi-squared by 75.16 relative to noninteracting HHDE and reduces the discrepancy with the local distance-ladder measurement to approximately one standard deviation when both uncertainties are combined.","authors":"Guo Chen, Wei Fang, Chao-Jun Feng","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO"],"topics":["dark"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.14456"},{"id":"2607.19494","title":"The silent dark sector: a field-theory approach to unified dark fluids with vanishing speed of sound","summary":"We introduce a novel class of field-theory models that provide a unified description of the dark sector as a single perfect fluid with vanishing sound speed. These models also admit the interpretation of cold dark matter interacting with vacuum energy while avoiding any phantom behavior and including standard $\\Lambda$CDM as a particular case. Built upon a single scalar field invariant under transverse diffeomorphisms, the framework naturally implements the constraints imposed in the so called mimetic models. We analyze a particular simple example exhibiting an effectively evolving dark energy and find that it can reproduce the DESI best-fit CPL evolution with the same number of free parameters.","authors":"Javier de Cruz P\\'erez, Dar\\'io Jaramillo-Garrido, Antonio L. Maroto, Prado Mart\\'in-Moruno","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","gr-qc"],"topics":["dark"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.19494"},{"id":"2611.18876","title":"Searching for Anisotropic Cosmic Birefringence with SPT-3G","summary":"We search for anisotropic cosmic birefringence using two years of data from the SPT-3G instrument on the South Pole Telescope. Anisotropic cosmic birefringence refers to a theorized dependence of the refractive index of the vacuum on the polarization of light that varies across the Universe in a statistically isotropic manner. Such an effect would manifest as a direction-dependent mixing of the $E$ and $B$ polarization modes of the cosmic microwave background (CMB), caused by rotation of the polarization angle as photons travel from the CMB last scattering surface to us. We present a measurement of the power spectrum of the rotation angle $\\alpha$ in six bins across the angular multipole range $20 < L < 2048$, finding no evidence for cosmic birefringence with the spectrum consistent with zero. At 95% confidence, we set a new best upper limit on the amplitude of a scale-invariant birefringence power spectrum of $A_{\\rm CB}<0.027$. Additionally, we place upper limits on a scale-dependent white noise model. We translate the scale-invariant upper limit to constraints on the axion coupling constant and primordial magnetic field strength, finding 95% confidence limits of $g_{\\alpha\\gamma} < 1.7\\times 10^{-2}/H_I$ and $B_{\\rm 1\\,Mpc} < 9.6\\,{\\rm nG}$ respectively. We expect these limits to improve several-fold with the complete SPT-3G survey.","authors":"A. Coerver, M. Rahimi, D. Anbajagane, E. Anderes, A. J. Anderson, B. Ansarinejad, M. Archipley, L. Balkenhol, D. R. Barron, P. S. Barry, K. Benabed, A. N. Bender, B. A. Benson, F. Bianchini, L. E. Bleem, S. Bocquet, F. R. Bouchet, E. Camphuis, M. G. Campitiello, J. E. Carlstrom, J. Carron, C. L. Chang, P. M. Chichura, A. Chokshi, T. -L. Chou, T. M. Crawford, C. Daley, T. de Haan, K. R. Dibert, M. A. Dobbs, M. Doohan, D. Dutcher, C. Feng, K. R. Ferguson, N. C. Ferree, K. Fichman, A. Foster, S. Galli, A. E. Gambrel, A. K. Gao, F. Ge, F. Guidi, S. Guns, N. W. Halverson, E. Hivon, G. P. Holder, W. L. Holzapfel, J. C. Hood, A. Hryciuk, N. Huang, T. Jhaveri, F. K\\'eruzor\\'e, A. R. Khalife, L. Knox, K. Kornoelje, C. -L. Kuo, K. Levy, Y. Li, A. E. Lowitz, C. Lu, G. P. Lynch, T. J. Maccarone, A. S. Maniyar, E. S. Martsen, F. Menanteau, M. Millea, J. Montgomery, Y. Nakato, T. Natoli, A. Ouellette, Z. Pan, P. Paschos, K. A. Phadke, K. Prabhu, W. Quan, A. Rahlin, C. L. Reichardt, M. Rouble, J. E. Ruhl, A. C. Silva Oliveira, A. Simpson, J. A. Sobrin, A. A. Stark, J. Stephen, C. Tandoi, Q. Taylor, C. Trendafilova, J. D. Vieira, A. G. Vieregg, A. Vitrier, Y. Wan, N. Whitehorn, W. L. K. Wu, M. R. Young, J. A. Zebrowski","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO"],"topics":["dark"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18876"},{"id":"2611.21911","title":"Self-gravitating Lee-Huang-Yang fluids as dark matter halos","summary":"We propose a model of dark matter halos based on a self-gravitating Bose fluid whose effective equation of state is dominated by Lee-Huang-Yang quantum fluctuations. The model is governed by coupled generalized Gross-Pitaevskii-Poisson equations, which incorporate quantum pressure, Lee-Huang-Yang-induced repulsion, and gravitational attraction. By retaining the full quantum pressure, we examine the halo structure from its central region to its asymptotic regime. We first derive the Jeans dispersion relation and analyze how the Lee-Huang-Yang fluctuations alter the gravitational instability criterion and the associated characteristic length and mass scales. We then obtain analytical expressions for the central and large-radius behaviors of the equilibrium solutions. To verify the validity and the performance of the model, we fit the model-predicted curves with a sample of 20 galaxies from the SPARC database and compare the results with those obtained from Navarro-Frenk-White, standard Bose-Einstein condensate, and quantum-droplet dark matter models. We find that the pure-LHY model reproduces a broad range of observed galactic kinematics, yielding reduced $\\chi^2$ values and a lower sample average compared to the aforementioned models. These results indicate that the combined effects of quantum pressure, LHY quantum fluctuations and the self-gravity provide additional flexibility in describing both the central and extended outer regions of galactic halos. We also discuss the implications of the inferred halo parameters and emphasize their dependence on the assumed effective equation of state and its microscopic interpretation. The pure-LHY model constitutes an ideal platform for exploring the role of quantum-fluctuation-induced interaction in the formation and structure of dark matter halos.","authors":"Abdelaali Boudjemaa","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc"],"topics":["dark"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21911"},{"id":"2611.22134","title":"Finite-wavelength corrections to dynamical friction in fuzzy dark matter: a causal linear-response approach","summary":"A massive body moving through fuzzy dark matter (FDM) generates a density wake whose gravitational backreaction produces dynamical friction. We investigate how finite-wavelength effects modify this force using causal linear-response theory and the statistical Wigner formulation. For a homogeneous Maxwellian background and general isotropic finite-size perturbers, we derive an analytic representation of the gravitational drag and recover the classical Chandrasekhar result under matched regularization conditions. In the controlled semiclassical regime, the leading finite-wavelength correction is quadratic in $\\hbar/m_\\chi$ and factorizes into a velocity-dependent function and a ratio of spectral moments characterizing the source structure. Remarkably, this correction reduces the drag magnitude independently of the detailed radial source profile within the adopted Maxwellian model. Gaussian and exponential sources illustrate this result. We further establish a controlled remainder bound and clarify the nonuniform behavior of the point-source limit. These findings identify the physical conditions under which wave effects modify classical dynamical friction.","authors":"Yu Wang, Meilin Liu, Haiguang Xu, Shoupan Liu","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc"],"topics":["dark"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22134"},{"id":"2610.19522","title":"Nambu's Dynamics, Strings and Gravitized Quantum Theory","summary":"In this article we present a new view on string theory that is rooted in quantum foundations extended to the domain of quantum gravity in the form of gravitized quantum theory, in which the canonical probability measure captured by the Born rule of quantum theory, turns out to be dynamical and contextual. We claim that this gravitization of quantum theory is the essence of string theory. String theory, according to this view, originates in foundations of quantum theory and Nambu's dynamics, which features in the non-perturbative definition of string theory in terms of a T-duality covariant and non-commutative formulation, called the metastring. Nambu's dynamics and the associated cubic and hypercubic objects also appear as the natural language for exploring the new observables and phenomena implied by a dynamical Born rule, including triple and higher order quantum interference, that can be investigated in experiments involving massive quantum probes, and that are also needed for direct empirical tests of the Born rule, a cornerstone of quantum theory.","authors":"Tristan H\\\"ubsch, Djordje Minic","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc","hep-th"],"topics":["foundations","qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19522"},{"id":"2611.21889","title":"Quantum Fisher information matrices for time-evolved bosonic Gaussian thermal states","summary":"Quantum Fisher information (QFI) is a fundamental quantum metrological quantity arising from the second order term in a Taylor expansion of a smooth divergence evaluated at nearby quantum states. The $\\alpha$-$z$ information matrix is a QFI measure arising from the $\\alpha$-$z$ R\\'enyi relative entropy, and QFI measures corresponding to various underlying smooth divergences may be recovered from the $\\alpha$-$z$ information matrix, including the standard symmetric logarithmic derivative (SLD) QFI. Bosonic Gaussian states are common probe states for quantum metrological tasks and admit a complete characterization in terms of their first and second moments. In this work, we consider the quantum metrological task of estimating parameters that characterize a Gaussian unitary time evolution of a faithful bosonic Gaussian thermal state. We establish a formula for the $\\alpha$-$z$ information matrix of faithful bosonic Gaussian thermal states that have undergone a Gaussian unitary time evolution. As an example, we calculate entries of the $\\alpha$-$z$ information matrix for a two-mode Gaussian thermal state that has undergone a beam splitting operation. For a parametrized family of time-evolved bosonic Gaussian states, we also derive a formula for the SLD operator, which is known to provide a measurement scheme that saturates the quantum Cr\\'amer-Rao bound and thus achieves the ultimate precision for single parameter estimation.","authors":"Hailey S. Murray, Diego Estrada, Mark M. Wilde","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["sensing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21889"},{"id":"2611.22428","title":"Generating Massive Mechanical Superpositions through Qubit-Mediated Interference","summary":"We propose a method towards demonstrating a quantum superposition of a massive mechanical resonator without the need for exact ground-state cooling, a requirement that becomes increasingly difficult to satisfy for low-frequency resonators. Our scheme relies on coupling the resonator to a spin-qubit. Using magnetic force coupling between the motion of the mechanical resonator and the spin-qubit, the resonator is forced into attracted and repelled states as the spin-qubit is coherently controlled. Interference between the resonator states can be observed when a series of $\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}$-pulses is applied to the spin-qubit within the coherence time of the resonator. The protocol describes a true which-path interference experiment suitable for use at ultra-low temperatures, since it requires only three $\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}$-pulses, separated in time by half a mechanical period, thus minimizing the heat-load and duration of the experiment. By analysing the experimental requirements necessary to successfully execute the protocol, we show that the protocol is suitable for resonators in a broad regime of resonance frequencies, ranging from 1kHz resonators, which have the advantage of a relatively high spin-coupling, to resonators of more than 100kHz, whose mechanical mode is easier to cool. We show that interference remains visible for resonator mode occupations exceeding 10 phonons. Lastly, we observe that an implementation of the protocol with low-frequency mechanical resonators provides a promising approach towards testing objective collapse models.","authors":"Jos\\'e P. Dupont, Loek van Everdingen, Tjerk H. Oosterkamp, Louk Rademaker","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["foundations"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22428"},{"id":"2105.06037","title":"A Diamond Quantum Oscilloscope","summary":"Quantum-enhanced metrology has achieved remarkable precision in parameter estimation, but extending these advantages to continuous time-dependent fields (waveform sensing) remains a formidable challenge due to the infinite-dimensional nature of the signal. Here, we report the first experimental realization of a room-temperature solid-state quantum oscilloscope based on a sequential quantum difference amplification strategy. Within a zero-order-hold reconstruction framework, our method completely bypasses the need for massive entanglement. Crucially, we demonstrate that the overall waveform reconstruction error unequivocally follows the theoretically predicted Heisenberg scaling of \\(O(N^{-1/2})\\), decisively surpassing the classical \\(O(N^{-1/3})\\) limit. This transformative precision requires approximately 30-fold fewer quantum resources than conventional methods to achieve the same waveform error. Furthermore, we demonstrate the reconstruction of a multicomponent waveform and distinctly resolve weak high-frequency components that are entirely drowned in the shot noise of classical sampling. These results transition quantum metrology from parameter estimation into a practical instrument, establishing a genuine quantum advantage in continuous waveform reconstruction and paving the way for critical applications such as nanoscale nuclear magnetic resonance imaging, where detecting extremely weak signals is crucial for resolving molecular structures.","authors":"Yang Dong, Yu Zheng, Wang Jiang, Zhi-Wei Liu, Shao-Chun Zhang, Xiang-Dong Chena, Guang-Can Guo, Fang-Wen Sun","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["sensing","computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.06037"},{"id":"2511.22476","title":"Improved parameter initialization for the (local) unitary cluster Jastrow ansatz","summary":"The unitary cluster Jastrow (UCJ) ansatz and its variant known as local UCJ (LUCJ) are promising choices for variational quantum algorithms for chemistry due to their combination of physical motivation and hardware efficiency. The parameters of these ansatzes can be initialized from the output of a coupled cluster, singles and doubles (CCSD) calculation performed on a classical computer. However, truncating the number of repetitions of the ansatz, as well as discarding interactions to accommodate the connectivity constraints of near-term quantum processors, degrade the approximation to CCSD and the resulting energy accuracy. In this work, we propose two methods to improve the parameter initialization. The first method, which is applicable to both expectation value- and sample-based algorithms, uses compressed double factorization of the CCSD amplitudes to improve or recover the CCSD approximation. The second method, which is applicable to sample-based algorithms, uses approximate tensor network simulation to improve the quality of samples produced by the ansatz circuit. Both methods perform classical preprocessing to construct the initial circuit parameters and can be followed by parameter optimization using quantum measurements, although we do not perform such optimization in this work. We validate our methods using exact state vector simulation on systems of up to 52 qubits, as well as experiments on superconducting quantum processors using up to 65 qubits. Our results indicate that our methods can significantly improve the output of both expectation value- and sample-based quantum algorithms.","authors":"Wan-Hsuan Lin, Fangchun Liang, Mario Motta, Haimeng Zhang, Kenneth M. Merz Jr., Kevin J. Sung","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["foundations","computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.22476"},{"id":"2610.25518","title":"Randomness Certification from Statistically Hiding Commitments","summary":"Randomness certification aims to guarantee that the output of an untrusted quantum device contains genuine randomness that remains unpredictable even given quantum side information held by an adversary. For a classical verifier interacting with a single quantum device, such a guarantee cannot rely on the physical separation underlying standard Bell tests and must instead follow from computational restrictions on the prover. Building on the framework of computational Bell inequalities, we distinguish classical and quantum virtual-input hiding, showing that the former suffices for proofs of quantumness, whereas the latter enables randomness certification against quantum side information. Our main technical contribution is to establish quantum virtual-input hiding for a commitment-based protocol derived from an earlier proof of quantumness (Morimae and Yamakawa, CRYPTO, 2024). We show that statistical hiding together with ordinary computational binding against quantum polynomial-time senders suffices, without requiring stronger quantum binding notions such as sum-binding or collapse-binding. This yields a randomness-certification protocol from post-quantum statistically hiding bit commitments. We give three instantiations. The NOVY commitment from any post-quantum one-way permutation yields randomness certification in the standard model with an inefficient classical verifier. Post-quantum trapdoor claw-free functions additionally provide trapdoor inversion, enabling an efficient classical verifier. In the quantum random-oracle model, a hash-based commitment yields randomness certification without additional computational assumptions, again with an inefficient verifier. These instantiations separate the cryptographic properties sufficient for randomness certification from the additional structure required for efficient verification within our commitment-based framework.","authors":"Ilya Merkulov, Rotem Arnon","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["foundations"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25518"},{"id":"2611.21560","title":"Optimal Quantum Sensing Algorithms for Discrete-Frequency Spectroscopy","summary":"Spectroscopy is a versatile tool that drives scientific discovery and underpins modern technologies. Quantum sensing offers promising opportunities to advance spectroscopy through enhanced sensitivity and spectral and spatial resolution. However, existing approaches largely focus on improving general measurement capabilities, often overlooking the structure of specific sensing tasks and leaving potential performance gains untapped. Here, we develop an algorithmic approach to quantum sensing tailored to various spectroscopic tasks, providing new avenues for improvement. Specifically, we consider general time-varying signals supported on a known set of $N$ candidate frequencies, a setting we call discrete-frequency spectroscopy. We formulate three types of practically motivated sensing tasks---Detection, Search, and Learning---that require progressively more spectral information. For all tasks, we design optimal quantum sensing algorithms achieving a $\\sqrt{N}$ speedup over conventional frequency-scanning approaches. Optimal Detection and Search need only a single-qubit sensor under $\\pi$-pulse control, whereas optimal Learning provably requires coherent processing with a quantum computer. End-to-end numerical simulations incorporating various experimental imperfections predict up to two-orders-of-magnitude speedup in Detection. As an example, we analyze the feasibility of our Detection and Search algorithms for real-world molecular screening based on state-of-the-art nitrogen-vacancy centers. We also show how quantum-computing-enhanced Learning enables wireless communication in a weak-signal regime where conventional methods fail. Our results establish a practical route toward realizing quantum advantages via designing task-specific sensing algorithms.","authors":"Yu-Jie Liu, Richard R. Allen, Ruohan Shen, Bingtian Ye, Timothy T. Chang, Joonhee Choi, Soonwon Choi","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["sensing","computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21560"},{"id":"2611.21681","title":"Remark on internal relaxation in large number qubit systems","summary":"For a time $t_{c}$ of calculation in an $N$ qubit quantum computer that is much shorter than the intrinsic Heisenberg time $t_H$, $t_c\\ll t_H$, the dynamics of the system is effectively irreversible and the couplings between the qubits give rise to a relaxation time scale $\\tau_r$. If $t_H\\gg t_c >\\sim \\tau_r$ one looses quantum coherence exponentially fast. Since $t_H$ scales exponentially with $N$ there is a threshold $N_c$ such that for $N\\gg N_c$ one must be aware of possible intrinsic relaxation during calculation. As an order of magnitude estimation we find $N_c\\approx \\ln(\\tau_r/T)$ where $T$ is a typical system specific oscillation time of a single qubit. $\\tau_r$ can be estimated as the typical inverse transition rate between two parts of the system, one of which is treated as an environment of the other.","authors":"Martin Jan{\\ss}en","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph","cond-mat.stat-mech"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21681"},{"id":"2611.21748","title":"Restoring quantum neural network trainability through reusable calibration","summary":"Quantum neural networks (QNNs) rely on measured quantum observables to guide optimization, but hardware noise can suppress the parameter-dependent signals essential for learning. Here, we develop a structure-preserving error-mitigation framework that improves QNN trainability through reusable calibration of the quantum circuit's effective noise response. By calibrating a reference circuit with the same gate architecture as the trainable circuit, we construct regularized estimators of local quantum features that remain fixed throughout training. We quantify how calibration errors and response mismatches affect the recovery of both observables and their derivatives. Numerical simulations demonstrate enhanced loss-landscape contrast, faster convergence, and improved classification performance, and an IBM Quantum experiment confirms improved closed-loop variational optimization under hardware noise. We further demonstrate calibration reuse in a modular QNN comprising 25 independent four-qubit channels, corresponding to 100 qubits in aggregate. Our results establish reusable feature calibration as a practical approach to improving noise-limited quantum machine learning without repeated calibration at every training step.","authors":"Ruizhe Shen, Xianquan Yan, Kenji Kawaguchi, Ching Hua Lee","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21748"},{"id":"2611.21749","title":"Robust Passive Quantum Memory in Three Dimensions, Part I: Thermal and Zero-Temperature Perturbative Stability","summary":"Whether a three-dimensional quantum many-body system with geometrically local, bounded-degree interactions can passively protect encoded quantum information has remained a central open question since Kitaev introduced topological quantum computation. Passive protection requires stability against both thermal noise at nonzero temperature and generic static Hamiltonian perturbations Balasubramanian, Davydova, and Lin (BDL) recently established unperturbed three-dimensional thermal self-correction using a recursively defined family of stabilizer Hamiltonians. However, they left open whether their construction remains stable against quantum perturbations, even at zero temperature. In this paper, we show that a slight variant of the BDL code family achieves stability along both boundary axes of the perturbation-temperature phase diagram as the system size grows. Following BDL, in the absence of perturbation it is thermally stable below a critical temperature, achieving a stretched-exponentially long memory lifetime when coupled to any local Markovian thermal bath generated by single-site system-bath interactions satisfying detailed balance with bounded coupling rates. At zero temperature under arbitrary weak, superpolynomially decaying quantum perturbations, the family maintains a uniform bulk spectral gap, superpolynomially small ground-space splitting, and a superpolynomially long closed-system unitary memory lifetime for quasi-locally dressed logical observables. To prove this zero-temperature stability, we establish local topological quantum order across the multiscale hierarchy using a linearly dilated relaxation of the usual Bravyi-Hastings-Michalakis criteria. In the companion paper, we combine the tools developed here with finite-temperature worldline cluster expansions to establish passive self-correction throughout the phase diagram's positive-temperature perturbed interior.","authors":"Patrick Hayden","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph","cond-mat.str-el","math-ph","math.MP"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21749"},{"id":"2611.21785","title":"Microwave-optical quantum transduction with quantum-well exciton polaritons","summary":"Linking superconducting quantum processors over optical fiber requires microwave-optical quantum transducers that are efficient, high-bandwidth, and low-noise. This combination has been out of reach because of the weak nonlinearities of conventional electro-optic (EO) materials. We propose a transducer with an engineered, strong EO nonlinearity using the quantum-confined Stark effect (QCSE) of quantum-well excitons. The QCSE transducer leverages the strong light-matter interactions and extreme polarizability of quantum well excitons. Strong coupling to an optical cavity forms exciton-polaritons whose vacuum EO coupling is up to five orders of magnitude larger than in a typical thin-film lithium niobate device. We describe a GaAs/AlGaAs stepped-quantum-well transducer design with predicted transduction efficiencies above 60% and fewer than 0.01 added noise quanta over a $\\sim$60 MHz bandwidth, using pump powers below 10 $\\mu$W and loaded optical and microwave quality factors of only $3\\times10^4$ and $10^3$. We further show that the cavity protection of the polariton provides robustness against inhomogeneous broadening of the quantum well excitons.","authors":"Nicholas S. Yama, Mohammad Khalifa, Yuxuan Wei, Adbhut Gupta, Loren N. Pfeiffer, Feng Wang, Alp Sipahigil","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21785"},{"id":"2611.21840","title":"Quantum-Classical Separation in Probability Distribution Transport via Nonlocal Games","summary":"In many-body physics and generative artificial intelligence, locality provides a natural notion of phase equivalence: two quantum states or classical probability distributions are regarded as in the same phase when local dynamics can reversibly transform one into the other. This raises a fundamental question: can local quantum dynamics transport classical probability distributions in ways that local classical dynamics cannot? We establish a quantum-classical separation in distribution transport: two classical distributions can be connected exactly and reversibly by a constant-depth local quantum circuit, whereas classical stochastic transport requires a substantially larger spatial range. We demonstrate this separation explicitly on a one-dimensional chain and develop a general construction principle on graphs, based on nonlocal games. Moreover, the source distribution can itself be generated by a simple local classical process, establishing a quantum advantage in distribution generation as well. Our results reveal a fundamental distinction between classical and quantum local reversibility, even when both the initial and final states are entirely classical.","authors":"Fangjun Hu, Xun Gao","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["cs.CC","cs.LG","quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21840"},{"id":"2611.21906","title":"Probing the real-time decoding accuracy bounds in trapped ion architectures","summary":"Fault tolerant quantum computers will rely on real-time decoders that process syndromes faster than the QEC cycle duration. The millisecond cycle times offered by trapped ion platforms relax low-latency decoding demands, but simulations and experiments have not yet exploited this time budget to reach maximum logical error suppression. We show how a hybrid sliding window decoding scheme composed of a lower-accuracy and a higher-accuracy decoder can yield substantially improved logical error rates, while preserving low latency. All windows are handled by the lower-accuracy decoder, with some windows being infrequently switched to the higher-accuracy decoder. We extract soft-output information from the lower-accuracy decoder efficiently for any QLDPC code, which allows us to identify decoding windows of low confidence. We simulate memory experiments of bivariate bicycle (BB) codes, using trapped-ion informed noise models. Depending on the physical error rate and BB code, we find up to 3-4 orders of magnitude logical error rate reduction compared to the $\\texttt{BP-LSD}$ decoder, and up to 2-3 orders of magnitude lower logical error rate compared to the $\\texttt{Beamsearch}$ decoder. By leveraging available classical processing time, our decoding protocol significantly boosts decoding accuracy, ultimately reducing the demands on increasing code size.","authors":"Arianna Meinking, Evangelia Takou, Kenneth R. Brown","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21906"},{"id":"2611.21928","title":"Simplifying models of noisy stabilizer QEC on leakage qubits by Pauli twirling","summary":"The growing capabilities of quantum error-correction (QEC) experiments make it increasingly important to develop methods that predict or assess logical performance over many QEC cycles. Prior work has shown that a stabilizer QEC cycle subject to Pauli-stochastic noise can be modeled by a classical Markov process over syndrome states, with a logical Pauli channel attached to each syndrome transition. Here, we extend this framework to systems in which physical qubits are subject to leakage. We first develop the theory of leakage-dephased Pauli twirling and show that twirled processes can be expressed in terms of Pauli maps on the computational subspace, conditioned on transitions between leakage patterns. We apply this theory to noisy stabilizer QEC with leakage, demonstrating that a QEC cycle with leakage can be modeled by a classical Markov process over the joint syndrome and leakage patterns, with a logical Pauli channel attached to each joint transition. Finally, we derive a transfer-matrix description of repeated QEC cycles and an exact transfer-matrix expression for the success probability of a QEC memory experiment. This framework connects leaky error models of the individual components of the QEC cycle to many-cycle logical performance and provides a foundation for logical characterization and benchmarking methods that can account for leakage.","authors":"Alex Kwiatkowski, Aaron J. Friedman","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21928"},{"id":"2611.22087","title":"Commutator-guided repair and classical-accessibility boundaries for shallow quantum circuits","summary":"Trainability does not guarantee low-energy arrival: a shallow quantum circuit can be optimizable yet fail, under fixed depth and evaluation budgets, to reach Hamiltonian-selected states. We introduce a commutator-guided repair for this operational failure mode. Low-order host--Hamiltonian commutators and fixed same-support companion axes define a restricted pool of local Pauli rotations, from which a compact completion layer is selected before full reoptimization. Across 189 paired six-qubit trials, unrepaired circuits achieve 0/189 strict successes, a non-deployable per-trial control envelope achieves 109/189, and two predeclared selectors achieve 133/189 and 127/189. The repair loses to matched controls for transverse-field Ising, is weak for pure-ZZ MaxCut, and remains size-limited for weighted MIS. Under fixed-depth geometric locality, repaired losses and gradients are classically computable, while corresponding ground-state targets admit low-bond-dimension tensor-network approximations through the tested sizes. The method reveals a trainability--arrival--classical-accessibility tradeoff rather than quantum advantage.","authors":"Yifeng Peng, Sunpreet Singh Arora, Harshal Shah, Srinivasan Raghuraman, Kim Wagner, Bin Yan","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22087"},{"id":"2611.22108","title":"Perturbative Recovery of Stabilizer-Subspace Correlations for Quantum Computational Chemistry","summary":"Stabilizer-based subspace methods can substantially reduce the quantum resources required for electronic structure calculations, but the resulting projection neglects Hamiltonian couplings to configurations outside the retained subspace. In this work, we introduce Stabilizer-Subspace Perturbation Theory (SSPT) to recover the associated correlation contributions, using the Contextual Subspace Variational Quantum Eigensolver (CS-VQE) as a representative application. The central idea is to exploit the Pauli terms eliminated by stabilizer projection as perturbative operators that generate an external manifold from the optimized reference state. Their energetic contributions are evaluated using an Epstein-Nesbet-type second-order correction or a resolvent-based formulation that retains couplings within the external space. By exploiting the factorized structure of the stabilizer-constrained reference state, the required matrix elements can be evaluated through Pauli expectation values on the reduced contextual register without explicitly reconstructing the full-space wave function. Molecular benchmarks demonstrate substantial reductions in projection-induced energy errors, allowing chemical precision to be achieved with fewer contextual qubits in several cases. These results establish stabilizer-guided perturbation theory as a promising strategy for improving the accuracy of resource-efficient quantum chemistry calculations.","authors":"Qianjun Yao, Jie Liu, He Li","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph","physics.chem-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22108"},{"id":"2611.22118","title":"Variational Relevance-Informed Sparsification of Term Measurements for Variational Quantum Algorithms","summary":"Repeated measurements are a major bottleneck in variational quantum algorithms (VQAs). We uncover and characterize parameter-specific sparsity in the matrix of qubit-wise commuting (QWC) group contributions to parameter-shift gradients: most absolute contribution mass concentrates in a subset of groups whose relevance varies across parameters and optimization states. Building on this structure, we introduce a learned controller that predicts group relevance from Hamiltonian, circuit, light-cone, and current-state features, then selects measurements through a tunable coverage threshold. Exact contributions provide offline supervision without being required at deployment. Across 15 molecular VQE settings, our method saves 49.6% of nominal gradient-measurement shots with a mean absolute energy gap of 5.67 mHa to Full-QWC. We then ask whether large language models (LLMs) can solve the same selection problem without fine-tuning. The results reveal both promise and a clear limitation: LLMs can identify useful measurement groups, yet without guidance, local models do not consistently prioritize the physical information that matters most. Our learned controller uses these cues more effectively. This contrast points toward a broader direction for AI-assisted science: combining the flexibility of general-purpose models with explicit physical representations, task-specific objectives, and domain-grounded validation.","authors":"Yifeng Peng, Bin Yan, Harshal Shah, Srinivasan Raghuraman, Kim Wagner, Sunpreet Singh Arora","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22118"},{"id":"2611.22125","title":"Exceptional-point Sensing with Quantum Advantage","summary":"Exceptional-point sensors exploit singular non-Hermitian dynamics to enhance their response to weak perturbations. Recently, however, a no-go result has been established for a gain-loss-balanced dimer coupled to independent, phase-insensitive reservoirs: the exceptional-point susceptibility amplifies the signal and reservoir noise equally, precluding any quantum advantage. Here, we circumvent this limitation by engineering non-Hermitian dynamics in particle-hole Nambu space. A single common reservoir acts on modes forming a quantum-mechanics-free subsystem and generates an enhanced response protected from additional noise. We demonstrate quantum-metrological advantage for weak-signal sensing and static-parameter estimation. A continuous interpolation of the operator basis at fixed exceptional-point drift, with a singular midpoint, reveals how reservoir noise vanishes toward the protected limit. Our results provide a route to exceptional-point sensing with genuine quantum-metrological advantage through reservoir engineering.","authors":"Chuming Wang, Xinyao Guo, Dezhi Zhang, Mingchen Liu, Yifei Li, Haixing Miao, Yiqiu Ma","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22125"},{"id":"2611.22166","title":"Demonstration of a Qubit Control Scheme Compatible with Superconducting Digital Signals","summary":"Superconducting quantum computing faces a critical scalability bottleneck arising from the escalating complexity of traditional analog control infrastructure. Single-Flux-Quantum(SFQ) systems operate at cryogenic temperatures and offer a potential route to addressing these challenges. Using microwave signals at qubit-dependent frequencies to trigger SFQ pulses requires many independent analog microwave sources, limiting the potential to reduce control wiring and increase integration density. A scalable SFQ control system therefore needs a scheme that can address qubits with different transition frequencies using a common clock. Here, we propose and experimentally demonstrate a control scheme for implementing both single- and two-qubit gates. The scheme uses guiding waveforms to rapidly generate bipolar high-speed pulse sequences on a common clock, providing a basis for future implementation in SFQ control systems. Numerically, this scheme achieves a simulated fidelity of 99.99% for 40ns $R(\\varphi, \\frac{\\pi}{2})$ gates with arbitrary initial phases and 99.9% for 100ns controlled-Z(CZ) gates. Experimentally, by utilizing an arbitrary waveform generator(AWG) operated at a fixed sampling rate of 25GSa/s to deliver the bipolar high-speed pulse sequences, we successfully demonstrate 40ns single-qubit gates with an average gate fidelity of 99.94% estimated using cross-entropy benchmarking(XEB) and 120ns CZ gates with 99.63% fidelity. These results demonstrate the feasibility of common-clock encoded pulse control for both single- and two-qubit gates.","authors":"Chenyin Sun, Yu Xu, Zihua Chen, Dayu Li, Tianzuo Sun, Chen Zha, Yuan Li, Jin Lin, Lei Jiang, Tao Rong, Tao Jiang, Shaoyu Zhan, Xueshen Wang, Shijian Wang, Wenhui Cao, Yeru Wang, Xiaomin Wang, Ming Gong, Yulin Wu, Yuhuai Li, Gang Wu, Hui Deng, Kai Yan, Sirui Cao, Futian Liang, Fusheng Chen, Qingling Zhu, Yangsen Ye, Hao Rong, Shaojun Guo, Haoran Qian, Haibin Zhang, Yisen Hu, Shaowei Li, Jinjin Li, Cheng-Zhi Peng, Xiaobo Zhu, Jian-Wei Pan","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22166"},{"id":"2611.22247","title":"CHEQ: A Concurrent Hardware Evaluation Platform for QEC Decoders via Batch-Invariant Circuit-Level Noise Sampling","summary":"Hardware quantum error correction (QEC) decoders require validation of logical error suppression and timing compliance before deployment. Rare logical failures and deadline violations demand extensive sampling, while algorithm-level software evaluation does not directly measure hardware response times. Circuit-level noise models contain many error mechanisms but few firings per shot at low physical error rates, making exhaustive per-shot scanning inefficient. We present CHEQ, an FPGA platform integrating on-chip sampling, concurrent decoder evaluation, scoring, and RISC-V control. Its Sampling Units use geometric skipping to sample firing locations from detector error models and amortize mechanism traversal across shots within each batch. Under independent random draws and exact geometric sampling, the shot distribution is invariant to batch size, enabling a memory-throughput tradeoff. A throughput model guides joint selection of the Sampling Unit count, batch size, and decoder count under FPGA resource constraints. Across evaluated conditions, the pooled one-sided 95% upper bound on the weighted root-mean-square relative firing-rate difference from Stim is 0.16%. At a batch size of 4,096, a Sampling Unit uses only 4.8%-11.2% of a Helios decoder's LUTs for code distances 7, 9, 11, and 13. On an AMD VCU118 FPGA at code distance 7 and physical error rate 10^-3, two Sampling Units supply 20 Helios decoders at 49.2 million shots/s, completing 10^10 shots in 3.4 minutes without host-side shot generation.","authors":"Sechan Park, Kyeongwon Lee, Mundo Jung, Chaebin Jung, Woojoo Lee","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22247"},{"id":"2611.22315","title":"Circuit compilation in the quspect quantum SDK: selecting routings after coupling-constrained resynthesis","summary":"quspect is an open-source quantum computing SDK that provides circuit construction, C++ state-vector simulation with adjoint gradients, support for variational and combinatorial-optimization algorithms, Hamiltonian simulation, control-pulse envelopes and backends for several cloud quantum processors. Here we describe and evaluate its circuit compiler. Routing and synthesis interact: inserting a SWAP can change the circuit structure that later optimization exploits. At its highest optimization level, the compiler evaluates a screened set of routing candidates by their cost after coupling-constrained resynthesis. The pipeline combines parity-state A* search for small CNOT-phase regions, heuristic synthesis for larger regions, and numerical three-qubit instantiation with a per-block 10^-9 check. For a fixed phase polynomial, A* certifies the minimum CX count when it reaches a goal within budget. On an evaluation set of 55 circuits from 41 arXiv papers on five topologies, the compiler uses 0.616 times as many two-qubit gates as Qiskit O3 (95% circuit-cluster bootstrap interval 0.555-0.679) at 0.592 times its depth. BQSKit level 2 reaches 0.853 and tket 0.930. On a 17-qubit superconducting processor, the compiled circuits had the fewest CZ gates in all six scenarios of the main run. Ablations assess the contributions within the integrated pipeline.","authors":"Xusheng Xu, Runqing Zhang, Hongtao Xue, Jize Han, Jingwei Wen, Ruoyu Zhou, Lei Jin, Xiaoxiao Hu, Gui-Lu Long","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22315"},{"id":"2611.22389","title":"Nonlinear function regression using quantum ensemble learning","summary":"Recent advances in artificial intelligence and quantum technologies have driven the emergence of quantum machine learning, yet imperfections in near-term quantum devices remain a major obstacle for achieving practical quantum advantage. In the absence of fault-tolerant machines, ensemble learning, which combines multiple imperfect quantum devices, may unlock new quantum computing applications. Here, we introduce two ensemble-learning algorithms, namely Bagging and AdaBoost, for enhancing the accuracy of nonlinear regression problems. While Bagging trains variational quantum regressors independently, AdaBoost employs a sequential training strategy and achieves significantly better prediction accuracy. By incorporating systematic feature selection into our ensemble learning algorithms, we enhance their predictive power for highly nonlinear functions. We further improve prediction accuracy through a feature-to-qubit mapping strategy based on repeated feature encoding. The performance of our ensemble-based algorithms improves with both the number of regressors in the ensemble and the size of each regressor. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach using four benchmarking functions with varying degrees of nonlinearity by relying on a feature pool limited to first- and second-order polynomial terms.","authors":"Yueen Chen, Mohammad Sharifian, Abolfazl Bayat","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22389"},{"id":"2611.22391","title":"Strong eigenstate thermalization in Singer-cycle Clifford circuits","summary":"Eigenstate thermalization hypothesis (ETH) provides a central framework for understanding thermalization in isolated quantum systems. Recently, the infinite-temperature ETH has been established for almost all Floquet eigenstates in linear feedback shift register quantum circuits. Here we introduce brickwork Clifford circuits with $L$ qubits where the induced transformation of Pauli operators is represented by an irreducible symplectic matrix over the finite field $\\mathbb F_2$ of the maximal order $2^L+1$, known as a Singer cycle. For these Singer-cycle Clifford circuits, we prove a strong version of ETH: all Floquet eigenstates are thermal at infinite temperature without exceptions. We derive uniform bounds on both diagonal deviations from thermal expectation values and off-diagonal matrix elements of arbitrary observables supported on a fixed number of sites. The results indicate that long-time averages of observables approach the infinite-temperature values in the thermodynamic limit for arbitrary initial states. We also show that the temporal fluctuation of expectation values of observables is exponentially suppressed in the thermodynamic limit, implying that each observable remains close to the infinite-temperature value for almost all times.","authors":"Naoto Tsuji","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph","cond-mat.stat-mech"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22391"},{"id":"2611.22530","title":"Quantum Codes for Distributed Quantum Fault Sensing","summary":"Targeted recovery in modular quantum processors requires identifying the component affected by a fault. Independent qubits can monitor faults through repeated preparation and measurement. Bell-pair probes improve the guaranteed correct-site probability for unknown local channels, but require partners kept away from the fault. Can entanglement among exposed probes supply this advantage without protected storage? We establish a quantum-code criterion for optimal distributed fault sensing of one unknown local qubit channel with strength in a prescribed positive range. For one interrogation with zero false alarms and no background noise, suitable code states attain the optimal worst-case correct-site probability, improving on independent probes by 50% with every qubit exposed. Exact attainment is equivalent to mutually orthogonal input and single-qubit Pauli error subspaces. Pairwise entanglement gives no improvement, and entanglement depth at most four cannot exceed 95.54% of the optimum without protected ancillas. Approaching the optimum without protected ancillas also requires a number of probes proportional to the array size to participate in larger entangled factors. For a persistent fault under memoryless depolarizing background of fixed strength 0 < eta < 1, independent probes already attain the optimal logarithmic interrogation scaling at fixed success between zero and one and a positive false-alarm budget. In three 1000-site comparisons with specified readouts, an encoded-probe protocol certifies fewer interrogations than independent probes and only one more than optimally decoded ideal Bell pairs, while storing no protected qubits.","authors":"Xiu-Hao Deng","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph","cond-mat.mes-hall"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22530"},{"id":"2611.22546","title":"Multilevel Effects and the Limits of the Quantum Rabi Model in Ultrastrong Coupling system","summary":"The spectrum of artificial atoms coupled to resonators in the ultrastrong coupling regime have been analyzed using the quantum Rabi model over a wide range of coupling strengths. However, the accuracy of models obtained by truncating multilevel artificial atoms to two levels, as well as the correspondence between effective parameters extracted from spectral fitting and the bare parameters of the circuit Hamiltonian, depends on the multilevel structure of the system. In this study, I analyze the spectrum of an ultrastrongly coupled circuit consisting of a multilevel superconducting artificial atom and a resonator using a circuit Hamiltonian, and investigate the effects of truncating higher atomic levels on the low-energy spectrum. Based on L\\\"owdin partitioning, I evaluate the contributions of virtual transitions through higher atomic levels and show that diagonal energy corrections reduce the errors arising from simple two-level truncation over a wide parameter range. I also show that there are parameter regimes in which readjustment of the effective Rabi parameters successfully reproduces the multilevel spectrum, while systematic deviations remain in other regimes. These results demonstrate the necessity of separately evaluating two-level truncation, perturbative corrections, and effective-parameter fitting in ultrastrongly coupled systems, and provide guidance for quantitative spectral analysis of multilevel quantum circuits.","authors":"Akiyoshi Tomonaga","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22546"},{"id":"2611.22621","title":"Entanglement Lower Bounds for Symmetric Quantum Circuits","summary":"Symmetric circuits require every symmetry of the input to extend to an automorphism of the circuit. In the classical setting, their connection to descriptive complexity yields strong unconditional lower bounds. We extend this approach to the quantum model of symmetric computation defined by Castro-Silva, Gur, and Strelchuk (ITCS 2026), proving a tradeoff between circuit size and entanglement width (the smallest $b$ such that, on every computational-basis input, the state after each layer factors into states on at most $b$ qubits each). Our main result identifies a graph property decidable in classical polynomial time that no polynomial-size family of symmetric quantum circuits on $n$-vertex graphs with entanglement width $o(n/\\log n)$ can compute. The bound holds for circuits with arbitrary one-qubit and two-qubit gates and unrestricted depth, and remains valid under the weaker notion of symmetry with respect to even permutations of vertices. Our proof is based on a new quantum pebble game based on the partial trace. The resulting indistinguishability theorem transfers classical pebble-game lower bounds to quantum circuits with restricted entanglement. By contrast, without entanglement restrictions, we show that every graph property in BQP can be computed by a uniform family of polynomial-size symmetric quantum circuits. Restricting to circuits with no entanglement at all, we show that polynomial-size symmetric quantum circuits with entanglement width 1 can compute every graph property definable in fixed-point logic with counting. Finally, we give an efficient symmetric implementation of QAOA for MAX 3XOR, providing a starting point for studying entanglement lower bounds in quantum optimization algorithms.","authors":"Davi Castro-Silva, Ryan Dancy, Sergii Strelchuk","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["cs.CC","cs.LO","quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22621"},{"id":"2611.22633","title":"Measurement-Conditioned Bloch-Sphere State Steering with a Continuous-Variable Pointer","summary":"We investigate measurement-conditioned state steering of a qubit coupled to a continuous-variable pointer through a state-dependent displacement interaction. A position measurement on the pointer induces a nonunitary conditional transformation of the qubit, allowing its post-measurement state to move along a trajectory on the Bloch sphere. We derive the conditional state, the measurement-outcome distribution, and the corresponding Bloch-sphere coordinates. For a real Gaussian pointer, the conditional state follows a meridian, whereas a phase-engineered Gaussian pointer produces a trajectory with a measurement-dependent azimuthal angle. We further describe outcome-dependent unitary feedback for preparing a prescribed target state from a known initial qubit state. We distinguish this state-preparation task from implementing a universal deterministic single-qubit gate, which does not follow from the proposed interaction and measurement alone. Finally, we derive the quantum Fisher information of the joint qubit--pointer state for estimating the displacement-generating parameter and discuss the implications of pointer squeezing, phase engineering, and experimental implementation. The results provide a framework for analysing conditional Bloch-sphere steering using continuous-variable measurement resources.","authors":"Necati \\c{C}elik","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22633"},{"id":"2611.21715","title":"The SymTFT and Quantum Phases of Matter","summary":"The symmetry topological field theory (SymTFT) provides a unified framework for ordinary and generalized symmetries of quantum many-body systems. By encoding symmetry in a topological theory in one higher dimension, it separates symmetry data from the details of a specific quantum system. In this review, we give a pedagogical introduction to this framework and survey its applications to phases of quantum matter. For fusion category symmetries in ${(1+1)}$d, we explain how the SymTFT characterizes anomalies, classifies gapped phases, and organizes the symmetry structure of gapless phases. We also give a broad overview of further developments of this framework, including extensions to symmetries in higher dimensions, continuous symmetries, fermionic systems, mixed states, and spacetime symmetries.","authors":"Salvatore D. Pace, Sakura Schafer-Nameki, Carolyn Zhang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["cond-mat.str-el","hep-ph","hep-th","quant-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21715"},{"id":"2611.22232","title":"MASQ: Measurement-Adaptive Sampling for Near-Clifford Quantum Error Correction Circuits","summary":"Classical sampling of quantum error correction (QEC) circuits supports fault-tolerant protocol evaluation, but estimating rare logical failures requires many independent shots. Frame-active-state simulators isolate non-stabilizer information in a dense active-state vector, yet dense-state processing remains exponential in the active width. This paper presents MASQ, an exact measurement-adaptive sampler for near-Clifford QEC circuits based on selective materialization of non-Clifford rotations. At each measurement boundary, MASQ materializes only rotations in a measurement-relevant closure capturing direct and indirect non-commuting dependencies, deferring the rest to limit active-state expansion. Stabilizer-assisted elimination further removes rotations certified to be invisible to all future measurements. A circuit-dependent schedule is computed once and shared across shots, while adaptive caching of conditional Born probabilities and batched execution reduce repeated sampling work while preserving the measurement-record distribution. Across 19 QEC benchmarks, MASQ reduces peak active width by up to 40 active virtual qubits and achieves up to 8,098-fold CPU throughput speedup over Clifft on multi-round circuits.","authors":"Chaebin Jung, Sangmin Jeon, Kyeongwon Lee, Sechan Park, Woojoo Lee","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["cs.ET","quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22232"},{"id":"2507.10784","title":"Quantum Advantage in Storage and Retrieval of Isometry Channels","summary":"Storage and retrieval refer to the task of encoding an unknown quantum channel $\\Lambda$ into a quantum state, known as the program state, such that the channel can later be retrieved. There are two strategies for this task: classical and quantum strategies. The classical strategy uses multiple queries to $\\Lambda$ to estimate $\\Lambda$ and retrieves the channel based on the estimate represented in classical bits. The classical strategy turns out to offer the optimal performance for the storage and retrieval of unitary channels. In this work, we analyze the asymptotic performance of the classical and quantum strategies for the storage and retrieval of isometry channels. We show that the optimal fidelity for isometry estimation is given by $F = 1-{d(D-d)\\over n} + O(n^{-4/3})$, where $d$ and $D$ denote the input and output dimensions of the isometry, and $n$ is the number of queries. This result indicates that, unlike in the case of unitary channels, the classical strategy is suboptimal for the storage and retrieval of isometry channels, which requires $n = \\Theta(\\epsilon^{-1})$ to achieve the diamond-norm error $\\epsilon$. We propose a more efficient quantum strategy based on port-based teleportation, which stores the isometry channel in a program state using only $n = \\Theta(1/\\sqrt{\\epsilon})$ queries, achieving a quadratic improvement over the classical strategy. As an application, we extend our approach to general quantum channels, achieving improved program cost compared to prior results by Gschwendtner, Bluhm, and Winter [Quantum \\textbf{5}, 488 (2021)].","authors":"Satoshi Yoshida, Jisho Miyazaki, Mio Murao","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.10784"},{"id":"2508.05346","title":"Geometric encoding of turbulence for end-to-end quantum simulation","summary":"Simulating multiscale turbulence in engineering remains challenging. Any quantum advantage for such simulations requires efficient initial-state preparation. Yet exact loading of an arbitrary classical snapshot requires exponential quantum resources. We introduce \"turbuloscope\", a physics-informed geometric method that generates statistically representative turbulent initial states in a gate-based register from a parameterized Pauli-spinor multiscale distribution. Scale invariance renders conditional rotations approximately affine in Gray-code space, enabling linear-depth, ancilla-free preparation. The generalized Madelung transform and Hopf fibration map the encoded Pauli spinor to fluid observables and vortex tubes. The qubit count scales logarithmically with the Reynolds number, yielding an exponential reduction in preparation overhead relative to full-grid classical spectral synthesis. We classically emulate a 30-qubit preparation circuit and generate a turbulent field over one billion grid points at a scale-inferred Reynolds number of 35,000, exhibiting Kolmogorov's five-thirds energy spectrum, tangled vortex tubes, and intermittency. This method provides scalable initial states for quantum simulation of turbulence and multiscale systems.","authors":"Zhaoyuan Meng, Xiao-Ming Zhang, Xiao Yuan, Yue Yang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph","nlin.CD","physics.flu-dyn"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.05346"},{"id":"2510.21075","title":"Harnessing Intrinsic Noise for Quantum Simulation of Open Quantum Systems","summary":"Simulating open quantum systems on quantum computers is challenging because dissipative dynamics are nonunitary, whereas gate-based quantum algorithms are typically built from unitary operations. Conventional approaches can overcome this mismatch by embedding nonunitary processes into enlarged unitary circuits, often with ancillary-qubit and gate overhead. In this work, we propose an alternative perspective in which we selectively preserve the physical noise to emulate nonunitary channels. More specifically, we present a partial encoding algorithm that iteratively leverages intrinsic noise to mimic the dynamics of a target open-system. We benchmark the convergence of this algorithm against the dissipative exciton transport Hamiltonian and retrieve their dynamics. By utilizing intrinsic hardware noise to emulate the target nonunitary dynamics, this approach avoids the dual overhead of error-correcting that noise and using ancillary qubits to re-introduce nonunitarity. This significantly reduces both qubit requirements and physical fidelity thresholds, paving the way for more efficient techniques to simulate open quantum systems on quantum computers.","authors":"Sameer Dambal, Akira Sone, Yu Zhang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21075"},{"id":"2601.17675","title":"Realisation of a Protected Cat-Qutrit Manifold via Engineered Quantum Tunnelling","summary":"Engineering quantum tunnelling in phase space has emerged as a viable method for creating a protected logical qubit manifold with biased-noise properties. A promising approach is to combine a Kerr nonlinearity with a multi-photon drive, resulting in a system known as a Kerr parametric oscillator (KPO). In this work, we implement a three-photon KPO and explore its potential as a protected bosonic qutrit. We confirm quantum coherence by demonstrating three-photon Rabi oscillations and performing direct Wigner function measurements that reveal the formation of three-component cat-like states. Crucially, we observe a breathing-like dynamic in phase space, a characteristic feature of driven quantum systems. This dynamic arises from macroscopic temporal interference between the cat-qutrit manifold and the excited states. The frequency of resulting oscillations in the mean photon number provides a direct, time-domain measurement of the energy gap separating the qutrit from the excited states, thereby establishing an experimental hallmark of qutrit manifold protection. Furthermore, we identify a parasitic higher-order pump term as the primary mechanism constraining the mean photon number, highlighting its mitigation as a requisite for maximising protection. Our findings elucidate the basic quantum properties of the three-photon KPO and establish the first step towards its use as an alternative qutrit platform.","authors":"Sangil Kwon, Daisuke Hoshi, Toshiaki Nagase, Daichi Sugiyama, Hiroto Mukai, Kengo Takemura, Rintaro Kojima, Yu Zhou, Shohei Watabe, Fumiki Yoshihara, Jaw-Shen Tsai","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.17675"},{"id":"2601.20263","title":"A Quantum Photonic Approach to Graph Coloring","summary":"Gaussian Boson Sampling (GBS) is a quantum computational model that leverages linear optics to solve sampling problems believed to be classically intractable. Recent experimental breakthroughs have demonstrated quantum advantage using GBS, motivating its application to real-world combinatorial optimization problems. In this work, we reformulate the graph coloring problem as an integer programming problem using the independent set formulation. This enables the use of GBS to identify cliques in the complement graph, which correspond to independent sets in the original graph. Our method is benchmarked against classical heuristics and exact algorithms on two sets of instances: Erd\\H{o}s-R\\'enyi random graphs and graphs derived from a smart-charging use case. The results demonstrate that GBS can provide competitive solutions, highlighting its potential as a quantum-enhanced heuristic for graph-based optimization.","authors":"Jesua Epequin, Pascale Bendotti, Joseph Mikael","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph","cs.DM"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.20263"},{"id":"2602.06171","title":"Quantum-enhanced Markov Chain Monte Carlo for Combinatorial Optimization","summary":"Quantum computing offers an alternative paradigm for addressing combinatorial optimization problems compared to classical methods. However, despite recent hardware improvements, the execution of empirical quantum optimization experiments at scales known to be hard for state-of-the-art classical solvers is not yet in reach. In this work, we offer a different way to approach combinatorial optimization with near-term quantum computing. Motivated by the promising results observed in using quantum-enhanced Markov chain Monte Carlo (QeMCMC) for approximating complicated probability distributions, we combine ideas of sampling from the device with QeMCMC together with warm-starting and parallel tempering, in the context of combinatorial optimization. We demonstrate empirically that our algorithm recovers the global optima for instances of the Maximum Independent Set problem (MIS) up to 117 decision variables using 117 qubits on IBM quantum hardware. We observe an indication of more favorable scaling relative to the tested classical MCMC methods on the chosen MIS instances. MIS is practically relevant across domains such as financial services and molecular biology. Although some MIS instances with a few hundred variables can be difficult for classical solvers, the instances studied here are solved efficiently by CPLEX.","authors":"Kate V. Marshall, Daniel J. Egger, Michael Garn, Francesca Schiavello, Sebastian Brandhofer, Christa Zoufal, Stefan Woerner","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06171"},{"id":"2604.07214","title":"Quantum Gibbs sampling through the detectability lemma","summary":"Gibbs state preparation is an important subroutine in quantum computing. In this work we use the detectability lemma to improve Gibbs state preparation. Specifically, we design new Gibbs state preparation methods that do not rely on simulating Lindbladian evolution, thus avoiding the overhead from it. For local Lindbladians consisting of $M$ terms, this approach reduces the cost by a factor of $O(M)$. We also combine the detectability lemma operator and quantum singular value transformation to implement ground state projection operators of frustration-free Hamiltonians, resulting in a quadratic speedup in the spectral gap dependence. Applying this method to Lindbladians for the Gibbs state of local commuting Hamiltonians, we achieve quadratically better dependence on the Lindbladian spectral gap.","authors":"Di Fang, Jianfeng Lu, Yu Tong, Chu Zhao","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph","cs.NA","math-ph","math.MP","math.NA"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.07214"},{"id":"2605.02565","title":"Sample-Based Quantum Diagonalization with Amplitude Amplification","summary":"Recently, sample-based quantum diagonalization (SQD) has emerged as a promising approach to compute ground and excited states of problem Hamiltonians. This method classically diagonalizes a Hamiltonian in a subspace that is spanned by samples obtained from a quantum computer. However, by its nature, SQD suffers from a fundamental sampling problem, as some basis states that are required for a targeted accuracy may only be sampled extremely rarely. To alleviate this limitation, we introduce the SQD-AA algorithm that combines SQD with amplitude amplification (AA). SQD-AA uses AA to sequentially reduce probabilities of already measured bitstrings, thus making the observation of new ones more likely. We observe a reduction in the total query complexity of more than a factor 100 for algebraically and exponentially decaying model distributions, and analytically show a quadratic advantage for the latter. Moreover, we evaluate real molecules in an early fault-tolerant scenario and compare SQD-AA to SQD and iterative quantum phase estimation (iQPE). For almost all considered examples, we observe the lowest total number of T-gates for SQD-AA while only requiring circuits that are 2-4 orders of magnitude shallower than those needed for iQPE. Given this substantial reduction in circuit depth compared to iQPE while saving 2 orders of magnitude in total runtime compared to SQD, we expect a significant regime in early fault-tolerance where SQD-AA runs feasibly, but iQPE circuits are too deep to execute confidently.","authors":"Nina Stockinger, Ludwig N\\\"utzel, Michael J. Hartmann","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.02565"},{"id":"2611.06448","title":"Helium States Inspired by Quantum Photonics Circuit","summary":"We present a four-mode Fock-state photonics implementation of the variational quantum deflation (VQD) algorithm to calculate the ground-state and excited-state energies for the neutral helium atom in a CAS(2,2) Subspace. Its CAS(2,2) electronic Hilbert space is encoded into a bosonic Fock-space representation and subsequently optimized using a continuous-variable quantum circuit. The circuit architecture under study is constructed of single-mode phase shifts, beam splitters, and cross-Kerr interactions. The variational circuit is initialized in the Fock state $\\ket{1100}$ and its performance is benchmarked against exact diagonalization of the active-space configuration-interaction (CASCI) Hamiltonian. We also extend the method to optimize from the first to three excited states of the helium atom with the aid of the variational quantum deflation $(VQD)$ method in a CAS(2,2). We then investigate the performance of this circuit architecture as a basis for implementing the VQD method. In the ideal noiseless simulation setting, we see that the survival probability of the ground and the excited states within the target $CAS$ subspace is unity, because the total photon number and the photon number in the two spin sectors $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are preserved. We demonstrate that the proposed approach enables the determination of the ground and excited-state energies within the CAS(2,2) electronic Hilbert space using a continuous-variable quantum circuit and the variational quantum deflation ($VQD$) algorithm, while satisfying the orthogonality conditions with negligible errors.","authors":"Forouzan Mirmasoudi, Badie Ghavami","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph","cond-mat.mtrl-sci"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06448"},{"id":"2611.18091","title":"Efficient Iterative Symmetrization algorithm for quantum computing and hadron applications","summary":"Efficient algorithms for the (anti)symmetrization of quantum registers are necessary for upcoming few- and many-body physics applications, from atomic to particle-physics scales. We propose a recursive algorithm (anti)symmetrizing the last particle added to memory, each time one is created or destroyed, respect to the rest of already (anti)symmetrized ones. Given a generalized Dicke state (GDS) of $(j-1)$ quantum registers, each assigned to a particle, the algorithm produces another GDS of $j$ registers (of $n_{qb}$ qubits each) using a mere $\\mathcal{O}(j\\log_2^2 j + j n_{qb})$ $\\mathtt{CNOT}$ and single-qubit gates, and $3j+\\lceil\\log_2j\\rceil+\\mathcal{O}(\\max(n_{qb},\\log_2 j))$ clean auxiliary qubits. The (anti)symmetrizing operator constructed for this purpose depends on the quantum numbers of the additional particle being introduced or ``created''. The algorithm improves upon the construction presented in Appendix A of \\cite{Galvez-Viruet:2024hry} by almost two powers of $j$ and is on par with the most efficient methods to date, while ideally suited for dynamical memory management with variable particle number. We exemplify its applicability with a small selection of problems from hadron physics which are expected to be addressable with upcoming quantum computers.","authors":"Nahia J. Dios-Bilbao, Adri\\'an Casta\\~no-Garc\\'ia, J. J. G\\'alvez-Viruet, Felipe J. Llanes-Estrada, Mario Logros\\'an-\\'Alvarez, Nicol\\'as M. Arenaza","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","nucl-th","quant-ph"],"topics":["computing","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18091"},{"id":"2611.21239","title":"What kind of quantum advantage is this?","summary":"Quantum advantage encompasses fundamentally different claims, from resource reductions to exponential separations in sample complexity. Evaluating these claims requires distinguishing how an algorithm achieves an advantage from what is necessary for the separation and how strongly it is supported against classical alternatives. We propose a multi-label taxonomy that makes these distinctions explicit, classifying structural, interference, and coherent mechanisms while separately recording their necessity and the evidence for the classical comparison. Coherent mechanisms are further divided into memory and output, reflecting distinct restrictions on quantum information processing. A two-part entanglement-breaking test distinguishes loss of information from loss of algorithmic efficiency. The hidden subgroup problem illustrates the distinction: among the cases examined, cross-copy coherence is information-theoretically necessary only for the symmetric group. We present this as a diagnostic methodology rather than a theory of quantum advantage: for a new claim, it identifies the relevant adversary, separates the mechanisms a protocol uses from those shown to be necessary, and records the strength of the evidence and the quality of the advantage separately.","authors":"Christophe Pere","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21239"},{"id":"2608.27944","title":"Quantum geometric bounds at finite temperature for one-dimensional chiral systems","summary":"The geometry and topology of quantum states are intimately related at zero temperature through exact bounds that constrain geometric quantities from below by topological invariants. At finite temperature, however, the analogous relations remain unclear. Here we establish rigorous geometric lower bounds for one-dimensional chiral-symmetric systems at finite temperature within the Uhlmann's framework for mixed states. We show that the Bures length is bounded by a continuous geometric phase angle. We further derive a temperature-dependent bound that interpolates between the zero-temperature limit and a trivial high-temperature regime. Our results are verified analytically and numerically using the Su-Schrieffer-Heeger model and the spinless Kitaev chain model. Finally, we discuss potential ways to detect the geometry of the density matrix in quantum circuits, with the quantum imaginary-time evolution method.","authors":"Peng He, Hai-Tao Ding, Yu-Guo Liu","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["cond-mat.mes-hall","quant-ph"],"topics":["computing"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.27944"},{"id":"2611.21807","title":"On the Structure of the Nuclear-Clock Isotope $^{229}$Th Beyond Quadratic Order","summary":"Precision measurements of the exceptionally low-energy nuclear transition of $^{229}$Th, offer an extraordinary sensitivity to a variety of new light fields. However, interpreting such measurements requires nuclear-structure information beyond what is currently constrained by spectroscopy. We develop a systematic description of the electric multipole interactions governing the hyperfine structure of ${}^{229}$Th, including the hexadecapole contribution, and perform a symmetry-based analysis of the resulting hyperfine splittings in crystalline environments. Hyperfine spectroscopy generally constrains combinations of nuclear moments and crystal-field gradients, leading to degeneracies when higher multipole interactions are allowed. We then combine experimental nuclear observables, particle-rotor relations, and a deformed-Fermi charge-density model to investigate the electromagnetic contribution to the nuclear clock's sensitivity to variations in the fine-structure constant. We find that higher-order nuclear deformation and state-dependent differences in the radial charge distribution of the clock states can substantially alter the predicted sensitivity, introducing large uncertainties that are not constrained by existing measurements. Our results highlight the importance of combining nuclear and solid-state theory with complementary measurements, including spectroscopy of higher-spin states, to constrain the nuclear structure and improve predictions of the clock's sensitivity to new physics. Our symmetry-based analysis also provides a framework for using hyperfine spectroscopy to investigate local defect symmetries in crystalline hosts.","authors":"Chaja Baruch, Doron Gazit, Hans-Werner Hammer, Fiona Kirk, Joachim Kopp, Gil Paz, Ekkehard Peik, Gilad Perez, Yotam Soreq, Konstantin Springmann","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","nucl-ex","nucl-th","physics.atom-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21807"},{"id":"2611.21810","title":"Probing scalar-mediated Lorentz violation with hydrogen masers and spin-precession of molecules","summary":"We propose using hydrogen maser spectroscopy and molecular spin-precession experiments, traditionally employed in precision frequency measurements and searches for the electron electric dipole moment, as complementary probes of light scalar-mediated, spin-dependent fifth forces. We investigate their sensitivity to Lorentz-violating interactions mediated by scalars with masses ranging from $\\mathcal{O}(10^{-4}~\\mu\\mathrm{eV})$ to $\\mathcal{O}(10~\\mathrm{keV})$. While hydrogen maser spectroscopy and molecular spin-precession measurements probe different combinations of Lorentz-violating couplings, we find that the strongest projected sensitivities can be achieved using spin-precession of ultracold molecules, benefiting from their extended coherence times. These experimental approaches provide access to previously unexplored regions of parameter space, probing interaction ranges extending from centimeter to sub-angstrom scales. Our proposals highlight the potential of precision atomic and molecular experiments to search for new spin-dependent scalar-mediated interactions and violations of fundamental spacetime symmetries.","authors":"Djuna Croon, Tanmay Kumar Poddar","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","physics.atom-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21810"},{"id":"2611.22201","title":"Quality Control for the LGAD sensors of the ATLAS High Granularity Timing Detector","summary":"The High Granularity Timing Detector (HGTD) currently in the production phase will mainly mitigate the pile-up effect in the ATLAS forward region and serve for bunch-by-bunch luminosity measurements in the high-luminosity (HL) phase of the Large Hadron Collider (LHC). The HGTD utilizes Low Gain Avalanche Detector (LGAD) technology, covering the pseudorapidity range between 2.4 and 4.0, and provides high-precision timing information to distinguish collisions occurring close in space but well-separated in time. A pre-production batch of 8-inch silicon wafers was fabricated and a systematic quality control test campaign was carried out. This paper describes the Quality Control (QC) required for full-size (15 $\\times$ 15 array) sensors, including leakage-current and breakdown measurements performed on all sensors at the vendor sites, as well as more detailed electrical characterizations at the participating ATLAS-HGTD institutes. In addition, the electrical characterization of full-size sensors after irradiation is reported.","authors":"K. Ma, W. Sun, M. Ait Tamlihat, H. M. Alfanda, N. Atanov, O. Atanova, I. Azzouzi, J. Barreiro Guimar\\~aes Da Costa, L. T. Bazzano Hurrell, T. Beau, D. Benchekroun, F. Bendebba, Y. Bimgdi, A. Blot, A. Boikov, D. Boumediene, C. Brito, A. S. Brogna, L. Cadamuro, Y. Cai, N. Carolino, N. Cartalade, Y. Che, X. Chen, M. Christiansen, P. Cistac, S. E. D. Clawson, L. D. Corpe, G. Costi, C. G. Crozatier, O. Cunha, L. D'Eramo, S. Dahbi, D. Dannheim, G. Daubard, Yu. I. Davydov, J. Debevc, Y. Degerli, E. Delagnes, F. Deliot, C. de La Taille, M. Dhellot, C. Dimitriadis, P. Dinaucourt, G. Di Gregorio, P. J. Dos Santos De Assis, C. Duan, O. Duarte, F. Dulucq, S. El Farrkh, Y. El Ghazali, A. El Moussaouy, R. Estevam de Paula, A. Falou, L. Fan, Z. Fan, Y. Fan, K. Farman, F. Fassi, M. Ferreira, F. Filthaut, F. Fischer, G. Frattari, J. Fu, P. Fust\\'e, J. Garc\\'ia Rodriquez, G. Gaspar De Andrade, Z. Ge, M. G. D. Gololo, R. Gon\\c{c}alo, M. Gouighri, S. Grinstein, K. Gritsay, F. Guilloux, S. Guindon, J. Guo, A. Haddad, S. E. D. Hammoud, L. Han, A. M. Henriques Correia, B. Hiti, B. Hodkinson, J. Hofner, S. Hou, P. J. Hsu, K. Hu, Y. Huang, X. Huang, C. Insa, J. Jeglot, X. Jia, S. F. Koch, G. Kramberger, M. Kuriyama, B. Y. Ky, D. Lacour, A. Lafarge, B. Lakssir, A. Lantheaume, D. Laporte, A. Leopold, T. J. Lepota, M. Li, Y. Li, L. Li, S. Li, H. Li, Z. Li, Z. Liang, S. Liang, C. Liao, M. A. Lisboa Leite, Y. L. Liu, K. Liu, K. Liu, Y. W. Liu, H. Lu, M. Lu, Y. J. Lu, F. L. Lucio Alves, X. Ma, D. Macina, R. Madar, N. Makovec, S. Malyukov, I. Mandi\\'{c}, T. Manoussos, S. Manzoni, G. Martin-Chassard, F. Martins, L. Masetti, R. Mazini, E. Mazzeo, R. Mekouar, R. Menegasso, J. -P. Meyer, Y. Miao, M. Mihovilovic, M. Milovanovic, M. Missio, V. Moskalenko, N. Mouadili, A. Moussa, Y. Nie, I. Nikolic-Audit, C. C. Ohm, H. Okawa, M. Ouchrif, C. P\\'en\\'elaud, B. Pascual Dias, Y. Peng, J. Pinol Bel, P. -O. Puhl, C. Puigdengoles Olive, M. Puklavec, M. Qi, J. Qin, H. Ren, S. Ridouani, R. T. Rimer, V. Rogozin, W. Roth, L. Royer, F. Rudnyckyj, G. T. Saito, A. Salem, H. Santos, Ph. Schwemling, N. Seguin-Moreau, L. Serin, R. P. Serrano Fernandez, Q. Sha, A. Shaikovskii, L. Shan, R. Shen, X. Shi, P. Skomina, H. Smitmanns, H. L. Snoek, A. P. Soulier, A. Stein, H. Stenzel, J. Strandberg, X. Sun, Y. Sun, K. Tariq, K. Tau, Y. Tayalati, S. Terzo, S. A. Thayil, S. Trincaz-Duvoid, L. F. Van Duren Watak, U. M. Vande Voorde, I. Velkovska, R. V. Vieira, L. A. Vieira Lopes, A. Visibile, M. Vogt, J. Wan, H. Wang, S. Wang, Z. Wang, C. Wang, Y. Wang, Y. Wang, A. Wang, T. Wang, T. Wang, S. M. Wang, Q. Weitzel, C. Wen, C. Wu, J. Wu, Y. Wu, W. Wu, Y. Wu, M. Wu, L. Xia, H. Xu, D. Xu, L. Xu, Z. Yan, X. Yang, H. Yang, H. Yang, J. Ye, Y. Yi, I. Youbi, J. Yuan, I. Zahir, T. Zhang, J. Zhang, Z. Zhang, L. Zhang, D. Zhang, M. Zhao, Z. Zhao, G. Zheng, X. Zheng, Z. Zhou, Y. Zhu, X. Zhuang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["physics.ins-det"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22201"},{"id":"2603.03353","title":"Improved Pion-Kaon Identification in Heavy-Ion Collisions with a Two-Dimensional Transformation","summary":"Accurate identification of charged pions and kaons is essential for precision measurements in relativistic heavy-ion collisions, but becomes increasingly challenging at intermediate and high transverse momentum because of the increasing overlap of the TPC and TOF particle identification responses. In this work, we quantitatively benchmark a two-dimensional shift-and-rotation PID procedure that combines the $n\\sigma_{\\pi}$ and mass-squared $(m^{2})$ information. The benchmark is performed using Au+Au collision events generated with the AMPT model, where the detector response is modeled using $p_{T}$-dependent PID parameters extracted from STAR data. Since the true particle identities and input yields are known in AMPT but are not used during the reconstruction, the procedure can be evaluated through a direct closure test that is not possible with experimental data alone. The two-dimensional procedure is compared with a reference one-dimensional method consisting of an $|n\\sigma_{\\pi}|<$ 3 preselection followed by a Gaussian fit to the $m^{2}$ distribution. While both methods perform well when pion and kaon PID responses are well separated, the two-dimensional procedure provides improved yield closure as the PID distributions increasingly overlap at high $p_{T}$. The reconstructed-to-input yield ratio remains within 2\\% of unity up to $p_T =$ 3 GeV/$c$, whereas the reference one-dimensional procedure shows a larger deviation. Reaction-plane $v_{2}$ closure tests show that both the reference one-dimensional and two-dimensional PID procedures reproduce the AMPT input $v_{2}$ within the statistical uncertainties over the studied $p_{T}$ range. These results demonstrate that the two-dimensional procedure substantially improves the high-$p_{T}$ yield reconstruction without introducing a detectable bias in the extracted $v_{2}$ under the STAR-like detector response considered here.","authors":"Shaowei Lan, Bijun Fan, Like Liu","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","nucl-ex","physics.ins-det"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.03353"},{"id":"2609.03822","title":"Optimisation of importance sampling implementation in rare-event shielding simulations using Geant4","summary":"Rare-event search experiments demand ever-lower backgrounds, and the resulting radio-pure materials and suppressive shielding make related background simulations computationally expensive without event biasing. We present a method for optimising the widely used importance splitting and Russian roulette technique for rare-event searches, in which a whole number of equal-width importance layers fill the shielding geometry. Balancing relative uncertainty against execution time, we optimise the layer thickness for both $\\gamma$-rays and neutrons, and across different shielding materials and thicknesses. Expressed as a multiple of the primary particle's mean free path, the optimal thickness generalises across energies for a given particle type and yields a physics-based prediction applicable to any shielding design. The same trend reveals that over-biasing is possible, with relative uncertainty worsening at high layer counts for fixed execution time. The optimal configuration gives an $\\mathcal{O}$(20) gain in computational efficiency over the unbiased case.","authors":"P. Knights, L. J. Milligan, K. Nikolopoulos","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","physics.comp-ph","physics.ins-det"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.03822"},{"id":"2610.13634","title":"A machine-learning method for the estimation of the background from jets misidentified as hadronic tau lepton decays","summary":"Backgrounds arising from quark- and gluon-initiated jets misidentified as hadronically decaying tau leptons constitute a major experimental challenge for analyses involving tau leptons. The MUltivariate Fake-Factor INference (MUFFIN) approach, a machine-learning-based method for estimating this background directly from observed events, is presented. Boosted decision tree reweighting is used to learn multidimensional, per-object correction factors directly from observed data, thereby modelling the jet to hadronic tau lepton misidentification probability across the kinematic and topological phase space without relying on low-dimensional parametrisations. Performance studies are presented using proton-proton collision data collected by the CMS experiment at the CERN LHC at $\\sqrt{s}$ = 13.6 TeV, corresponding to an integrated luminosity of 62.4 fb$^{-1}$. Compared to low-dimensional parametrisation methods, improved modelling of key kinematic observables is achieved, particularly in regions of high $p_\\mathrm{T}$. The improved modelling leads to a reduction of up to 70% in the systematic uncertainty associated with jets being misidentified as hadronically decaying tau leptons. The MUFFIN method provides a robust and scalable method for background estimation in precision measurements and searches for beyond-the-standard-model physics involving tau lepton final states.","authors":" CMS Collaboration","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","physics.ins-det"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13634"},{"id":"2611.21733","title":"Baryonification V: Thermal Sunyaev-Zel'dovich maps from dark-matter-only lightcones","summary":"Baryonic feedback is a major theoretical uncertainty for Stage-IV weak lensing (WL) surveys. The thermal Sunyaev-Zel'dovich (tSZ) effect traces the hot gas redistributed by feedback, so combining it with WL can calibrate feedback and improve cosmological constraints. Doing so with simulation-based inference requires correlated lensing and Compton-$y$ maps across many cosmologies and feedback scenarios. We present two methods to generate Compton-$y$ maps from dark-matter-only $N$-body light cones using the component-wise baryonification (BFC) model, which produces separate gas and stellar fields and computes the gas pressure from hydrostatic equilibrium. The two methods, painting pressure profiles onto halos or redistributing the signal along the baryonified gas, agree to within a few per cent. We calibrate the model to the fiducial and strongest AGN feedback FLAMINGO simulations by jointly fitting gas mass fractions and thermal pressure profiles, measuring the non-thermal pressure from the simulated gas velocities rather than treating it as a free parameter. The predicted Compton-$y$ profiles match FLAMINGO within halo-to-halo scatter, and when painted onto the FLAMINGO halos we reproduce the tSZ power spectrum of both feedback models to $\\sim5\\%$ for $500\\lesssim\\ell\\lesssim3000$. Applied to a \\emph{CosmoGridV1-like} $N$-body simulation, our maps agree with FLAMINGO within $\\sim10\\%$ for $300\\lesssim\\ell\\lesssim8000$; the remaining differences come from the few hundred most massive clusters, and removing them from both halo catalogs brings the two into agreement. Our method thus enables accurate and efficient tSZ forward models from $N$-body simulation suites such as CosmoGridV1, an important step towards joint WL and tSZ simulation-based inference for Euclid, LSST, and the Simons Observatory.","authors":"Michael Kova\\v{c}, Andrina Nicola, Jozef Bucko, Siyu Bian, Aurel Schneider, Robert Reischke, Jessica A. Cowell, Alexandre Refregier, Sambit K. Giri, Matthieu Schaller, Joop Schaye","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO"],"topics":["dark"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21733"},{"id":"2611.21821","title":"A Forecast for Testing the Decreasing $H_0$ Trend in Time-Delay Cosmography","summary":"In 2019 the H0LiCOW/TDCOSMO collaboration reported a decreasing Hubble constant $H_0$ trend with lens redshift in the flat $\\Lambda$CDM model at low statistical significance ($< 2 \\sigma$). The signal is expected if physics is missing from the $\\Lambda$CDM model in the late universe. In the wake of recent TDCOSMO 2026 results, we revisit the feature. Although the statistical significance of the trend has increased in a like-for-like comparison with earlier studies, propagating mass-sheet transformation (MST) uncertainties reduces the significance to $\\sim 1 \\sigma$. As a result, Akaike Information Criterion (AIC) currently favours a constant $H_0$ over a decreasing $H_0$ in the ratio 2.4:1. Nevertheless, we highlight good agreement on a negative slope with independent observables and forecast that approximately 40 and 90 lenses will be required to substantiate the trend at $\\sim 2 \\sigma$ and $\\sim 3 \\sigma$ significance, respectively, if the feature is physical and current uncertainties are representative. Even now, it is evident that the TDCOSMO 2026 $H_0$ constraint $H_0 \\approx 71$ km/s/Mpc reflects an average over the lens redshift distribution and is naturally anchored at intermediate lens redshifts.","authors":"Shane Brady, Eoin \\'O Colg\\'ain, M. M. Sheikh-Jabbari","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21821"},{"id":"2611.21729","title":"Supernova Bounds on Neutrino Dipole Moments from a Modern Perspective","summary":"Neutrinos may have nonstandard magnetic dipole moments $\\mu_\\nu$. In the dense cores of supernovae (SNe), these would convert part of the neutrinos into sterile, right-handed states through the electromagnetic fluctuations of the charged plasma. The streaming sterile neutrinos with 100-MeV energies can convert back to standard neutrinos in the Galactic magnetic field, leading to a high-energy neutrino flux unobserved from the historical SN 1987A. We reassess the production rates, including scattering on both protons and electrons, and, building upon advances in SN simulations and in the description of the Milky Way magnetic field, we revisit the bounds on magnetic dipole moments. We find for Dirac electron neutrinos $\\mu_{\\nu_e} \\lesssim 4.8 \\times 10^{-13} \\mu_B$, superseding all existing terrestrial and astrophysical constraints.","authors":"Damiano F. G. Fiorillo, Martina La Rosa, Federico Testagrossa, Edoardo Vitagliano","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","astro-ph.HE","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21729"},{"id":"2611.22266","title":"Gravitational waves from cosmological first-order phase transitions with fluctuation-induced friction","summary":"We study gravitational-wave production in a cosmological first-order phase transition with fluctuation-induced friction using three-dimensional lattice simulations of two coupled scalar fields. Kinetic and gradient energy in the additional field $s$ accumulates near the expanding bubble walls of $\\phi$ and affects their motion. The contribution from $s$ exceeds that from $\\phi$ at the spectral peak and has a slope similar to that of the bulk flow model over a finite interval above the peak. In the high-wave-number (UV) direction, $\\phi$ dominates and the total spectrum develops a broad feature. For the larger coupling, slowly contracting false vacuum remnants persist long after bubble merger.","authors":"Jun-Ye Chen, Dongdong Wei, Zong-Kuan Guo","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","gr-qc","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22266"},{"id":"2605.24089","title":"Exploring the Dark Sector: Interacting Radiation in Light of Modern Cosmological Probes","summary":"[abridged] We constrain a phenomenological dark radiation (DR) framework consisting of free-streaming and fluid-like components, providing a model-independent extension of the standard radiation sector. Using Planck CMB data, DESI DR2 BAO measurements, and Pantheon+ and DES Y5 (Dovekie) supernova samples, we derive constraints on additional relativistic degrees of freedom and assess their impact on cosmological tensions. We obtain $N_{\\rm fld}<0.66$ (95% C.L.) from CMB data alone, while the combination with BAO yields $N_{\\rm fs}=2.93\\pm0.23$ and $N_{\\rm fld}=0.36^{+0.16}_{-0.21}$ (68% C.L.), consistent with Standard Model expectations for free-streaming radiation. The DR framework significantly alleviates the Hubble tension through an enhanced early-time expansion rate, which reduces the sound horizon scale. The tension with SH0ES is reduced from highly significant in $\\Lambda$CDM to statistically non-significant for CMB+BAO data according to the $\\mathcal{T}$-statistic. Bayesian model comparison shows no decisive preference for DR over $\\Lambda$CDM when SH0ES is excluded, with results in the regime of weakly disfavoured. However, including SH0ES data leads to decisive Bayesian evidence in favour of the DR scenario. Overall, DR provides a compelling framework for resolving the Hubble tension. When CMB, BAO, Pantheon$+$ and SH0ES data are considered, we find an increased effective radiation content, $N_{\\rm tot}=3.63^{+0.13}_{-0.15}$, with a fraction of free-streaming radiation, $f_{\\rm fs}=0.392\\pm 0.026$, a reduced sound horizon scale, $r_d = 141.8^{+1.3}_{-1.2}\\,\\mathrm{Mpc}$, and a higher primordial helium fraction, $Y_{\\rm He}=0.2530 \\pm 0.0017$, which lies at the level of approximately $\\sim 2$-$2.5\\sigma$ above direct determinations from metal-poor H II regions, while remaining broadly consistent with other abundance measurements within current uncertainties.","authors":"Matteo Mescia, H\\'ector Gil-Mar\\'in","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO"],"topics":["dark"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.24089"},{"id":"2611.05504","title":"Gravitational waves flooding the Universe after wall domination","summary":"We discuss a possible early Universe scenario, where radiation era is interrupted by the epoch of domination of domain walls eventually annihilating into particles. During this period the Universe undergoes accelerated expansion, while the comoving correlation length of the domain wall network remains constant. The network serves as a source of tensor modes, which get frozen upon exiting the horizon similarly to the case of inflationary fluctuations. The modes re-enter the horizon during the subsequent stage of radiation domination and since then contribute to the gravitational wave background. The period of wall domination is manifested in the spectral shape of gravitational waves via a distinctive plateau replacing a peak, which is characteristic for the scenario with sub-dominant walls. The gravitational waves are strong enough to be observed with present-day and future facilities in a broad range of frequencies. In particular, their energy density is sufficient to produce a significant impact on the effective number of neutrino species, unless the duration of wall domination is suitably constrained.","authors":"Sabir Ramazanov, Zhen-Min Zeng","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05504"},{"id":"2505.01492","title":"Photo- and Hadrodisintegration constraints on massive relics decaying into neutrinos","summary":"We perform a detailed study of the cosmological constraints on the decay of a relic particle $\\phi$ into neutrinos, $\\phi \\rightarrow \\nu \\bar{\\nu}$, in particular those arising from the observed light-element abundances in the early Universe. We focus on the late-time disintegration of the light elements previously synthesised during BBN. Several processes are relevant, including final-state radiation associated with the decay, as well as subsequent interactions of the injected neutrinos with the thermal background neutrinos or between themselves. All processes generically contribute to the production of electromagnetic and often also hadronic material and may therefore induce late-time photodisintegration and hadrodisintegration reactions, i.e.~the destruction of light elements that have previously been formed during BBN. Here, we examine this scenario with a Monte-Carlo inspired probabilistic approach rather than Boltzmann techniques, taking into account all of these different reactions as well as their interplay. We find the resulting constraints to be very significant, covering a broad range of previously unexplored masses and lifetimes of the relic source particle.","authors":"Sara Bianco, Paul Frederik Depta, Jonas Frerick, Thomas Hambye, Marco Hufnagel, Kai Schmidt-Hoberg","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.01492"},{"id":"2606.02729","title":"Probabilistic Causality from Graviton Fluctuations","summary":"We compute the commutator of a scalar field minimally coupled to gravity at leading order in $G_N$. The commutator is operator-valued, with terms involving derivatives of Dirac deltas supported on the Minkowski light cone. When evaluated on classical/coherent graviton states, these terms ``bend\" the support of the commutator in precisely the way required to recover standard causality on a classical curved spacetime. However, these terms are also associated with a variance and are thus a source of uncertainty in the causal relations between events. We quantify this effect for a thermal state of gravitons at temperature $T$. In the large-time limit, the uncertainty of the lightcone position is given by $$ \\text{Var}(\\delta x) =\\frac{4G_NTt}{3}\\, , $$ where $\\delta x$ is the displacement from the classical lightcone at $|\\vec x| = t$. We obtain this result by subtracting a universal vacuum contribution that is logarithmically UV divergent and subleading at late times.","authors":"Giordano Cintia, Federico Piazza, Samuel Ramos","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","gr-qc","hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.02729"},{"id":"2606.11062","title":"A friendly phantom: Late-time AdS-to-dS transition and cosmological tensions","summary":"We present Ph-$\\Lambda_{\\rm s}$CDM, a phantom-scalar realization within general relativity of the sign-switching cosmological-constant idea, $\\Lambda_{\\rm s}$CDM, in which a phantom scalar evolving on a bounded hyperbolic-tangent potential induces a smooth mirror AdS-to-dS transition in the late-time dark-energy density. The wrong-sign kinetic term, usually viewed as pathological, becomes the mechanism lifting the field from a negative- to a positive-energy vacuum-like regime. The construction also shows that the field can become repulsive while its energy density is still negative. The cosmology nevertheless remains controlled: total energy stays positive, the late-time attractor is de Sitter rather than a Big Rip, and the dynamics remain safely infrared. Ph-$\\Lambda_{\\rm s}$CDM thus offers a concrete late-time mechanism with the potential to address multiple cosmological tensions.","authors":"\\\"Ozg\\\"ur Akarsu, Leandros Perivolaropoulos, A. Emrah Y\\\"ukselci, Alexander Zhuk","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","gr-qc","hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.11062"},{"id":"2607.27344","title":"Inflation in unimodular loop quantum cosmology","summary":"We study inflation in the setting of unimodular loop quantum cosmology, where time evolution is defined in unimodular time rather than with respect to a free, massless scalar field as is standard in loop quantum cosmology. The unimodular setting leads to a natural Schr\\\"odinger time evolution in a time coordinate with clear geometric meaning, defined independently of any particular matter content; an inflaton can be included but is not needed as a clock. We review the unimodular version of loop quantum cosmology and comment on possible connections to full (unimodular) loop quantum gravity. Then, focusing on semiclassical effective equations, we derive analytical solutions in simple cases such as a constant potential, emphasising the use of a unimodular time coordinate. We also discuss numerical solutions for phenomenologically interesting cases such as a quadratic potential and Starobinsky inflation, comparing different possible choices of initial conditions. In particular, we show that choosing an $\\alpha$-attractor potential allows for models of a bounce either dominated by kinetic or potential energy, which are compatible with observations while potentially including observable imprints of the quantum-gravity regime.","authors":"Steffen Gielen, Rita B. Neves","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","gr-qc","hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.27344"},{"id":"2608.01762","title":"Primordial Correlators from a Kaluza-Klein Graviton Continuum","summary":"Cosmological collider signals are usually discussed for isolated massive particles, whose exchange produces characteristic logarithmic oscillations in primordial correlators. In this work, we study how this signal is modified when the exchanged states form a continuous mass spectrum. We first develop a spectral representation for inflationary correlators mediated by a continuum field. In the soft limit, the non-analytic part of the seed function is expressed as a Fourier--Laplace transform of the spectral weight with respect to logarithmic momentum variables. This representation shows that a continuum superposes clock signals with different frequencies and can dephase the sharp oscillation associated with a single massive particle. We then realize this mechanism in an RS2-like inflationary braneworld, where the inflaton is localized on a de Sitter brane in a five-dimensional AdS bulk. The tensor sector contains a localized massless graviton and a continuum of Kaluza-Klein gravitons starting at \\(m=3H/2\\). We derive the KK wavefunctions and identify the brane spectral weight fixed by the continuum wavefunction on the brane. Applying this weight to the inflaton four-point function, we find a smooth seed function rather than a persistent logarithmic clock oscillation. This behavior follows from the fact that the continuum starts at zero clock frequency, while the KK spectral weight vanishes near threshold. Our results provide a concrete higher-dimensional example in which deviations from the standard cosmological collider signal encode the structure of a continuum high-energy spectrum.","authors":"Shuntaro Aoki","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","gr-qc","hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle","qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.01762"},{"id":"2609.26767","title":"Tachyonic particle production in strongly supercooled phase transitions","summary":"In strongly supercooled cosmological phase transitions, which generically lead to strong, observable gravitational-wave backgrounds, the scalar field typically transitions to a value well below the minimum of its potential. We study particle production caused by the subsequent evolution of the field. Because some modes feature temporarily tachyonic masses, they can be efficiently produced, and a significant fraction of the energy density released in the phase transition can get converted to particles rather than gradient energy. This has consequences for the shape and amplitude of the gravitational-wave spectrum caused by the interactions in bubbles and sources a new, high-frequency signal.","authors":"Mateusz Kulejewski, Bogumila Swiezewska, Jorinde van de Vis","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.CO","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26767"},{"id":"2611.21712","title":"Hidden wormholes from an action principle in $D=4$","summary":"We present new black hole solutions for the class of gravitational effective field theories in four spacetime dimensions that extend General Relativity while retaining the graviton as the only degree of freedom of gravitational origin. We consider terms up to cubic order in curvature, solve the resulting field equations, and find one-way hidden wormholes as numerical solutions: these are regular black holes with an interior wormhole throat that is crossable in one direction and leads to a cosmological spacetime in its interior. They are regular and have no inner horizon, thus evading the mass inflation instability that afflicts conventional regular black hole geometries.","authors":"Francesco Del Porro, Alessia Platania, Samuele Silveravalle","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc","hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21712"},{"id":"2611.21722","title":"Cosmic Censorship in the Landscape of SUSY-Broken AdS Vacua","summary":"We investigate signatures of weak cosmic censorship in non-supersymmetric vacua of both IIA and IIB string theory. In particular, we examine the Penrose Inequality, which can be proven holographically using Ryu-Takayanagi, and can be independently proven in SUSY AdS vacua for spherically symmetric initial data. We prove that any EFT of scalars coupled to gravity in AdS, including in particular those of string compactifications, satisfies both the Penrose Inequality and a positive mass theorem if a function that we call a \"weak fake superpotential\" exists. We demonstrate that this condition is satisfied globally for the DGKT skew-whiffed vacua in IIA. Since there is no known nonperturbative instability in this DGKT vacuum, this provides evidence that the IIA SUSY-broken AdS vacua may be absolutely stable. We also find the weak fake superpotential exists locally in field space in some SUSY-broken AdS vacua in a KKLT construction in IIB. Thus, within the framework considered here, some false SUSY-broken KKLT AdS vacua are stable against vacuum decay. Conversely, we find that some KKLT vacua do not have a weak fake superpotential, and correspondingly violate the Penrose Inequality and fail to have a positive mass theorem; the DGKT vacua only violate the Penrose Inequality in the presence of designer gravity boundary conditions. In all cases studied, this is due to false vacuum decay. We discuss the implications of these violations for holography and black hole thermodynamics in these SUSY-broken AdS vacua.","authors":"Netta Engelhardt, Richard Nally","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc","hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21722"},{"id":"2611.22041","title":"Quantum Born Geometries from Modular Quantization","summary":"A structural link between quantization and \"thermal-doubled\" non-commutative Born geometry (with its split-quaternionic structure) is uncovered via Tomita-Takesaki first-quantized modular theory for complexified and polarized ortho-symplectic spaces. Implications for the modular algebraic quantum gravity program (arXiv:1007.4094) hint at further deep modifications of the notions of space and geometry (thermal quantum space-time hypothesis) waiting to be further explored. Speculative suggestions in the direction of A.Sakharov and L.Boyle-K.Finn-N.Turok CPT-mirror cosmologies will be mentioned, among several others. The present work is based on the manuscript \"Modular Quantization and Non-commutative Space-time\" (now on alphaXiv) that has been shared, with a selected number of researchers, since end of April 2026.","authors":"Paolo Bertozzini, Roberto Conti","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc","hep-th","math-ph","math.MP","math.OA","math.SG"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22041"},{"id":"2607.24277","title":"Modified Mukhanov-Sasaki equation and primordial perturbations in $\\kappa$-deformed non-commutative space-time","summary":"We study the inflationary primordial perturbations in $\\kappa$-Minkowski non-commutative space-time, a Lie-algebraic type deformation of canonical space-time motivated by quantum gravity scenarios. Employing the $\\kappa$-deformed star product formalism, we construct the bilinear action for curvature perturbations and derive the $\\kappa$-deformed Mukhanov-Sasaki equation and obtain the perturbative solutions. Further, we compute the primordial power spectrum and spectral index, showing that the leading order corrections to the power spectrum induces scale-dependent term proportional to $(\\ln k)^2$. The spectral index also exhibits an explicit $\\ln k$ dependence, which persists even when the slow-roll parameters are constant. We also perform a Bayesian MCMC analysis using ACT DR6 data and constrain the $\\kappa$-deformation length scale to $\\lambda=1.93^{+1.88}_{-1.33}\\times 10^{-29}m$ at $1\\sigma$ CL, approximately four orders of magnitude larger than the Planck scale, demonstrating that the $\\kappa$-deformed space-time offers a potential window into quantum gravity phenomenology through precision cosmology.","authors":"Lei Yang, Vishnu Rajagopal, Puxun Wu","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.24277"},{"id":"2603.23824","title":"Gravitational-lensing-modulated leptonic CP violation","summary":"The discovery of high-energy astrophysical neutrinos has recently prompted interest in the role of gravity in neutrino flavor conversion. In this work, we investigate the gravity-modulated CP violation (CPV) in neutrino oscillations in the Reissner--Nordstr\\\"om and Hayward spacetimes. Employing analytical and numerical methods, we reveal the interplay among the CPV, the properties of neutrinos, and the space-time. The shapes of the flavor-oscillation curves show that CPV signals encode both space-time geometry and mass ordering, and that the imprints of $m_l$ are intrinsically correlated with the singular versus nonsingular structure of the background metric. In particular, our finding demonstrate that the CPV observables exceed the limitation that, in asymptotically flat space-time, gravity neither damps nor amplifies neutrino-oscillation amplitudes.","authors":"Ze-Wen Li, Shu-Jun Rong, Ya-Ru Wang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23824"},{"id":"2604.12854","title":"Mixed-helicity bracket of celestial symmetries","summary":"Celestial symmetries of gravity and gauge theory can be enhanced to a $w_{1+\\infty}$ algebra and an $S$-algebra respectively, when restricting to a single graviton/gluon helicity sector. Difficulties in combining both sectors in the full theory have been pointed out in the previous literature. In this work, we face this problem from the covariant phase space perspective and analyze in detail the structure of the mixed-helicity bracket of the higher-spin charges for both gravity and Yang--Mills theory. We show that, when restricting one of the two helicities to the wedge sector, a closed algebra can be obtained for all spins in terms of a notion of shadow charge we introduce. Furthermore, when focusing on the lower spin subalgebra sectors, in the case of gravity, we show that a dual mass extension of the BMS algebra can be consistently constructed; in the case of Maxwell theory, inclusion of magnetic charges allows us to recover a non-vanishing expression for the electromagnetic central charge previously obtained through different methods.","authors":"Daniele Pranzetti, Domenico Giuseppe Salluce","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc","hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.12854"},{"id":"2606.08080","title":"Weyl conformal geometry vs Riemannian geometry of Weyl gauge invariant (dressed) metric","summary":"Weyl conformal geometry is the natural underlying geometry of gauge theories of Weyl group (of dilatations and Poincar\\'e symmetry), such as Weyl quadratic gravity and its generalisation, Weyl-Dirac-Born-Infeld action (WDBI). These are local, Weyl-anomaly free (quantum) gauge theories of gravity. We describe Weyl gauge symmetry from a more familiar Riemannian view of Weyl gauge invariant dressed fields by the Wilson line of dilatations. Weyl geometry can then be seen as Riemannian geometry of non-local dressed metric ($g_{\\mu\\nu}^*$), at the \"cost\" of (gauge-induced) non-commutativity in the UV, due to Wilson line. Then Weyl quadratic gravity and WDBI actions of Weyl geometry, which are Weyl gauge invariant in $d$ dimensions, have the same expression in Riemannian geometry defined by $g^*_{\\mu\\nu}$. This is a non-local map and dual description of the two geometries and actions in the symmetric phase. Unlike for the metric, the equation of motion of Weyl gauge field ($\\omega_\\mu$) does not commute with the dressing of the metric. Quantum non-locality, in particular entanglement, and non-commutativity are (gauge-invariant) physical artefacts of \"translating\" (local) Weyl geometry and Weyl gauge covariance into our real-world Riemannian geometry of Weyl gauge invariant observables, and they are evidence of Weyl gauge symmetry. At lower energies, $\\omega_\\mu$ becomes massive, can decouple and Einstein-Hilbert action and commutativity are recovered. The case of a light $\\omega_\\mu$ is also discussed.","authors":"D. M. Ghilencea, V. -M. Mandric","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc","hep-ph","hep-th","math-ph","math.MP"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.08080"},{"id":"2609.10677","title":"Stokes Phenomena between AdS/CFT and dS/CFT","summary":"As parameters are varied, the set of saddle points contributing to a (path) integral may change discontinuously, leading to a corresponding change in the asymptotic expansion of the integral. This behavior is known as the Stokes phenomenon. We explore this phenomenon in the context of the analytic continuation problem relating the AdS/CFT and dS/CFT correspondences. In this paper, we study these correspondences for three-dimensional pure gravity and two-dimensional Liouville theory, using independent calculations in bulk minisuperspace and in the boundary Liouville zero-mode. In the bulk, the dS contour selects a single saddle and yields the tunneling wave function. Upon continuation to AdS, the contour instead selects an infinite family of saddles. The boundary calculation independently reproduces the same Stokes structure at leading semiclassical order, providing a nontrivial holographic consistency check. Our construction also offers a contour prescription for the conformal factor problem in Euclidean AdS$_3$ quantum gravity within minisuperspace.","authors":"Masazumi Honda, Kotaro Shinmyo","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc","hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10677"},{"id":"2610.11664","title":"Extremal black hole mimickers: geometry, stability, and long-delay signal trains","summary":"We present a spacetime that serves as a horizonless mimicker of extremal black holes. Unlike previously studied mimickers of black holes with non-degenerate horizons, whose geometries are of the wormhole type and consist of two asymptotically flat regions connected by a very long throat, the mimicker of an extremal black hole possesses a single asymptotically flat region at one end and an infinitely long throat at the other. In the throat region, the spacetime approaches the direct-product geometry $M_{1,1} \\times S^2$, where $M_{1,1}$ is two-dimensional Minkowski spacetime and $S^2$ is the two-sphere. We investigate scalar-field dynamics and the fate of the Aretakis instability. Although Aretakis-like modes arise in a local analysis of the throat, they do not lead to a physical instability of the mimicker. A scalar perturbation initially localized in the throat gives rise, at late times, to a sequence of signals with a characteristic separation $t_{\\rm delay}\\sim 1/\\lambda$, where $\\lambda\\ll 1$ is a deformation parameter. This timescale is parametrically larger than the echo time $t_{\\rm echo}\\sim \\ln(1/\\lambda)$ characteristic of non-extremal black-hole mimickers. We refer to this sequence as a {\\it long-delay signal train}. Geometrically, the periodicity of the long-delay signals is associated with periodic geodesics on $S^2$. We then extend the construction to extremal Kerr. In this case, the throat region approaches a generalized product geometry involving $M_{1,1}$ and a two-dimensional surface $\\Sigma_2$. The corresponding signals are expected to form a quasi-periodic signal train, reflecting the quasi-periodic geodesic motion on $\\Sigma_2$. We also comment on the generalization to wormhole mimickers of non-extremal black holes and on the implications of these results for unitarity in the context of the AdS/CFT correspondence.","authors":"Sergey N. Solodukhin","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["gr-qc","hep-th","math-ph","math.MP"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.11664"},{"id":"2611.21752","title":"Mapping the multimessenger horizon for $\\gamma$-ray counterparts of cosmic neutrinos","summary":"The joint detection of high-energy neutrinos and $\\gamma$-rays offers a powerful probe of the most energetic astrophysical accelerators, linking hadronic interactions to observable signatures across cosmic distances. Understanding the conditions under which these two messengers can be simultaneously observed is essential for identifying and characterizing the sources of cosmic neutrinos. In this work, we examine the detectability of $\\gamma$-ray cascades associated with neutrino production in source environments that are gamma-ray transparent, focusing on how electromagnetic cascades evolve as $\\gamma$-rays propagate through intergalactic space. By modeling photon interactions with the extragalactic background light and deflections in intergalactic magnetic fields, we determine the minimum neutrino fluence for transient emission and neutrino flux for steady emission required to produce a $\\gamma$-ray counterpart detectable by $\\gamma$-ray observatories, such as Fermi-LAT, HAWC, or LHAASO. This defines a ``multimessenger horizon\" -- the region in flux-distance parameter space where neutrinos and $\\gamma$-rays are jointly observable. We consider both energy-binned injection tied to IceCube's sensitivity and a continuous power-law spectrum extending from TeV to PeV energies. Our results reveal that detectability depends strongly on redshift, magnetic field strength, and photon energy, with higher-energy cascades suffering greater attenuation inside filamentary structures. The framework provides new constraints on source opacity, baryon loading, and neutrino production mechanisms, offering a predictive tool for interpreting current events and guiding future multimessenger campaigns.","authors":"Tianyi Ding, Jose Alonso Carpio, Ali Kheirandish, Kohta Murase","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.HE","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21752"},{"id":"2611.21788","title":"Neutron-Rich Disks, Nickel-Rich Ejecta: Can Direct-Horizon Collapsars Explain Early $r$-Process Enrichment?","summary":"Very massive stars above the pair-instability mass gap may collapse directly into black holes surrounded by massive disks. These \"direct-horizon collapsars\" accrete at $\\dot{M} \\gtrsim 10\\,M_\\odot\\,{\\rm s}^{-1}$ and have been proposed to eject a substantial amount of $r$-process material, powering \"super-kilonovae\". We present the first three-dimensional general-relativistic magnetohydrodynamic simulations of direct-horizon collapsars with M1 neutrino transport, following a $\\approx 150\\,M_\\odot$ helium core from collapse through the escape of magnetically driven outflows. Extreme accretion rates neutronize the disks to $Y_{\\rm e} \\approx 0.1$-$0.25$ and, at early times, allow them to satisfy the gravitational-instability and cooling criteria for fragmentation. However, the strong poloidal fields needed to eject the neutron-rich gas promptly also drain the disks and lift their electron degeneracy. The neutron-rich ejecta composition is set in a brief phase, such that the ejecta carry $\\sim 0.1$-$0.3\\,M_\\odot$ of nuclei with $A \\geq 69$, including $\\lesssim 2\\times10^{-4}\\,M_\\odot$ of lanthanides, alongside $\\sim 1$-$4\\,M_\\odot$ of $^{56}$Ni. Assuming proportional retention and mixing of Eu and Fe, these yields imply $[{\\rm Eu/Fe}]\\approx-0.4$ to $+0.3$, and simple dilution estimates permit enrichment at $[{\\rm Fe/H}]\\lesssim-2.6$. Direct-horizon collapsars are therefore potential prompt contributors to iron and $r$-process enrichment at low metallicity. Their predicted Eu/Fe ratios fall below those of strongly $r$-process-enhanced stars, but uncertainties in the relative yields, particularly of iron, may close this gap. The ${}^{56}{\\rm Ni}$-rich ejecta would power luminous supernovae with rise times of weeks, potentially accompanied by exceptionally powerful gamma-ray bursts, rather than lanthanide-rich super-kilonovae.","authors":"Danat Issa, Ore Gottlieb","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.GA","astro-ph.HE","astro-ph.SR"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21788"},{"id":"2611.22417","title":"Relativistic tidal excitation of g- and i-modes in neutron star binaries","summary":"When the gravitational wave frequency of an inspiraling binary matches a normal-mode frequency of a neutron star (NS), the mode can be resonantly excited. The excitation transfers orbital energy to the star and shifts the phase of the emitted gravitational-wave (GW) signal, thereby providing a potential probe of the NS interior. However, estimates of this phase shift in the literature vary by orders of magnitude due to different approximations and formulations used in calculating the tidal coupling strength. We revisit this problem using a fully relativistic scattering formalism, which serves as our benchmark calculation and provides a gauge-invariant mode response together with an effective tidal-mode coupling strength. We calculate the phase shifts induced by the excitation of stratification g-modes, crustal interface i-modes, and hadron-quark interface i-modes, and estimate their detectability with Fisher-matrix forecasts for current and next-generation detectors. In our models, direct g-mode excitation remains below the detection thresholds, even for the strongest stratification considered. Crust-core i-modes at large shear speeds provide the most favorable prospects and crust melting can produce a substantially larger phase shift. Hadron-quark i-modes remain below the next-generation detection thresholds.","authors":"Binyu Zhong, Zhiqiang Miao, Zhen Pan, Huan Yang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.HE"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22417"},{"id":"2611.22044","title":"Current Accretion State versus Black Hole Mass: Imprints on Multiphase Gas in IllustrisTNG","summary":"The coupling between AGN feedback and the multiphase circumgalactic medium (CGM) is central to understanding galaxy evolution. In a previous paper, we showed that the black hole mass ($M_{\\rm BH}$) correlates negatively with cold gas mass in IllustrisTNG100. We now extend this to the Eddington ratio ($\\lambda_{\\rm Edd}$), which traces the current accretion state, using the same sample of 5089 central galaxies at $z=0$ under the same group-specific partial correlation framework, controlling for stellar and dark matter halo mass. We find that $\\lambda_{\\rm Edd}$ correlates positively with warm gas in star-forming galaxies ($\\rho \\approx 0.6$-$0.7$), persisting after jointly controlling for $M_{\\rm BH}$, the mass in the other non-star-forming gas phases, and specific star formation rate. The $\\lambda_{\\rm Edd}$-cold gas correlation instead reverses sign, from negative in star-forming galaxies ($\\rho \\approx -0.32$) to positive in quenched galaxies ($\\rho \\approx 0.40$). Controlling for $M_{\\rm BH}$ leaves this correlation essentially unchanged in all three galaxy types. Further controlling for the total gas mass in the other phases strengthens the star-forming correlation and weakens the quenched correlation to $\\rho \\approx 0.21$, which remains significant. These results establish the Eddington ratio as a phase- and type-dependent tracer of the gas-black hole connection: the cold gas reservoir records the integrated black hole growth, the warm gas in star-forming galaxies carries the signature of the current accretion state, and the sign reversal of the cold-gas correlation marks the transition between the star-forming and quenched regimes.","authors":"Xiaoxia Zhang, Taotao Fang, Shulan Yan, Si-Yue Yu, Feng Yuan","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.GA","astro-ph.HE"],"topics":["dark"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22044"},{"id":"2602.05955","title":"Improved Heavy Dark Matter Annihilation Search from Dwarf Galaxies with HAWC","summary":"Understanding dark matter's elusive nature is crucial for the framework of particle physics and expanding the Standard Model. This analysis utilizes the High Altitude Water Cherenkov (HAWC) gamma ray Observatory to indirectly search for dark matter (DM) by studying gamma ray emission from dwarf spheroidal galaxies (dSphs). Selected for their high ratio of dark matter to baryonic matter, dSphs are useful for this type of search owing to the low background emission. In comparison to previous HAWC studies, we significantly improve our sensitivity to DM from dSphs due to improvements to our event reconstruction and reduced hadronic contamination. We expanded the number of dSphs studied, DM annihilation channels into the Standard Model (SM), and the amount of data collected on each previously studied dSph. We searched for DM signals in each dSph using the latest version of the algorithms used to reconstruct data from the primary detector of the HAWC instrument. We report that we do not detect evidence of DM from dSphs, so we place upper limits for the velocity-weighted DM annihilation cross-section ($\\langle\\sigma v \\rangle$) on the order of $10^{-23}~\\text{cm}^3\\text{s}^{-1}$ for a DM mass range of $1-10^4$ TeV.","authors":"A. Albert, R. Alfaro, C. Alvarez, A. Andr\\'es, E. Anita-Rangel, M. Araya, J. C. Arteaga-Vel\\'azquez, D. Avila Rojas, H. A. Ayala Solares, R. Babu, P. Bangale, E. Belmont-Moreno, A. Bernal, K. S. Caballero-Mora, T. Capistr\\'an, A. Carrami\\~nana, F. Carre\\'on, S. Casanova, A. L. Colmenero-Cesar, U. Cotti, J. Cotzomi, S. Couti\\~no de Le\\'on, E. De la Fuente, C. de Le\\'on, P. Desiati, N. Di Lalla, R. Diaz Hernandez, M. A. DuVernois, J. C. D\\'iaz-V\\'elez, K. Engel, T. Ergin, C. Espinoza, N. Fraija, S. Fraija, A. Galv\\'an-G\\'amez, J. A. Garc\\'ia-Gonz\\'alez, F. Garfias, N. Ghosh, A. Gonzalez Mu\\~noz, M. M. Gonz\\'alez, J. A. Gonz\\'alez, J. A. Goodman, J. Gyeong, J. P. Harding, S. Hern\\'andez-Cadena, I. Herzog, D. Huang, F. Hueyotl-Zahuantitla, P. H\\\"untemeyer, A. Iriarte, S. Kaufmann, D. Kieda, K. Leavitt, W. H. Lee, J. Lee, H. Le\\'on Vargas, J. T. Linnemann, A. L. Longinotti, G. Luis-Raya, C. Lundy, K. Malone, O. Martinez, J. Mart\\'inez-Castro, H. Mart\\'inez-Huerta, J. A. Matthews, P. Miranda-Romagnoli, P. E. Mir\\'on-Enriquez, J. A. Morales-Soto, E. Moreno, M. Mostaf\\'a, M. Najafi, A. Nayerhoda, L. Nellen, M. U. Nisa, R. Noriega-Papaqui, N. Omodei, E. Ponce, Y. P\\'erez Araujo, E. G. P\\'erez-P\\'erez, C. D. Rho, A. Rodriguez Parra, D. Rosa-Gonz\\'alez, M. Roth, H. Salazar, D. Salazar-Gallegos, A. Sandoval, M. Schneider, J. Serna-Franco, M. Shin, A. J. Smith, Y. Son, R. W. Springer, O. Tibolla, K. Tollefson, I. Torres, R. Torres-Escobedo, F. Ure\\~na-Mena, E. Varela, L. Villase\\~nor, X. Wang, Z. Wang, I. J. Watson, H. Wu, S. Yu, X. Zhang, H. Zhou","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.HE"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.05955"},{"id":"2605.19040","title":"IceCube Second Track Data Release IceTracks-DR2: Data from 2008-2022 for Neutrino Source Searches","summary":"We present IceCube's latest release of muon track data for neutrino point-source searches, extending the previously published 10-year dataset to cover 14 years of observations (April 6, 2008 - May 23, 2022). This release features an updated event selection and improved detector calibration for data recorded after June 1, 2010. The release also includes binned instrument response functions and effective areas, enabling the community to perform sensitive searches for steady and transient neutrino sources. We report on key science results obtained with this dataset using internal IceCube analysis tools and compare them to those derived from analyses based on the binned response functions included in this public release. To facilitate reproducible research, we provide benchmark results obtained using this data release and publicly available software. This release represents IceCube's most sensitive and comprehensive publicly available all-sky muon track dataset to date and should be preferred over previous releases.","authors":"R. Abbasi, M. Ackermann, J. Adams, J. A. Aguilar, M. Ahlers, J. M. Alameddine, S. Ali, N. M. Amin, K. Andeen, C. Arg\\\"uelles, Y. Ashida, S. Athanasiadou, S. N. Axani, R. Babu, X. Bai, A. Balagopal V., S. W. Barwick, V. Basu, R. Bay, J. J. Beatty, J. Becker Tjus, P. Behrens, J. Beise, C. Bellenghi, S. Benkel, S. BenZvi, D. Berley, E. Bernardini, D. Z. Besson, E. Blaufuss, L. Bloom, S. Blot, F. Bontempo, J. Y. Book Motzkin, C. Boscolo Meneguolo, S. B\\\"oser, O. Botner, J. B\\\"ottcher, J. Braun, B. Brinson, Z. Brisson-Tsavoussis, R. T. Burley, D. Butterfield, K. Carloni, J. Carpio, N. Chau, Y. C. Chen, Z. Chen, D. Chirkin, S. Choi, A. Chubarov, B. A. Clark, G. H. Collin, D. A. Coloma Borja, A. Connolly, J. M. Conrad, D. F. Cowen, C. De Clercq, J. J. DeLaunay, D. Delgado, T. Delmeulle, S. Deng, P. Desiati, K. D. de Vries, G. de Wasseige, T. DeYoung, J. C. D\\'iaz-V\\'elez, S. DiKerby, T. Ding, M. Dittmer, A. Domi, L. Draper, L. Dueser, D. Durnford, K. Dutta, M. A. DuVernois, T. Ehrhardt, L. Eidenschink, A. Eimer, C. Eldridge, P. Eller, E. Ellinger, D. Els\\\"asser, R. Engel, H. Erpenbeck, W. Esmail, S. Eulig, J. Evans, P. A. Evenson, K. L. Fan, K. Fang, K. Farrag, A. R. Fazely, A. Fedynitch, N. Feigl, C. Finley, D. Fox, A. Franckowiak, S. Fukami, P. F\\\"urst, J. Gallagher, E. Ganster, A. Garcia, M. Garcia, E. Genton, L. Gerhardt, A. Ghadimi, C. Glaser, T. Gl\\\"usenkamp, J. G. Gonzalez, S. Goswami, A. Granados, D. Grant, S. J. Gray, S. Griffin, K. M. Groth, D. Guevel, C. G\\\"unther, P. Gutjahr, C. Ha, A. Hallgren, L. Halve, F. Halzen, L. Hamacher, M. Handt, K. Hanson, J. Hardin, A. A. Harnisch, P. Hatch, A. Haungs, J. H\\\"au{\\ss}ler, K. Helbing, J. Hellrung, B. Henke, L. Hennig, F. Henningsen, L. Heuermann, R. Hewett, N. Heyer, S. Hickford, A. Hidvegi, C. Hill, G. C. Hill, R. Hmaid, K. D. Hoffman, A. Hollnagel, D. Hooper, S. Hori, K. Hoshina, M. Hostert, W. Hou, M. Hrywniak, T. Huber, K. Hultqvist, K. Hymon, A. Ishihara, W. Iwakiri, M. Jacquart, S. Jain, O. Janik, M. Jansson, M. Jin, N. Kamp, D. Kang, W. Kang, A. Kappes, L. Kardum, T. Karg, A. Karle, A. Katil, M. Kauer, J. L. Kelley, M. Khanal, A. Khatee Zathul, A. Kheirandish, T. Kim, H. Kimku, F. Kirchner, J. Kiryluk, C. Klein, S. R. Klein, Y. Kobayashi, S. Koch, A. Kochocki, R. Koirala, H. Kolanoski, T. Kontrimas, L. K\\\"opke, C. Kopper, D. J. Koskinen, P. Koundal, M. Kowalski, T. Kozynets, A. Kravka, N. Krieger, T. Krishnan, K. Kruiswijk, E. Krupczak, A. Kumar, E. Kun, N. Kurahashi, C. Lagunas Gualda, L. Lallement Arnaud, M. J. Larson, F. Lauber, J. P. Lazar, K. Leonard DeHolton, A. Leszczy\\'nska, C. Li, J. Liao, C. Lin, Q. R. Liu, Y. T. Liu, M. Liubarska, C. Love, L. Lu, F. Lucarelli, W. Luszczak, Y. Lyu, M. Macdonald, E. Magnus, Y. Makino, E. Manao, S. Mancina, A. Mand, I. C. Mari\\c{s}, S. Marka, Z. Marka, L. Marten, I. Martinez-Soler, R. Maruyama, J. Mauro, F. Mayhew, F. McNally, K. Meagher, A. Medina, M. Meier, Y. Merckx, L. Merten, J. Mitchell, L. Molchany, S. Mondal, T. Montaruli, R. W. Moore, Y. Morii, A. Mosbrugger, D. Mousadi, E. Moyaux, T. Mukherjee, M. Nakos, U. Naumann, L. Neste, M. Neumann, H. Niederhausen, M. U. Nisa, K. Noda, A. Noell, A. Novikov, A. Obertacke, V. O'Dell, A. Olivas, R. Orsoe, J. Osborn, E. O'Sullivan, B. Owens, V. Palusova, H. Pandya, A. Parenti, N. Park, V. Parrish, E. N. Paudel, L. Paul, C. P\\'erez de los Heros, T. Pernice, T. C. Petersen, J. Peterson, S. Pick, M. Plum, A. Pont\\'en, V. Poojyam, B. Pries, R. Procter-Murphy, G. T. Przybylski, L. Pyras, C. Raab, J. Rack-Helleis, N. Rad, M. Ravn, K. Rawlins, Z. Rechav, A. Rehman, I. Reistroffer, E. Resconi, C. D. Rho, W. Rhode, L. Ricca, B. Riedel, A. Rifaie, E. J. Roberts, S. Rodan, M. Rongen, A. Rosted, C. Rott, T. Ruhe, L. Ruohan, D. Ryckbosch, J. Saffer, D. Salazar-Gallegos, P. Sampathkumar, A. Sandrock, G. Sanger-Johnson, M. Santander, S. Sarkar, M. Scarnera, M. Schaufel, H. Schieler, S. Schindler, L. Schlickmann, B. Schl\\\"uter, F. Schl\\\"uter, N. Schmeisser, T. Schmidt, A. Scholz, F. G. Schr\\\"oder, S. Schwirn, S. Sclafani, D. Seckel, L. Seen, M. Seikh, S. Seunarine, P. A. Sevle Myhr, R. Shah, S. Shah, S. Shefali, N. Shimizu, B. Skrzypek, R. Snihur, J. Soedingrekso, D. Soldin, P. Soldin, G. Sommani, D. Song, C. Spannfellner, G. M. Spiczak, C. Spiering, J. Stachurska, M. Stamatikos, T. Stanev, T. Stezelberger, T. St\\\"urwald, T. Stuttard, G. W. Sullivan, I. Taboada, S. Ter-Antonyan, A. Terliuk, A. Thakuri, M. Thiesmeyer, W. G. Thompson, J. Thwaites, S. Tilav, K. Tollefson, J. A. Torres, S. Toscano, D. Tosi, K. Upshaw, A. Vaidyanathan, N. Valtonen-Mattila, J. Valverde, J. Vandenbroucke, T. Van Eeden, N. van Eijndhoven, L. Van Rootselaar, J. van Santen, J. Vara, F. Varsi, M. Venugopal, M. Vereecken, S. Vergara Carrasco, S. Verpoest, D. Veske, A. Vijai, J. Villarreal, C. Walck, A. Wang, E. H. S. Warrick, C. Weaver, P. Weigel, A. Weindl, J. Weldert, A. Y. Wen, C. Wendt, J. Werthebach, M. Weyrauch, N. Whitehorn, C. H. Wiebusch, D. R. Williams, L. Witthaus, G. Wrede, X. W. Xu, J. P. Yanez, Y. Yao, E. Yildizci, S. Yoshida, R. Young, F. Yu, S. Yu, T. Yuan, S. Yun-C\\'arcamo, A. Zander Jurowitzki, A. Zegarelli, S. Zhang, Z. Zhang, P. Zhelnin, P. Zilberman, C. Zilleruelo Ca\\~nas","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.HE","hep-ex"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.19040"},{"id":"2608.05106","title":"Constraining Lorentz invariance violation from the depth of air-shower maximum","summary":"Hypothetical Lorentz invariance violation (LIV) remains strongly constrained but has not been excluded as a possible manifestation of new high-energy physics. In subluminal photon-sector LIV, suppressed Bethe$\\unicode{x2013}$Heitler pair production modifies the electromagnetic component of extensive air showers. We present a simulation-based toy analysis of this effect using published Pierre Auger fluorescence-detector distributions of the reconstructed depth of air-shower maximum. The modified distributions of the depth of the shower maximum are folded with the Auger detector-response parametrizations and compared with the data using free composition fractions in each reconstructed-energy bin. Within this approach, the comparison gives a sensitivity to LIV compatible with that expected from ultra-high-energy photon-induced showers. We obtain $M_{\\rm LIV}\\gtrsim 6.5\\times 10^{18}\\,\\text{GeV}$ for the photon-sector LIV mass scale $M_{\\rm LIV}$ ($95\\%$ confidence level). This result should not be interpreted as a detector-level experimental limit because the detector response and reconstruction are simplified, and the result remains composition dependent. However, the approach presented here opens the way for constraining LIV in hadronic air showers with more detailed analyses.","authors":"Nickolay S. Martynenko, Grigory I. Rubtsov, Petr S. Satunin, Andrey K. Sharofeev, Sergey V. Troitsky","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.HE","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.05106"},{"id":"2608.28975","title":"Little Red Dots as Shock-Powered High-Energy Neutrino Sources","summary":"Little Red Dots (LRDs) are compact, high-redshift sources whose physical nature remains uncertain. Their optical spectra motivate a scenario in which their emission may be powered by shocks interacting with dense surrounding material. We investigate whether such interactions can power LRDs and contribute to the diffuse high-energy neutrino intensity measured by IceCube. In our simplified model, a fast central-engine outflow drives a shock through stationary or slow outflowing material, followed by a prescribed quasi-stationary interaction near the LRD photosphere. We consider the time-averaged neutrino emission from shock-active LRDs without requiring a single shock to persist throughout the LRD lifetime. We explore parameter ranges motivated by the observed and inferred properties of LRDs, finding solutions with shock luminosities 2.0e43 < L_s < 3.5e44 erg/s. Using an analytical framework for cosmic-ray acceleration and hadronic interactions in dense shock environments, we calculate the resulting high-energy neutrino emission and integrate it over the cosmological LRD population. For our fiducial cosmic-ray parameters, the average predicted contribution below 2e5 GeV increases from 0.2% for the lowest-luminosity quintile to 2% for the highest-luminosity quintile, while an illustrative higher-efficiency case reaches 13% of the IceCube diffuse neutrino intensity. These estimates assume all observed LRDs are shock-active. A smaller active fraction or a reduced covering factor would decrease the predicted intensity, whereas survey incompleteness could increase it. Improved models and population synthesis are needed to constrain the total LRD contribution.","authors":"Tyco Mera, Chris Ashall, Shunsaku Horiuchi, Rohan Naidu, Kyle Medler, Peter Hoeflich","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.GA","astro-ph.HE"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.28975"},{"id":"2609.28057","title":"A Diagnostic Method for Proto-Neutron Star Magnetic Fields by Supernova Fallback Neutrinos","summary":"Young isolated neutron stars exhibit a wide range of magnetic field strengths. To understand the origin of this diversity, it is important to observationally constrain magnetic fields during the proto-neutron star (PNS) phase. We propose a novel diagnostic of PNS magnetic fields using neutrinos associated with supernova fallback. By performing one-dimensional general-relativistic magnetohydrodynamic simulations of the supernova fallback onto a magnetized PNS, we investigate how the energy spectra of the fallback neutrinos depend on the PNS magnetic field strength and the fallback accretion rate. We find that stronger magnetic fields and lower accretion rates produce softer spectra because the fallback neutrinos are emitted mainly near the magnetospheric radius, which becomes larger under these conditions. To quantify this spectral softening, we define a hardness ratio based on event rates above and below 30 MeV and introduce a hardness-event-rate diagram. Using this diagram, we identify the parameter region in which our diagnostic is applicable. Its applicability depends on the PNS magnetic field strength, fallback accretion rate, onset time of the fallback phase, and neutrino mass ordering. We also investigate the dependence on the PNS radius and find that a larger radius produces softer spectra, indicating a degeneracy between the magnetic field and PNS compactness. An independent constraint on the PNS compactness would therefore enable us to better constrain the magnetic field. These results suggest that future multimessenger observations of galactic supernovae can provide a new window into the NS diversity.","authors":"Akihiro Inoue, Yudai Suwa, Ryuichiro Akaho, Shinsuke Takasao, Kazumi Kashiyama, Hiroyuki R. Takahashi","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.HE"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.28057"},{"id":"2611.21741","title":"Smuggling baryon asymmetry through strong washout","summary":"We propose a scheme in which a primordial net baryon number generated at or around the unification scale may survive subsequent strong washout phases associated with active sphalerons and interactions of right-handed neutrinos inherent to thermal leptogenesis. A simple realization of this, namely, a long-lived scalar population injecting the net $B-L$ it carries into the Standard Model matter sector late enough to be safe from a strong washout, is constructed and studied in the context of a simple Pati-Salam scalar sector with a heavy $SU(4)_C$ sextet scalar.","authors":"K{\\aa}re Fridell, Michal Malinsk\\'y, Javier Perez-Soler","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21741"},{"id":"2611.21747","title":"Perturbative QCD Analysis for the Electric Dipole Moment of $\\Xi$ and $\\Sigma$ Hyperons from CP-Violating Dipole Interactions","summary":"Hyperon electric dipole moments (EDMs) probe CP-violating interactions beyond the Standard Model. Motivated by the recent BESIII measurement [Ablikim et al., Science 393, 1027] and the perturbative QCD analysis of the $\\Lambda$ EDM form factor $d_\\Lambda$ [Chen et al., Phys. Rev. Lett. 136, 051902], we extend the perturbative QCD calculation of the EDM form factor to the $\\Xi$ and $\\Sigma$ hyperons. We derive factorization formulas from quark EDM and chromoelectric dipole moment contributions in terms of leading-twist light-cone distribution amplitudes (LCDAs). Using lattice-QCD results for the LCDAs and the strange-quark dipole moment ranges inferred from the BESIII $d_\\Lambda$ bound and neutron EDM constraints, we obtain the conditional estimates $-4.8 \\times 10^{-18} < d_{\\Xi^-} < 1.8\\times10^{-18}\\,e\\,\\mathrm{cm}$ and $-6.1\\times10^{-19} < d_{\\Sigma^+} < 2.4\\times10^{-19}\\,e\\,\\mathrm{cm}$ at the scale of ${J/\\psi}$ mass. Dedicated searches at BESIII and the future Super Tau-Charm Factory could further constrain these quark-level sources of CP violation.","authors":"Xi-Yu Mu, Kai-Bao Chen, Jian-Ping Ma, Xuan-Bo Tong","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21747"},{"id":"2611.21757","title":"Analytic next-to-leading order electroweak corrections to Higgs boson pair production in the forward limit","summary":"We calculate next-to-leading order electroweak corrections for Higgs boson pair production in the gluon fusion channel, in the forward limit. We perform an analytic calculation and obtain a final result for the partonic cross section that depends explicitly on all kinematic parameters and particle masses. We describe our method, which can be readily applied to similar processes. We observe rapid convergence within the validity range of the forward expansion. Furthermore, we demonstrate that the entire phase space can be covered when this approach is combined with the high-energy expansion.","authors":"Joshua Davies, Dominik Grau, Kay Sch\\\"onwald, Matthias Steinhauser, Daniel Stremmer","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21757"},{"id":"2611.21763","title":"A new angle on coherence-violating logarithms in color-singlet processes","summary":"We introduce a new formulation for the resummation of coherence-violating logarithms for the transverse energy flow in rapidity gaps in the Drell-Yan process. Our formalism allows us to resum coherence-violating logarithms in the gap cross section in the phenomenologically important regime where the gap size is comparable to the logarithm of the transverse energy. In the formal small gap limit, our result reduces to the known state-of-the-art resummation of coherence-violating logarithms for the Drell-Yan process. However, we identify sizable new corrections even for moderate gap widths that significantly modify the phenomenological picture for LHC applications. This work will form an important element of future extensions to a range of global and non-global observables.","authors":"Mrinal Dasgupta, Alexander Fraley, Pier Francesco Monni, Saad Nabeebaccus","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21763"},{"id":"2611.21813","title":"Probing $\\tau$ lepton dipole moments at multi-TeV muon colliders","summary":"We investigate the sensitivity of future multi-TeV muon colliders to the electromagnetic and weak dipole moments of the $\\tau$ lepton within the dimension-six Standard Model Effective Field Theory. We consider $\\tau^+\\tau^-$ production through $\\mu^+\\mu^-$ annihilation and vector-boson scattering, electroweak-boson-associated $\\tau$ production, and the radiative Higgs decay $H\\to\\tau^+\\tau^-\\gamma$. At high energies, the dipole-induced contributions quadratic in the Wilson coefficients become particularly important, with associated Higgs production, $\\mu^+\\mu^-\\to\\tau^+\\tau^-H$, providing the strongest sensitivity through energy-enhanced contact interactions. The remaining channels offer complementary probes of the underlying dipole operators. For a $10$~TeV muon collider with an integrated luminosity of $10\\,\\mathrm{ab}^{-1}$, we obtain combined sensitivities at the nominal $95\\%$ confidence level of $|\\Delta a_\\tau|<1.2\\times10^{-5}$, $|d_\\tau|<6.6\\times10^{-20}\\,e\\,\\mathrm{cm}$, $|\\Delta a_\\tau^Z|<1.8\\times10^{-5}$, and $|d_\\tau^Z|<1.0\\times10^{-19}\\,e\\,\\mathrm{cm}$. Compared with FCC-ee projections, the muon collider offers substantially stronger sensitivity to the electric dipole moments, while both facilities offer complementary probes of magnetic dipole moments. These results motivate further studies incorporating realistic detector effects and systematic uncertainties.","authors":"Dario Buttazzo, Gabriele Levati, Yang Ma, Fabio Maltoni, Paride Paradisi, ZeQiang Wang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21813"},{"id":"2611.22036","title":"Double parton scattering singularities in two-loop gluon-fusion amplitudes","summary":"Gluon fusion to two massive colour singlets can develop a double parton scattering (DPS) singularity as $p_T\\to0$: each gluon splits, and its daughters fuse pairwise. We determine two-loop logarithms using a common kernel and derive finite matching functions analytically for gluon and quark splittings. Normalised to one loop, the cubic coefficient changes sign between channels. Complete $gg\\to HH$, $gg\\to AA$ and $gg\\to ZZ$ amplitudes confirm the predicted colour, helicity and parity dependence and phase. The kernel gives leading DPS logarithms for other pairs with this DPS configuration.","authors":"Arunima Bhattacharya","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22036"},{"id":"2611.22237","title":"Flavor Entanglement as a CPT Violation Witness in Neutrino Oscillations","summary":"We present a comprehensive study linking the single-particle flavor entanglement of a \\(\\nu_{e} \\rightarrow \\nu_{\\mu}\\) oscillation to CPT violation using the concurrence. In a two-flavor vacuum with CP-conserving mixing, CPT invariance strictly demands \\(\\mathcal{C}_{\\nu}(L) = \\mathcal{C}_{\\bar{\\nu}}(L)\\), meaning any measured difference \\(\\Delta\\mathcal{C}_{\\mathrm{CPT}} \\equiv \\mathcal{C}_{\\nu} - \\mathcal{C}_{\\bar{\\nu}}\\) serves as a clean, background-free witness of CPT violation. We derive exact, first-order analytical relations connecting this entanglement asymmetry to the standard probability asymmetry, the baseline reflection sums, and the long-baseline decoherence floor. To rigorously test the experimental viability of this framework, we perform extensive numerical simulations. We demonstrate that the framework projects stringent sensitivity limits on Standard-Model Extension (SME) parameters at DUNE-like densities, isolate a coordinate-independent CPT invariant, and show that the signal remains robust across realistic, variable Earth matter density profiles. Crucially, we identify a distinct 24-hour sidereal modulation of the entanglement asymmetry, providing a definitive temporal \"smoking gun\" to disentangle CPT violation from matter effects. Finally, we show that at PeV astrophysical energies, the reflection-sum mismatch scales up to \\(10^{13}\\) meters, opening a novel frontier for CPT tests beyond terrestrial experiments.","authors":"Bipin Singh Koranga, Biswmkwr Boro, Vivek Kumar Nautiyal","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22237"},{"id":"2611.22421","title":"Top-Quark Physics after Evidence for Toponium","summary":"The top quark decays before it hadronizes, and toponium, the t\\bar t bound state, was long thought unobservable as a resonance. CMS and ATLAS each report an excess of top-quark pairs at threshold, above five standard deviations over fixed-order quantum chromodynamics (QCD), and CMS confirms it in a second decay channel. Its angular distributions match the color-singlet pseudoscalar quasi-bound state \\eta_t of nonrelativistic QCD, although an additional pseudoscalar boson is not excluded and higher-order fixed-order QCD accounts for part of the enhancement. We review the theory, ATLAS's nominal Green's-function signal model, and the measurements, whose cross sections range from about 3 to 13 pb. Part of the spread comes from the signal model and part persists between analyses using the same model. We discuss consequences for entanglement, the top mass, and heavy-pseudoscalar searches. We then review the top-quark program, from mass definitions to electroweak precision, flavor, the charge-conjugation and parity (CP) structure of the top Yukawa coupling, supersymmetry, multi-top production, and effective field theory. We close with prospects at the High-Luminosity LHC and future colliders, where an e^+e^- threshold scan can determine the top-quark mass in a short-distance scheme to a few tens of MeV.","authors":"Howard Baer, Vernon Barger","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22421"},{"id":"2611.22424","title":"Triangle singularity with different dynamical couplings","summary":"We carry out a systematic study of the impact of dynamical couplings of the vertices on the manifestations of the triangle singularity (TS) in the loop amplitude. Twelve combinations of external and internal spin-parity quantum numbers, covering the $PPS$, $VPP$, $VVP$, $VPA$, $VVV$, $VVA$, and $VVS$ effective vertices, are enumerated. Each loop amplitude is reduced via the Passarino--Veltman decomposition into scalar integrals $A_{0}$, $B_{0}$, and $C_{0}$, whose Lorentz-scalar coefficients encode the dynamical content of the vertices. Using the masses and kinematics of the $\\eta(1405)\\to\\pi^{+}\\pi^{-}\\pi^{0}$ transition via the $K^{*}\\bar{K}K$ loop with the intermediate $f_{0}(980)$ resonance in the $\\pi^+\\pi^-$ invariant spectrum as a common numerical benchmark for the twelve spin-parity assignments, we find that the same loop topology produces three distinct phenomena which manifest through the lineshapes, i.e. a narrow peak dominated by the $C_{0}$ contribution; a suppressed peak in which the $B_{0}$ and $C_{0}$ function contributions are comparable; and a cusp-like profile in which the coefficient of the leading Landau logarithm vanishes at the physical TS point. These results indicate that the visibility of a TS enhancement is not fixed by the Landau condition alone but reshaped by the detailed dynamics involved in the loop transitions.","authors":"Jun Wang, Xiao-Hai Liu, Qiang Zhao","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22424"},{"id":"2611.22615","title":"Lie-Poisson Formulation of Dissipative Spin Hydrodynamics","summary":"Covariance and the second law cannot fix how fluid flow couples to spin, since reversible couplings produce no entropy. We show that the spin commutation relations and the Poincar\\'e algebra fix these couplings. Reversibly, a flow exerts no torque on the spin: its magnitude per particle is conserved, its direction is Fermi--Walker transported, and spin--shear couplings reflect a deforming triad. The magnitude changes only through higher multipoles, as in spinor condensates, or dissipation, whose first-order spin--shear coupling reorients the spin away from the vorticity above a critical shear rate.","authors":"Zi-Hao Liu, Mei Huang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["cond-mat.soft","hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22615"},{"id":"2212.06862","title":"Global Fit of Modified Quark Couplings to EW Gauge Bosons and Vector-Like Quarks in Light of the Cabibbo Angle Anomaly","summary":"There are two tensions related to the Cabibbo angle of the CKM matrix. First, the determinations of $V_{us}$ from $K_{\\mu 2}$, $K_{\\ell3}$, and $\\tau$ decays disagree at the $3\\sigma$ level. Second, using the average of these results in combination with $\\beta$ decays (including super-allowed $\\beta$ decays and neutron decay), a deficit in first-row CKM unitarity with a significance of again about $3\\sigma$ is found. These discrepancies, known as the Cabibbo Angle anomaly, can in principle be solved by modifications of $W$ boson couplings to quarks. However, due to $SU(2)_L$ invariance, $Z$ couplings to quarks are also modified and flavour changing neutral currents can occur. In order to consistently assess the agreement of a new physics hypothesis with data, we perform a combined analysis for all dimension-six Standard Model Effective Field Theory operators that generate modified $W$ couplings to first and second generation quarks. We then study models with vector-like quarks, which are prime candidates for a corresponding UV completion as they can affect $W$-quark couplings at tree level, and we perform a global fit including flavour observables (in particular loop effects in $\\Delta F=2$ processes). We find that the best fit can be obtained for the $SU(2)_L$ doublet vector-like quark $Q$ as it can generate right-handed $W$-$u$-$d$ and $W$-$u$-$s$ couplings as preferred by data.","authors":"Andreas Crivellin, Matthew Kirk, Teppei Kitahara, Federico Mescia","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.06862"},{"id":"2404.04021","title":"Vacuum correlations of the stress-energy-momentum tensor with constituent quarks","summary":"The two point correlation function of the stress-energy-momentum tensor describes the propagation of a space-time ``micro-earthquake'' in the vacuum. In the framework of the path integral formulation of field theory in curved space-time, we discuss the Ward-Takashi identity for two-point Green's function of the stress-energy-momentum tensor for a general case of a non-conformal theory. The identity constrains the longitudinal part of the correlator, with the vacuum expectation value of the stress-energy-momentum, non-zero in a non-conformal theory. The obtained formula is demonstrated on the free massive Dirac fermion theory, treated at the one-loop level. This example befits a class of phenomenological chiral quarks models which have been used successfully in numerous applications in the soft non-perturbative regime of strong interactions. We stress the role of the constraints following from the Ward-Takahashi identity for the correlation functions in these models and point out the differences with the low-energy theorems (sum rules), not complying to the symmetry requirements. We also show the temporal representation of the two-point correlators, which is an object amenable to lattice QCD simulations.","authors":"Mirko Serino, Wojciech Broniowski, Enrique Ruiz Arriola","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-lat","hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04021"},{"id":"2503.02931","title":"Relativistic Navier-Stokes description of the quark-gluon plasma radial flow","summary":"Using relativistic Navier-Stokes in the well-posed Bemfica-Disconzi-Noronha-Kovtun (BDNK) formulation, with a QCD equation of state and transport coefficients, we perform simulations that describe the radial flow in central Pb-Pb collisions measured at the LHC, providing a proof of principle for its viability in heavy-ion phenomenology. We identify causal frames that retain the Landau-frame interpretation, and verify frame robustness. M\\\"uller-Israel-Stewart simulations with identical input show large second-order effects, exceeding the frame dependence of relativistic Navier-Stokes, as well as causality violations.","authors":"Yago Bea","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","hep-th","nucl-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.02931"},{"id":"2504.00069","title":"Transverse Energy--Energy Correlators at Small $x$ for Photon--Hadron Production","summary":"We study the transverse energy--energy correlator (TEEC) observable in photon--hadron and photon--jet production in p+p and p+A collisions at small $x$. We derive the relevant expressions in the high-energy limit of the scattering where the dipole picture is applicable and show how the dependence on the fragmentation function of the hadron cancels due to the momentum-sum rule. The nonperturbative scattering with the target nucleus is expressed in terms of the dipole amplitude, which also describes nonlinear gluon saturation effects. The TEEC observable is computed in the RHIC and LHC kinematics, and we show that it can be sensitive to the dipole amplitude, making it a potentially good observable for studying saturation effects.","authors":"Zhong-Bo Kang, Robert Kao, Meijian Li, Jani Penttala","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph","nucl-ex","nucl-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.00069"},{"id":"2504.06656","title":"Machine Learning in the Hunt for Charged Higgs Bosons at Gamma-Gamma Colliders in the Type III Two Higgs Doublet Model","summary":"We study charged Higgs boson pair production, $\\gamma\\gamma\\to H^{+}H^{-}$, in the Type-III Two-Higgs-Doublet Model (2HDM-III) for $100\\leq M_{H^{\\pm}}\\leq190~\\text{GeV}$ at a $\\gamma\\gamma$ collider with $\\sqrt{s}=3~\\text{TeV}$ and $\\mathcal{L}_{int}=3000~\\text{fb}^{-1}$. Two benchmark points, BP1 ($\\tan\\beta=10$, $\\cos(\\beta-\\alpha)=-0.2$) and BP2 ($\\tan\\beta=20$, $\\cos(\\beta-\\alpha)=-0.1$), are chosen in agreement with theoretical constraints, flavor-physics data and the signal strengths of the $125~\\text{GeV}$ Higgs boson. The total width of $H^{\\pm}$ is $10^{-4}$--$10^{-1}~\\text{GeV}$, i.e. a narrow resonance, and $\\mathrm{BR}(H^{+}\\to\\tau\\nu_{\\tau})$ falls from $4.6\\times10^{-3}$ (BP1) and $2.9\\times10^{-4}$ (BP2) at $100~\\text{GeV}$ to $1.8\\times10^{-4}$ and $1.2\\times10^{-5}$ at $190~\\text{GeV}$. The $\\tau\\nu_{\\tau}$ and $tb$ final states are analyzed with four TMVA classifiers (BDT, MLP, Likelihood and LikelihoodD) using $1.0\\times10^{5}$ generated events per process. For $171$ signal and $604$ background events, the MLP and BDT reach AUC values of $0.934$ and $0.933$ and maximum significances $S/\\sqrt{S+B}$ of $9.86$ and $9.82$, compared with $0.870$ and $8.54$ for LikelihoodD and $0.815$ and $7.55$ for Likelihood. The preselection cuts raise the significance by only $0.09$--$0.91\\%$, depending on the classifier. The quoted significances are statistical; systematic uncertainties are not included.","authors":"Abdul Quddus, Ijaz Ahmed, Jamil Muhammad, M. A. Arroyo-Ure","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.06656"},{"id":"2511.15779","title":"SURFing to the Fundamental Limit of Jet Tagging","summary":"Beyond the practical goal of improving search and measurement sensitivity through better jet tagging algorithms, there is a deeper question: what are their upper performance limits? Generative surrogate models with learned likelihood functions offer a new approach to this problem, provided the surrogate correctly captures the underlying data distribution. In this work, we introduce the SUrrogate ReFerence (SURF) method, a new approach to validating generative models. This framework enables exact Neyman-Pearson tests by training the target model on samples from another tractable surrogate, which is itself trained on real data. We argue that the EPiC-FM generative model is a valid surrogate reference for JetClass jets and apply SURF to show that modern jet taggers may already be operating close to the true statistical limit. By contrast, we find that autoregressive GPT models unphysically exaggerate top vs. QCD separation power encoded in the surrogate reference, implying that they are giving a misleading picture of the fundamental limit.","authors":"Ian Pang, Darius A. Faroughy, David Shih, Ranit Das, Gregor Kasieczka","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["cs.LG","hep-ex","hep-ph","physics.data-an"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.15779"},{"id":"2601.02887","title":"Study of $CP$ violation in $\\Lambda_b^0\\rightarrow N^*M$ decays with the final-state rescattering mechanism","summary":"In this work, we investigate the charmless nonleptonic two-body $\\Lambda_b$ decays within the framework of final-state rescattering mechanism. Using this framework, we evaluate the relevant hadronic triangle diagrams by performing the loop integrations, retaining both their absorptive and dispersive parts. Based on the established formalism, we analyze the $\\Lambda_b \\to N^*(1535,1520)M$ decay processes with $M =K_S, K^*_0(700)$, $f_0(500,980), \\rho(770), \\bar{K}^{*0}$, $\\phi$, and predict various physical observables, such as their branching ratios, direct and partial-wave $CP$ asymmetries, as well as decay asymmetry parameters. These two-body decay processes are expected to contribute primarily to the subsequent four-body decay channels, such as $\\Lambda_b^0 \\to p\\,\\pi^-\\,\\pi^+\\,\\pi^-$, whose $CP$ asymmetry measurements will be accessible at the LHCb experiment.","authors":"Hui-Qiang Shang, Tian-Liang Feng, Jing Gao, Qin Qin, Fu-Sheng Yu","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.02887"},{"id":"2602.06895","title":"MadSpace -- Event Generation for the Era of GPUs and ML","summary":"MadSpace is a new modular phase-space and event-generation library written in C++ with native GPU support via CUDA and HIP. It provides a unified compute-graph-based framework for phase-space construction, adaptive and neural importance sampling, and event unweighting. It includes a wide range of mappings, from the standard MadGraph multi-channel phase space to optimized normalizing flows with analytic inverse transformations. All components operate on batches of events and support end-to-end on-device workflows. A high-level Python interface enables seamless integration with machine-learning libraries such as PyTorch.","authors":"Theo Heimel, Olivier Mattelaer, Ramon Winterhalder","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06895"},{"id":"2602.15100","title":"Dispersive analysis of the $\\boldsymbol{\\phi \\to \\gamma \\pi^0 \\pi^0}$ process","summary":"We present an analysis of the radiative decay $\\phi \\to \\gamma \\pi^0 \\pi^0$ in a dispersive framework, where the two-pion subsystem undergoes strong final-state interactions that cover the $f_0(500)$ and $f_0(980)$ regions. We employ a coupled-channel Muskhelishvili-Omn\\`es framework that allows for a consistent treatment of two scalar resonances and crossed-channel singularities induced by the Born and vector-meson exchanges. We explicitly verify the equivalence between the modified and standard Muskhelishvili-Omn\\`es representations for vector-meson pole contributions when the isoscalar Omn\\`es matrix is chosen asymptotically bounded, and we adopt the standard representation in decay kinematics. This yields, for the first time, a parameter-free dispersive prediction for the kaon Born rescattering, which provides a dominant contribution. To obtain a good fit to the KLOE and SND data, we employ a once-subtracted coupled-channel dispersion relation with heavier left-hand cut contributions and two unknown subtraction constants. The results demonstrate the consistency among the data for $\\pi\\pi$ scattering, $\\gamma\\gamma$ fusion, and $\\phi $ radiative decay, thereby validating the underlying dispersive formalism and the input used for the hadronic Omn\\`es matrix and left-hand cuts.","authors":"Bai-Long Hoid, Igor Danilkin, Marc Vanderhaeghen","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph","nucl-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.15100"},{"id":"2603.02300","title":"One-point energy correlator for deep inelastic scattering at small $x$","summary":"We derive the expressions for the one-point energy correlator (OPEC) in deep inelastic scattering in the high-energy (small-$x$) limit within the Color Glass Condensate (CGC) framework. The OPEC is computed as a function of the angle between the energy flow and the target proton or nucleus, enabling a systematic exploration of different momentum scales in the scattering process. Owing to the momentum sum rule, the dependence on fragmentation functions cancels, leaving the dipole amplitude as the only nonperturbative input. As a result, the OPEC provides a sensitive and promising probe of gluon saturation dynamics at small $x$. We present numerical results for representative kinematic configurations relevant to the future Electron--Ion Collider, demonstrating sizable nuclear suppression effects within the CGC framework and highlighting the consistency of this observable with predictions from gluon saturation.","authors":"Zhong-Bo Kang, Robert Kao, Meijian Li, Jani Penttala","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph","nucl-ex","nucl-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.02300"},{"id":"2604.26537","title":"Particle seismology: mechanical and gravitational properties from parton-hadron duality","summary":"The internal structure of hadrons is characterized by form factors which correspond to matrix elements of currents. Among those, the stress-energy-momentum tensor is a universally conserved quantity providing the gravitational form factors, from which mechanical properties may be derived via the response to the space-time fluctuations. They have received much attention because of their role as moments of the Generalized Parton Distributions, where the stress-energy-momentum tensor couples to two photons, and more recently, due to the explicit lattice QCD determination for the pion and nucleon. In these lectures we attempt a pedagogical review of the topic from a purely hadronic point of view, based on the notion of dispersion relations, meson dominance, and parton-hadron duality. We show that despite the overwhelming simplicity of the approach, a rather successful description of the lattice QCD data is achieved.","authors":"Enrique Ruiz Arriola, Wojciech Broniowski","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-lat","hep-ph","nucl-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.26537"},{"id":"2605.09000","title":"Optimizing Yukawa couplings to suppress Dimension-five Proton Decay in $SU(5)$ GUT","summary":"The minimal supersymmetric $SU(5)$ grand unified theory (GUT) provides a highly compelling framework for physics beyond the Standard Model (SM). However, it suffers from a severe phenomenological challenge: rapid proton decay mediated by colored-Higgsino exchange via dimension-five operators. Resolving this issue often requires adjustments to the Yukawa couplings and the potential sectors, generating a vast and complex parameter space where traditional brute-force numerical scans are rendered computationally intractable due to the curse of dimensionality. In this paper, we overcome this limitation by applying machine learning optimization techniques. We investigate a supersymmetric $SU(5)$ model extended with $\\mathbf{45}$ and $\\overline{\\mathbf{45}}$ Higgs representations, defining a loss function based on the partial decay width of $p \\to K^+ \\bar{\\nu}$. Utilizing the Adam optimizer, we systematically explore the 33-dimensional parameter space to identify regions that suppress proton decay. Furthermore, we vary $\\tan \\beta$ to thoroughly investigate whether the optimized proton lifetime can consistently exceed the stringent experimental lower bound of $5.9 \\times 10^{33}$ years established by the Super-Kamiokande collaboration.","authors":"Naoyuki Haba, Junpei Ikemoto, Yasuhiro Shimizu, Toshifumi Yamada","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.09000"},{"id":"2606.01966","title":"Reinterpreting the ATLAS HHH$\\to 6b$ Search with CheckMATE and Rivet: Validation, TRSM Benchmarks, and HL-LHC Prospects","summary":"We present an implementation in CheckMATE and Rivet of the ATLAS collaboration search for triple Higgs boson production in a six $b$-jets final state. The search relies on event selection using a deep neural network and a statistical model based on the HS3 format. Owing to a rich validation material provided by ATLAS, we perform a thorough validation of neural network input features and exclusion limits in the Standard Model and its extension with two additional singlet scalar fields (TRSM). Finally, we discuss expected performance of the search at the High Luminosity LHC in several scenarios of systematic uncertainty. We present projected exclusion limits for a set of TRSM benchmark models beyond those considered by ATLAS.","authors":"Tomasz Procter, Krzysztof Rolbiecki, Andrzej Si\\'odmok","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.01966"},{"id":"2606.06105","title":"Bulk viscosity of a binary mixture: the role of the intra-species interaction","summary":"The bulk viscosity $\\zeta$ is a transport coefficient which is of central importance for various areas of modern physics. In particular, its determination for a fluid with two components is challenging, since it involves a complex interplay of multiple microscopic processes that operate on different time scales. Within the Chapman-Enskog framework, based on a series expansion of the Boltzmann distribution function, many previous works have derived the 1$^{\\text{st}}$ order result for the $\\zeta$ of a mixture. However, such a result fails to reproduce relevant physical features of the system, especially when the masses of the two components are similar. In this work we improve the 1$^{\\text{st}}$ order Chapman-Enskog result by deriving the $\\zeta$ at the 2$^{\\text{nd}}$ order in the expansion. We show that this improved formula encodes many physical properties that the 1$^{\\text{st}}$ order result misses: under specific conditions, the 2$^{\\text{nd}}$ order result can be qualitatively and quantitatively very different from the 1$^{\\text{st}}$ order one. Moreover, this result is compared against the $\\zeta$ evaluated within the Green-Kubo formalism, by means of a numerical solution of the Relativistic Boltzmann equation. The agreement with respect to this benchmark is significantly improved when moving from the 1$^{\\text{st}}$ to the 2$^{\\text{nd}}$ order CE result.","authors":"Gabriele Parisi, Vincenzo Nugara, Shams Ul Arfeen, Salvatore Plumari, Vincenzo Greco","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.06105"},{"id":"2606.07744","title":"Open quantum system approach to the transverse momentum broadening of a colour dipole","summary":"Using the open quantum systems formalism, we study the propagation of a quark-antiquark pair propagating through a dense QCD plasma of size $L$ and transverse momentum broadening transport coefficient $\\hat q$, and we derive the Lindblad evolution equation for the density matrix of the system. We focus on the boosted regime where the opening angle $\\theta_{q\\bar q}$ of this effective colour dipole satisfies $\\theta_{q\\bar q}\\ll 1$. In the correlation limit where the quark-antiquark relative transverse momentum $p_\\perp$ is much larger than the imbalance $q_\\perp$ as well as the medium typical transverse momentum scale $Q_s=\\sqrt{\\hat q L}$, we demonstrate that the resulting Wigner distribution displays quasi factorisation between a hard factor describing the hard splitting producing the $q\\bar q$ pair and the transverse momentum imbalance $q_\\perp$-distribution encoding the broadening induced by the medium. The factorisation is violated by a \"colour decoherence\" factor that controls the $\\theta_{q\\bar q}$ dependence of the $q_\\perp$-distribution through the ratio $\\theta_{q\\bar q}/\\theta_c$, with $\\theta_c \\sim (\\hat q L^3)^{-1/2}$. The open quantum systems approach enables us to clarify the role of this critical angle $\\theta_c$ and its associated critical time $t_c$ in the genuine quantum decoherence of the density matrix in colour and kinematic space: in particular, $t_c$ controls both the suppression of the off-diagonal elements of the density matrix in colour space and the transition between singlet and octet states. We find, however, that colour decoherence sets in earlier than the full decoherence of the density matrix, thereby marking the onset of classical behaviour in the system. Finally, we investigate the corrections beyond the quasi-factorised picture due to the quantum diffusion term in $p_\\perp$ of the Lindblad equation and we find that these corrections are mild.","authors":"Fran\\c{c}ois Arleo, Pietro Benzoni, Paul Caucal, Pol Bernard Gossiaux","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","nucl-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.07744"},{"id":"2606.09768","title":"Dispersive analysis of the $\\boldsymbol{J/\\psi \\to \\gamma \\pi^0 \\pi^0}$ process","summary":"We present a dispersive amplitude analysis of the low-energy $\\pi^0\\pi^0$ system in the radiative decay $J/\\psi\\to\\gamma\\pi^0\\pi^0$, using the mass-independent BESIII $0^{++}$ and $2^{++}$ intensities, the total spectrum, and the measured $0^{++}-2^{++}$ $E1$ phase difference. The isoscalar $S$-wave is described by a coupled-channel $\\pi\\pi/K\\bar K$ Muskhelishvili-Omn\\`es representation, which implements the strong final-state interactions associated with the $f_0(500)$ and $f_0(980)$. The $D$-wave is treated with a single-channel $\\pi\\pi$ Muskhelishvili-Omn\\`es representation, where we identify all kinematic constraints of helicity amplitudes before transforming them to the experimental $E1$, $M2$, and $E3$ multipoles. Smooth short-distance production of a pseudoscalar-meson pair is encoded in subtraction polynomials, while left-hand-cut effects are estimated and found to be numerically subleading. We identify the negative solution of the BESIII $0^{++}-2^{++}$ $E1$ phase ambiguity, after using the modulo-$\\pi$ freedom of production amplitudes, as the phase solution compatible with unitarity constraints, showing that the measured phase information can be accommodated with the Omn\\`es phase motion without requiring large additional phases. By normalizing the BESIII intensities with the extracted branching fraction, we fix the absolute scale of the fitted amplitudes, making them suitable as input for future dispersive studies of two-pion contributions to gravitational form factors.","authors":"Bai-Long Hoid, Igor Danilkin, Anna Testa, Marc Vanderhaeghen","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-lat","hep-ph","nucl-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.09768"},{"id":"2607.03343","title":"First look at the evaluation of two-loop Feynman integrals for radiative return processes","summary":"Precision studies of radiative return processes at low-energy electron--positron colliders require next-to-next-to-leading order QED predictions retaining full dependence on the electron mass. We present the calculation of planar two-loop four-point Feynman integrals relevant for initial-state radiation contributions to these processes. The calculation presents considerable analytical complexity, due to the presence of a nested square root and of integrals associated with elliptic geometries. We construct differential equations for the Feynman integrals which are polynomial in the dimensional regulator, and are suitable for numerical integration. We demonstrate stable numerical evaluations throughout the physical region relevant for low-energy experiments, despite the presence of large hierarchies of scales. Our results provide essential building blocks for NNLO predictions for radiative return processes.","authors":"Mattia Pozzoli, William J. Torres Bobadilla","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.03343"},{"id":"2607.04132","title":"Left-right symmetry breaking in $E_6^L\\times E_6^R$ occurs only in spacetime -- with possible implications for strong $CP$","summary":"Two-sided octonionic $E_6^L\\times E_6^R$ unification trinifies each factor and so carries two colour groups: the visible $SU(3)_c$ and a second $SU(3)_{c'}$ which, gauged like the first, would colour the charged lepton and must be suppressed by hand. We argue no such device is needed: the left-right symmetry acts in spacetime, not internally -- in the gravi-weak reading $SU(2)_R$ is the gravitational frame group and the exchange is ordinary parity on the Dirac field; a spacetime operation cannot double the internal colour. Colour is single and vector-like, $SU(3)_{c'}=SU(3)_c$, and the electron is a colour singlet. The right sector contributes one colour-blind datum, $\\sqrt m$ (values: the exceptional-Jordan trace split, an input; $N_R$ fixes only the colour representation). An anomaly no-go shows that no gauged $U(1)$ on the visible fermions can carry the $\\sqrt m$ pattern: the dark-electromagnetic $U(1)_{\\rm dem}$ carries the parity mirror of electric charge on the dark sector, the visible fermions are dem-neutral, and no visible fifth force is predicted. Hypercharge is recovered as the consistency relation $Y=Q-T^3_L$, $Q=N/3$. As an application, parity restricts the QCD angle to $\\{0,\\pi\\}$; $\\theta_{\\rm QCD}=0$ is selected, not derived, by matching to the sign content of the quark mass determinant, whose phase vanishes because the flavour rotors have real determinant. Hence $\\bar\\theta=0$ at tree level, conditional on the gravi-weak identification, with a nonzero CKM phase. Loop stability is open: the two-loop $\\bar\\theta$ of the minimal universal-seesaw parity model arises from scalar exchange, so the absence of a gauged $W_R$ here does not remove its mechanism, which the Higgs-bridge sector shares. We tag throughout what is derived, what is input, and what is open.","authors":"Tejinder P. Singh","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.04132"},{"id":"2607.13743","title":"Polarized observables in $\\tau \\to VV \\nu_{\\tau}$ decays","summary":"We study polarization observables in $\\tau^- \\to V_1^- V_2^0 \\nu_\\tau$ decays for $(V_1^-, V_2^0)=(\\rho^-, \\rho^0), (\\rho^-, \\omega), (K^{*-}, \\rho^0), (K^{*-}, \\omega), (\\rho^-, \\bar{K}^{*0})$ using the helicity formalism. While the obtained branching fraction for $\\tau^-\\to \\rho^-\\rho^0\\nu_\\tau$ is consistent with that of angular momentum algebra method, the results for the other four channels are comparable with (smaller than) those from the effective chiral model (the aforementioned method). Our results for the longitudinal polarization fractions $f_L$ indicate that nonnegligible corrections to the endpoint limit ($f_L = 1/3$), a consequence of Lorentz symmetry, are present at the scale of tau mass. Future measurements of $f_L$ at Belle II for $\\tau^- \\to V_1^- V_2^0 \\nu_\\tau$ decays will provide crucial information on polarization patterns observed in nonleptonic charm decays.","authors":"Vindhyawasini Prasad, Hiroyuki Umeeda","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.13743"},{"id":"2608.05304","title":"GOOFy-compatible 3HDMs and beyond","summary":"Beyond conventional Higgs-family and general CP transformations, one may also consider a broader class of non-standard \"GOOFy\" transformations, in which the scalar fields and their conjugates are assigned related but inequivalent transformations. Although these transformations are not symmetries of a full Lagrangian in the conventional sense, some nevertheless stabilise the scalar potential. Their impact on the quadratic sector has not yet been systematically classified. We develop a sign-orbit technique to determine the bilinear structures compatible with these transformations. Applied to three-Higgs-doublet models, and formulated so as to extend to larger multi-Higgs sectors, the method shows that a non-vanishing GOOFy-compatible quadratic sector exists only when two independent conditions are met: the underlying group admits a non-trivial sign character, and that character is realised within the tensor product of the scalar representation and its conjugate. As a key consequence, we demonstrate that the renormalisation-group stability of the $r_0 \\to -r_0$ manifold in isolation is not a generic feature of multi-Higgs potentials, but rather a direct consequence of special algebraic properties of $SU(2)$. These results provide a classification of the admissible GOOFy quadratic structures and their invariant bilinear subspaces, highlighting the algebraic obstructions to their renormalisation-group stability.","authors":"I. de Medeiros Varzielas, A. Kun\\v{c}inas","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.05304"},{"id":"2608.16266","title":"Amplified Isospin Breaking from Coupled-Channel Dynamics Near Threshold","summary":"We identify the dynamical origin of amplified isospin breaking in near-threshold states. Using a pole-residue-based measure defined directly from the pole couplings, rather than from decay observables that are also affected by different final-state kinematics, we show that threshold proximity of the pole alone is insufficient: large isospin breaking requires a sizable interaction in the companion isospin channel. In particular, the isospin-violating pole-coupling ratio vanishes when the companion-channel interaction is switched off, even at finite threshold splitting. Applied to the $X(3872)$, the observed large isospin breaking points to a nearby partner pole, $W_{c1}$, as predicted in previous studies. As a concrete realization of the previously discussed leading/shadow-pole interchange, we find that the $W_{c1}$ pole, which predominantly affects the line shapes near the charged threshold in the physical case, does not evolve into the pure $I=1$ eigenstate in the isospin limit; instead, that eigenstate is continuously connected to a more distant shadow pole.","authors":"Xiang-Kun Dong, Feng-Kun Guo, Christoph Hanhart, Teng Ji, Ulf-G. Mei{\\ss}ner","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-lat","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.16266"},{"id":"2609.00912","title":"Radiative pion-pair production in disperon QED","summary":"Based on the previously developed disperon QED method for incorporating data input into loop processes in Monte Carlo codes, we calculate $ee\\to\\pi\\pi\\gamma$ at NLO and include the pion vector form factor within the form-factor scalar QED approximation wherever it is physically justified. In particular, this includes insertions of the two-pion two-photon Compton tensor into loop diagrams. We also consider a subset of contributions beyond, involving the pion Compton tensor with three photons. For these terms the form-factor scalar QED approach is less well justified, but we gain insight into the associated theory uncertainty for selected experimental scenarios. All calculations are implemented and made available within the Monte Carlo framework McMule.","authors":"Yizhou Fang, Sophie Kollatzsch, Adrian Signer, Max Zoller","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.00912"},{"id":"2609.20953","title":"Prospects for testing Lorentz symmetry with highly charged ions","summary":"We investigate the prospects for testing Lorentz symmetry using highly charged ions. We derive the Lorentz-violating energy shifts relevant to Zeeman and Zeeman-hyperfine transitions within the ground state of H-like ions and to Zeeman and Zeeman-fine transitions within the ground state of B-like ions. The resulting signals include sidereal variations through the first harmonic of the sidereal frequency for H-like ions and through the third harmonic for B-like ions. For certain SME coefficients whose contributions scale with the fourth power of the electron momentum, the large electron momentum in H-like ions can provide sensitivity competitive with the best existing bounds at the precision achievable in H-like-ion spectroscopy. We also find that B-like-ion spectroscopy can provide competitive sensitivity to certain SME coefficients.","authors":"Arnaldo J. Vargas","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.20953"},{"id":"2610.11937","title":"Reassessing the $X(2370)$ in $J/\\psi\\to\\gamma K_S^0K_S^0\\pi^0$ beyond the one-dimensional mass projections","summary":"The BESIII Collaboration recently extracted the properties of the $X(2370)$ from a fit to the one-dimensional $K_S^0K_S^0\\pi^0$ invariant-mass spectrum in $J/\\psi\\to\\gamma K_S^0K_S^0\\pi^0$. The intermediate two-body structures visible in the mass correlations, including the $K_0^*(1430)$ bands, require a coherent treatment of cross-channel interference when interpreting the enhancement near 2.3 GeV. We reassess the need for an additional $X(2370)$ contribution by fitting the published data using an effective coupled-channel framework with an approximate detection efficiency depending on the three-body invariant mass. The three-body parent amplitude is parameterized using a $K$-matrix that describes scattering among several quasi-two-body channels. The seven- and eight-bare-state models give similar mass distributions. Adding the eighth bare state lowers the Poisson deviance by 1.22% and produces an additional pole at $E=(2419-i152)$ MeV, substantially broader than the $X(2370)$ mass-width reference. This pole is model-dependent, and its identification with the $X(2370)$ is not clear. The present analysis does not establish the need for an additional $X(2370)$ contribution in $J/\\psi\\to\\gamma K_S^0K_S^0\\pi^0$, thereby weakening the flavor-singlet argument based on the suppression of $K^*\\bar K$ relative to the total $K\\bar K\\pi$ contribution. A more comprehensive experimental amplitude analysis, incorporating coherent interference, mass and angular correlations, and detector effects across related decay channels, is needed to establish the role and decay properties of the $X(2370)$.","authors":"Xiang-Kun Dong, Teng Ji, Meike K\\\"u{\\ss}ner, Ulf-G. Mei\\ss ner","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-lat","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.11937"},{"id":"2610.21307","title":"Nonleptonic decays of the double-charmed baryon $\\Xi_{cc}^{+}$ with dynamically determined baryon wave functions","summary":"Motivated by the recent observation of the double-charmed baryon $\\Xi_{cc}^{+}$ by the LHCb Collaboration, we systematically investigate the two-body nonleptonic decays of the $\\Xi_{cc}^{+}$ into a single-charmed baryon and a light pseudoscalar meson. In our calculation, both factorizable and nonfactorizable decay amplitudes are evaluated within the framework of nonrelativistic quark model combined with the baryon wave functions dynamically determined by numerically solving the three-body Schr\\\"{o}dinger equation with a nonrelativistic potential model. In particular, the nonfactorizable $W$-exchange amplitudes are estimated under pole model by considering $J^{P}=1/2^{\\pm}$ low-lying intermediate charmed-baryon poles, and our results show that these contributions are comparable to or even dominate the factorizable amplitudes in several decay channels. Moreover, assuming a lifetime of $\\tau_{\\Xi_{cc}^{+}}=47~\\text{fs}$, we predict relatively large branching fractions for several CF decays, including $\\mathcal{B}(\\Xi_{cc}^{+}\\to\\Xi_{c}^{\\prime0}\\pi^{+})=8.90\\%$, $\\mathcal{B}(\\Xi_{cc}^{+}\\to\\Omega_{c}^{0}K^{+})=7.09\\%$, $\\mathcal{B}(\\Xi_{cc}^{+}\\to\\Sigma_{c}^{+}\\bar{K}^{0})=6.92\\%$, $\\mathcal{B}(\\Xi_{cc}^{+}\\to\\Xi_{c}^{+}\\pi^{0})=5.28\\%$, and $\\mathcal{B}(\\Xi_{cc}^{+}\\to\\Sigma_{c}^{++}K^{-})=5.17\\%$. These channels are promising candidates for future experimental searches at the LHCb.","authors":"Yu-Shuai Li, Hong-Tao An","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21307"},{"id":"2611.04110","title":"Holographic spin alignment of vector mesons in a spinning black-hole background","summary":"Holographic predictions for vector-meson spin alignment may depend on the background geometry and on the meson model. We examine the opposite polarization preferences observed for charmonium and light vector mesons by placing \\(J/\\psi\\) and \\(\\phi\\) in one soft-wall model on the planar limit of a five-dimensional equal-spin Myers--Perry AdS black hole. Spectral functions and spin alignment are computed within the same current-correlator framework; the nonrotating results of Sheng et al. [Phys. Rev. D 110, 056047 (2024)] are recovered at \\(a=0\\). At \\(T=0.15~\\mathrm{GeV}\\), \\(J/\\psi\\) retains a clear resonance peak, whereas \\(\\phi\\) shows no narrow peak and instead behaves as a broad distribution consistent with unstable \\(s\\bar{s}\\) pairs. For \\(J/\\psi\\) along \\(z\\), rotation enhances the positive deviation of \\(\\rho_{00}^{h}\\) from \\(1/3\\) at low and intermediate momenta but suppresses it at high momenta; along \\(x\\), the effect is small. For \\(\\phi\\), rotation enhances the negative deviation, more along \\(z\\) than along \\(x\\). The opposite helicity-frame signs and the opposing azimuthal and rapidity trends of the global alignment provide a reference for interpreting data: trends inconsistent with these would challenge the simplest single homogeneous rotating-medium interpretation. We distinguish model-dependent numerical details from qualitative features stable within this model class.","authors":"Fei Wang, Zi-qiang Zhang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04110"},{"id":"2611.05635","title":"Proton generalized parton distributions from electromagnetic form factors: A theoretically constrained phenomenological reconstruction","summary":"We present a theoretically constrained phenomenological reconstruction of the unpolarized, chiral-even, leading-twist proton generalized parton distributions $H^q$ and $E^q$. At zero skewness, valence-quark distributions are combined with flavor-dependent exponential profiles calibrated simultaneously against phenomenological representations of the proton and neutron electromagnetic form factors. The construction imposes exact valence and anomalous-magnetic-moment normalizations, the low-scale condition $B^{u+d}(0)=0$, the required endpoint behavior, and the complete Gaussian impact-parameter positivity bound. The resulting parametrization provides a reasonable description of the electromagnetic form factors and their slopes while revealing the tension between strict GPD consistency and a precision reproduction of all low-$Q^2$ observables within the adopted ansatz. We extend the distributions to nonzero skewness using normalized double distributions and introduce an independent $D$-term to complete the relevant Mellin polynomials. We examine electromagnetic and energy--momentum-tensor form factors, impact-parameter distributions, positivity, polynomiality, and illustrative mechanical properties. The analysis distinguishes information constrained by electromagnetic data from additional assumptions governing skewness and the mechanical sector, thereby providing a transparent elastic baseline for future studies of nucleon-to-resonance transition GPDs.","authors":"A. Capelo-Astudillo, T. Aguilar, M. Conde-Correa, A. Duenas-Vidal, P. G. Ortega, J. Segovia","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-lat","hep-ph","nucl-ex","nucl-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05635"},{"id":"2611.07518","title":"Power corrections to heavy-quark spin correlations in small-$x$ deep-inelastic scattering","summary":"Heavy-quark spin correlations in small-$x$ deep-inelastic scattering receive corrections beyond the back-to-back limit. For a rotationally symmetric target at leading power, the target dependence cancels from the normalized spin density matrix after full azimuthal averaging, for fixed kinematics and spin axes related by transverse light-front boosts. We calculate the first nonvanishing correction at tree level for finite quark mass and finite $N_c$ in a homogeneous Gaussian target. The expansion through fourth order in the dipole sizes includes logarithms, contact terms and all interference contributions at that order. Comparison with the improved transverse-momentum-dependent approximation separates kinematic corrections from terms with additional insertions of the target current. Numerical integration of the unexpanded Gaussian kernel tests the approximation at 28 parameter points. Spin correlations differ between the two targets in all 12 kinematic comparisons; in one comparison both spin states remain separable. At fixed photon virtuality, the leading large-$P$ behavior of the fourth-order correction to the normalized transverse spin state is generically proportional to $m\\ln P/P^3$. We also compare two fixed Gaussian targets using unpolarized momentum distributions and joint spin measurements. For a purely transverse photon, three narrow momentum bins and an ideal measurement of both spins along the common quark-momentum axis defined by the light-front boost, spin information adds 3.5-3.7 percent to the expected log likelihood ratio after the overall normalization is fitted. This increase depends on the spin analyzing power and bin selection. The results quantify a model-dependent effect under idealized measurement conditions.","authors":"Meisen Gao","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","nucl-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07518"},{"id":"2611.14593","title":"Strongly supercooled first-order phase transitions: monopole abundance, entropy production and homogeneity consistency","summary":"We investigate whether monopole production in the early universe from a first-order phase transition in a unified gauge theory can be significantly suppressed if the phase transition is subject to strong supercooling but still completes successfully. This is facilitated by adopting a Gaussian nucleation model and assuming the false vacuum energy released is transferred to sectors which behave effectively as radiation in terms of cosmological evolution. We distinguish three criteria of phase-transition completion: the comoving measure completion (CMC) characterized by the eventual vanishing of the false-vacuum fraction (FVF), the physical volume completion (PVC) characterized by the eventual vanishing of the physical volume of false vacuum, and the asymptotic thermodynamic completion (ATC), which refers to the finiteness of total entropy production. Assuming an idealized Friedmann-Lema\\^itre-Robertson-Walker (FLRW) cosmology, it is shown that the three criteria share exactly the same completion boundary, away from which they are completely equivalent. It is further shown that monopole abundance can be arbitrarily suppressed for parameter points approaching the completion boundary from the successful side. Nevertheless, for parameter points too close to the completion boundary, justification of the self-consistency of a homogeneous FLRW cosmology is difficult. We propose the use of the Hubble-scale FVF root-mean-square fluctuation to diagnose the homogeneity consistency issue, and find that arbitrarily strong suppression of monopole abundance relative to a reasonable Kibble estimate is not justifiable in terms of homogeneity consistency. These findings also have implications for gravitational wave production in strongly supercooled phase transitions.","authors":"Chen Zhang, Yue Niu, Xin Zhang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14593"},{"id":"2611.16807","title":"Solar Neutrinos at the ESSnuSB experiment","summary":"We investigate the sensitivity of the ESSnuSB large underground water-Cherenkov (WC) detector to two key solar neutrino signatures: the MSW spectral upturn in the $^8$B electron neutrino survival probability $P_{ee}(E_\\nu)$, and the first direct observation of the rare hep neutrino flux. The analysis incorporates detector systematic uncertainties (energy resolution and flux normalization) and models environmental backgrounds from cosmic-ray muon spallation and $^{222}$Rn contamination. We adopt a shape-driven analysis strategy with a floating overall normalization, so that the discovery power relies on the spectral distortion rather than on the absolute event rate. In a realistic scenario with 90% spallation rejection efficiency, informed by the Super-Kamiokande experience and recent JUNO analyses, residual spallation backgrounds prevent either signal from reaching $5\\sigma$ significance within 20 years of exposure. For the hep flux, we present a detailed sensitivity study to both its discovery and its precision, and quantify how the required spallation veto efficiency and recoil energy threshold jointly determine the achievable significance for both signals. At the adopted thresholds, discovery-level sensitivity for both signals requires approximately 99% spallation rejection over a 20-year exposure.","authors":" ESSnuSB Collaboration, J. Aguilar, D. Bar\\v{c}ot, E. Baussan, A. K. Bhattacharyya, M. Blennow, M. Bogomilov, B. Bolling, E. Bouquerel, F. Bramati, A. Branca, G. Brunetti, I. Bustinduy, C. J. Carlile, J. Cederkall, T. W. Choi, S. Choubey, P. Christiansen, I. Christodoulou, E. Cristaldo Morales, P. Cupia{\\l}, D. D'Ago, H. Danared, J. P. A. M. de Andr\\'e, E. A. D\\'iaz, M. Dracos, I. Efthymiopoulos, T. Ekel\\\"of, M. Eshraqi, G. Fanourakis, A. Farricker, E. Fasoula, T. Fukuda, S. Gago, N. Gazis, Th. Geralis, M. Ghosh, A. Giarnetti, O. Gilanliogullari, G. Gokbulut, C. Hagner, L. Hali\\'c, S. G. Hern\\'andez, J. Hiegel, M. Hooft, K. E. Iversen, N. Jachowicz, M. Jakkapu, M. Jensen, I. Karakoulias, E. Kasimi, A. Kayis Topaksu, B. Kli\\v{c}ek, K. Kordas, B. Kova\\v{c}, A. Leisos, A. Longhin, M. L\\'opez, C. Maiano, S. Marangoni, J. G. Marcos, C. Marrelli, D. Meloni, M. Mezzetto, N. Milas, J. L. Mu\\~noz, K. Niewczas, M. Oglakci, T. Ohlsson, M. Olveg{\\aa}rd, A. Opanasenko, M. Pari, D. Patrzalek, G. Petkov, Ch. Petridou, P. Poussot, A. Psallidas, F. Pupilli, M. L. Reguera, D. Saiang, E. Salehi, D. Sampsonidis, A. Scanu, C. Schwab, F. Sordo, G. Stavropoulos, M. Stip\\v{c}evi\\'c, R. Tarkeshian, F. Terranova, T. Tolba, M. Topp-Mugglestone, E. Trachanas, R. Tsenov, A. Tsirigotis, S. E. Tzamarias, M. Vanderpoorten, G. Vankova-Kirilova, N. Vassilopoulos, S. Vihonen, J. Wurtz, V. Zeter, O. Zormpa","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16807"},{"id":"2408.15006","title":"Long- and short-range longitudinal flow decorrelations in the quark-gluon plasma","summary":"The longitudinal structure of the quark-gluon plasma (QGP) remains poorly constrained. Using a transport-model calculation of two-particle elliptic-flow correlations $V_{2\\Delta}(\\Delta\\eta)$ in relative pseudorapidity $\\Delta\\eta$, we find two distinct initial-state components of flow decorrelation: a long-range component driven by the global geometry of participating nucleons and a short-range component reflecting local energy-deposition fluctuations. This short-range component extends to $|\\Delta\\eta|\\sim 4$ and can be misidentified as final-state nonflow. For isobar-like nuclei with different shapes, the calculation predicts an experimentally testable fingerprint of this component: it dilutes the deformation contribution at small $|\\Delta\\eta|$, so that the $V_{2\\Delta}(\\Delta\\eta)$ ratio between deformed and near-spherical systems, such as U+U-to-Au+Au, rises with $|\\Delta\\eta|$. Separating long- and short-range flow decorrelations from nonflow gives access to the longitudinal initial structure and dynamics of the QGP.","authors":"Jiangyong Jia, Shengli Huang, Chunjian Zhang, Somadutta Bhatta","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["nucl-th","hep-ph","nucl-ex"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.15006"},{"id":"2508.18022","title":"Precision Measurements of the Electroweak Mixing Angle in the Region of the Z pole at the Large Hadron Collider","summary":"In the Standard Model (SM), the effective leptonic weak mixing angle $\\sin^2\\theta^\\ell_{\\rm eff}$ can be predicted to very high accuracy by using the experimental values of the mass of the Z boson ($M_Z$), the Fermi constant, the fine structure constant, the mass of Higgs boson, the quark masses, and the value of the Quantum Chromodynamics (QCD) strong coupling ($\\alpha_s$) at $M_Z$. Therefore, deviations of measurements from SM predictions can be indications of new physics. With recent advances in experimental and analysis techniques, measurements of $\\sin^2\\theta^\\ell_{\\rm eff}$ at the Large Hadron Collider (LHC), extracted from the forward-backward asymmetry ($A_{\\rm FB}$) in Drell-Yan dilepton events, are now competitive with previously published measurements at $e^+e^-$ collider experiments. We report on a more precise extraction of the effective leptonic weak mixing angle via an updated analysis of the CMS Drell-Yan $A_{\\rm FB}$ measurements at 13 TeV. We obtain $\\sin^2\\theta^\\ell_{\\rm eff}$=0.23154$\\pm$0.00024 with additional PDF constraints from measurements of the W decay lepton charge asymmetry at 13 TeV and the W/Z cross section ratios. It is the most precise single experiment measurement to date and is in excellent agreement with the SM 2026 indirect prediction of 0.23152$\\pm$0.00006.","authors":"Arie Bodek, Hyon-San Seo, Un-Ki Yang","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.18022"},{"id":"2607.00079","title":"QFT as a set of ODEs: higher dimensions","summary":"Correlation functions of local operators in Quantum Field Theory (QFT) in Anti-de Sitter space (AdS) are completely fixed by the QFT data: the set of scaling dimensions $\\Delta_i$ and OPE coefficients $C_{ijk}$ of the boundary operators, and the bulk-boundary (BOE) coefficients $b^{\\hat\\Phi}_i$ encoding how bulk fields decompose into boundary operators. In this work, we generalize the ordinary differential equations (ODEs) that govern the variation of the QFT data under a bulk relevant deformation, originally derived for AdS$_2$ \\cite{Loparco:2026fki}, to the cases of AdS$_3$ and AdS$_4$. We demonstrate that these flow equations natively capture the mechanism of merger-annihilation when a boundary operator hits marginality, as well as level repulsion when different $\\Delta_i$'s approach each other. Furthermore, we address the practical implementation of the framework: we propose substituting the ODE for the OPE coefficients with the crossing equation for greater efficiency, and we observe that Pad\\'e approximants dramatically improve the convergence of the sums over boundary operators, at least in free theories. Altogether, these advances lay the groundwork for the future application of the flow equations to the study of strongly coupled QFTs in AdS and their flat space limits.","authors":"Fabiana De Cesare, Manuel Loparco","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.00079"},{"id":"2607.10403","title":"The muon Moonshot: Moon subsurface tomography with upward-going muons","summary":"We propose a novel muon Moonshot concept for lunar subsurface tomography based on upward-going muons originating from the lunar regolith. Unlike Earth, the Moon lacks an atmosphere, leaving dense regolith below and a near-vacuum environment above. Consequently, most downward-going hadrons are absorbed before decaying, while upward-going hadrons escaping the regolith can decay in flight, producing a significant flux of lunar muons. These muons can be detected by instruments on the lunar surface or in near-lunar orbit. We perform Monte Carlo simulations to investigate their energy spectra, angular distributions, and integrated fluxes under various theoretical and detector configurations. The results indicate that the lunar muon flux is sensitive to detector altitude under idealized geometry. This dependence demonstrates its potential as a novel, non-invasive probe of shallow subsurface voids. We also present case studies on the detection of underground cavities and water resources. For shallow overburden, large cavity signals remain detectable with estimated backgrounds and systematics. Weaker water signals remain challenging. Potential implementations in future lunar missions are also discussed.","authors":"Zimo Hu, Leyun Gao, Zhengyun You, Qite Li, Qiang Li, Yuhong Yu, Liangwen Chen, Xueheng Zhang, Zhiyu Sun","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.EP","astro-ph.IM","hep-ex","hep-ph","physics.space-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.10403"},{"id":"2611.01117","title":"LH$\\mu$C Concept: Energy-Frontier Antimuon-Proton Collisions at CERN","summary":"The concept of the LH$\\mu$C, an energy-frontier antimuon-proton collider based on the HL-LHC, is presented. After recalling why energy-frontier lepton-hadron colliders are indispensable as QCD explorers and reviewing the history of muon-proton collider proposals, two ways of realising $\\mu p$ collisions at the LHC are compared: with muon-collider beams and with ultra-cold $\\mu^+$ beams as proposed for $\\mu$TRISTAN. For the latter, a $\\mu$TRISTAN-type booster and the re-use of the LHeC energy-recovery-linac tunnel are discussed; colliding 1 TeV antimuons with 7 TeV protons gives $\\sqrt{s}=5.3$ TeV and peak luminosities of $8.8\\times10^{33}$ and $7.2\\times10^{33}$ cm$^{-2}$s$^{-1}$, respectively. The detector concept and the physics potential, including searches for colour-octet muons and R-parity-violating squarks, are discussed together with the main challenges.","authors":"Arda Goroglu, Saleh Sultansoy","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex","hep-ph","nucl-ex","physics.acc-ph"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01117"},{"id":"2611.22092","title":"Search for the low-mass isotropic signature of QCD instantons in $pp$ collisions at $\\sqrt{s} = 13$ TeV using the ATLAS detector","summary":"This Letter presents the first search for QCD instanton-induced events at the LHC. Instantons are semi-classical solutions of the Yang-Mills equations of QCD and correspond to non-perturbative tunneling between topologically distinct vacuum sectors. The search is performed in 1.6 $\\text{nb}^{-1}$ of very low instantaneous luminosity proton-proton collision data collected by the ATLAS detector at 13 TeV. Angular variables based on reconstructed charged-particle tracks are used to discriminate the expected rest-frame isotropy of low-mass instanton events from the minimum-bias background. The minimum-bias event background is predicted using a data-driven technique and reweighted Monte Carlo models. A profile-likelihood fit is performed in a two-dimensional classifier variable to extract a possible signal contribution, yielding an excess of low-mass isotropic events with an observed significance of $3.5\\sigma$.","authors":" ATLAS Collaboration","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22092"},{"id":"2610.20774","title":"Signatures of Leptophilic Baryogenesis at Muon Colliders","summary":"The baryon asymmetry of the Universe may be produced in a model with 3 Higgs fields, a Higgs Troika, without the need for a strong first order electroweak phase transition. The couplings of the new Higgs fields to Standard Model fermions are assumed in accord with the \"Spontaneous Flavor Violation (SFV)\" framework, in order to avoid large deviations from flavor physics constraints. We consider a leptophilic version of SFV and outline its potential signatures at future muon colliders, envisioned to be multi-TeV partonic center of momentum machines. Hence, they provide optimal venues to search for the leptophilic Higgs Troika baryogenesis mechanism. The projected reach for signals of the new scalars allows for probing the key components of the baryogenesis model at scales that accommodate cosmological requirements.","authors":"Hooman Davoudiasl, Ian M. Lewis, Miguel A. Soto Alcaraz","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","hep-ex"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20774"},{"id":"2512.23992","title":"Prospect for measurement of CP-violating parameters of $B_s^0 \\to \\phi\\gamma$ at the Tera Z factory","summary":"$b \\to s\\gamma$ transition is a critical flavor-changing neutral current (FCNC) process that could be used to probe CP violation (CPV) and new physics (NP). We quantify the anticipated precision for measuring $B_s^0 \\to \\phi\\gamma$ at the CEPC Z pole operation, showing that the relative statistical uncertainty could be as low as 0.16\\%, improved by approximately two orders of magnitude compared to existing measurements. Additionally, we perform a time-dependent analysis of the $B_s^0 \\to \\phi\\gamma$ decay, accounting for $B_s^0/\\bar{B}_s^0$ mixing extract the mixing-induced and CP-violating parameters $\\boldsymbol{\\mathcal{A}_{\\phi\\gamma}^\\Delta}$, $\\boldsymbol{C_{\\phi\\gamma}}$ and $\\boldsymbol{S_{\\phi\\gamma}}$. Using central value from LHCb measurement as input, we evaluate the anticipated accuracy of measurements of these parameters. The projected statistical uncertainties are $\\sigma_{A_{\\phi\\gamma}^{\\Delta}{}^{\\text{stat}}} = 0.021$, $\\sigma_C^{\\text{stat}} = 0.0092$ and $\\sigma_S^{\\text{stat}} = 0.0096$, and the systematic uncertainties are $\\sigma_{A_{\\phi\\gamma}^{\\Delta}{}^{\\text{syst}}} = 0.035$, $\\sigma_C^{\\text{syst}} = 0.0027$ and $\\sigma_S^{\\text{syst}} = 0.0064$. Furthermore, the 1$\\sigma$ sensitivity boundaries for NP in this study are found to be $\\mathcal{A}_{\\phi\\gamma}^\\Delta < -0.05$ or $\\mathcal{A}_{\\phi\\gamma}^\\Delta > 0.15$, $\\mathcal{C}_{\\phi\\gamma} < -0.02$ or $\\mathcal{C}_{\\phi\\gamma} > 0.04$, and $\\mathcal{S}_{\\phi\\gamma} < -0.04$ or $\\mathcal{S}_{\\phi\\gamma} > 0.04$. We also conduct a relevant detector optimization study by establishing the correlation between the anticipated precision and the intrinsic resolution of the ECAL, as well as the performance of the PID system.","authors":"Hengyu Wang, Hanhua Cui, Yongfeng Zhu, Hao Liang, Yuexin Wang, Kaili Zhang, Yi Wang, Weizheng Song, Lingfeng Li, Shanzhen Chen, Manqi Ruan","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ex"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.23992"},{"id":"2602.10658","title":"Inclusive and multiplicity-dependent pseudorapidity densities of charged particles in pp collisions at $\\mathbf{\\sqrt{s} = 13.6}$ TeV","summary":"The distribution of the pseudorapidity density of primary charged particles (${\\rm d}N_{\\rm ch}/ {\\rm d}\\eta$) in $|\\eta|<1.5$ is measured in pp collisions at a center-of-mass energy of 13.6 TeV using the ALICE detector. Tracks are reconstructed with the upgraded Inner Tracking System and Time Projection Chamber, and results are reported for events having at least one charged particle in $|\\eta|<1$. The multiplicity dependence of the pseudorapidity density of charged particles is also explored with the multiplicity determined by the forward detectors. The average charged-particle pseudorapidity density at midrapidity ($|\\eta|<0.5$) is measured to be $\\langle \\mathrm{d}N_{\\mathrm{ch}}/\\mathrm{d}\\eta \\rangle = 7.10 \\pm 0.20$. Combined with lower-energy measurements, the results follow a power-law scaling with the center-of-mass energy. These measurements provide a new reference for charged-particle production at the highest proton$-$proton collision energy available at the LHC.","authors":" ALICE Collaboration","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["nucl-ex","hep-ex"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.10658"},{"id":"2611.21860","title":"Anomaly inflow for simplicial K\\\"{a}hler fermions","summary":"We study massive K\\\"{a}hler Dirac fermions propagating on finite triangulations of various three dimensional manifolds with one or more boundaries. To probe the anomaly structure of these models we decompose the K\\\"{a}hler field into two reduced K\\\"{a}hler fields and choose boundary conditions that ensure that any edge modes are necessarily massless. We then show how to factorize the Pfaffian of this system into a product of bulk and boundary sectors each of which can separately pick up a phase under a global $Z_4$ transformation of the fermions that simultaneously changes the sign of the mass. These phases are topological, depending only on the index of the lattice K\\\"{a}hler operator in that sector. For the boundary sector a non-trivial phase signals the presence of a 't Hooft anomaly. We study cases where the boundary is anomalous but an inflow from the bulk cancels its phase. Canceling this anomaly for a non-orientable boundary in the absence of the bulk recovers the known classification of topological superconductors. We also give a continuum interpretation of these results in terms of inflow from a gravitational Chern Simons theory which is induced by integrating out massive bulk K\\\"{a}hler Dirac fermions. In the presence of a boundary the latter gives rise to Wen-Zee terms that mediate the anomaly inflow mechanism.","authors":"Simon Catterall","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-lat","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21860"},{"id":"2609.24861","title":"Quantum vacuum inversion and multi-kink unbinding in the non-degenerate double sine-Gordon model","summary":"Quantum fluctuations can unbind a composite soliton while the correction to its mass stays small. We show this beyond perturbation theory for the multi-kinks of the non-degenerate double sine-Gordon model. At one loop the vacuum at the centre of the multi-kink becomes the true vacuum at a coupling of order the classical lifting of the vacua. Tensor-network calculations of the lattice theory show that this inversion survives beyond one loop and has a continuum limit, and that the Gaussian effective potential captures it. On finite chains the multi-kink unbinds when the tension of the string of central vacuum between its two halves falls through zero","authors":"Jonathan Lozano-Mayo","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-th","cond-mat.str-el","hep-lat","nlin.PS"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.24861"},{"id":"2611.21743","title":"Center anomalies without twists: torsion, exact degeneracies, and condensates","summary":"Mixed center anomalies are usually exposed by 't Hooft's twisted boundary conditions in a Hamiltonian formalism. On $\\mathbb {RP}^3$, torsion makes the twist unnecessary: a center operator on the untwisted $SU(2)$ Hilbert space anticommutes with time reversal in Yang-Mills theory at $\\theta=\\pi$ and with the chiral symmetry of super Yang-Mills theory, so every level is at least doubly degenerate. The relevant semiclassical object is the BPST instanton, which descends to a charge-$\\tfrac12$ saddle on the orbifold $\\mathbb R^4/\\mathbb Z_2$. We complete it in two ways: applying a conformal transformation maps the orbifold to $\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb {RP}^3$, with the saddle describing the tunneling event between the two center sectors; by resolving the orbifold singularity, we obtain the Eguchi-Hanson space, which gives the gaugino condensate at its weak-coupling value.","authors":"Mohamed M. Anber","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["cond-mat.str-el","hep-lat","hep-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21743"},{"id":"2611.21726","title":"Not All Holographic Entropy Inequalities Are Free of Area Law Ultraviolet Divergences","summary":"In the AdS/CFT correspondence, linear constraints on subsystem entropies, which are necessary conditions for the validity of the Ryu-Takayanagi proposal, define the holographic entropy cone. We disprove a prior claim that all holographic constraints of this type are free of area law divergences (superbalanced). We analyze one explicit counterexample and explain how to easily find more; we report signatures of $\\mathcal{O}(10^5)$ counterexamples. Our findings invalidate multiple prior claims and conjectures.","authors":"Xin-Xiang Ju, Bartlomiej Czech","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21726"},{"id":"2611.21751","title":"From Top to Bottom: Quark Mass Hierarchies in Heterotic String Theory","summary":"Standard Model fermion masses exhibit large hierarchies, spanning more than five orders of magnitude in the quark sector alone. In this paper, we study whether these hierarchies can be generated perturbatively at tree level in smooth heterotic Calabi-Yau compactifications, where the supergravity approximation remains under computational control. In such backgrounds, physical Yukawa couplings combine holomorphic superpotential integrals with matter field Kahler metrics. The former can be computed algebraically, while the latter require solving a coupled system of partial differential equations for the Ricci-flat metric, Hermitian Yang-Mills connections, and harmonic bundle-valued forms. Within this framework, we identify two new tree-level mechanisms by which complex structure moduli can generate fermion mass hierarchies and show analytically and numerically that both mechanisms are obstructed for previously studied tetra-quadric models. Motivated by these obstructions, we scan the existing databases of tens of thousands of complete intersection Calabi-Yau line bundle models and find a unique smooth quotient admitting a rank-three up-quark Yukawa matrix at tree level. Evaluating the physical couplings on this manifold and evolving them under the renormalization group, we obtain realistic mass hierarchies for the top, charm, up, and bottom quarks in the smooth supergravity regime.","authors":"Yuri Chervonyi, Fabian Ruehle, Nenad Tomasev","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-th","cs.AI"],"topics":["qgravity","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21751"},{"id":"2611.21754","title":"Giant gravitons without giant gravitons","summary":"We demonstrate that the partition function of a free bosonic $U(N)$ multi-matrix model admits a giant graviton expansion. For $d$ matrices, there are $d$ distinct giant gravitons, and the expansion can be characterized by a $d$-node quiver with bifundamentals connecting the nodes. This suggests the intriguing possibility of a more generalized holography where models without any obvious gravitational dual can nevertheless be given a dual brane interpretation. The giant graviton expansion of this multi-matrix model is somewhat unconventional though, as the giant graviton partition functions will necessarily have intrinsic $N$-dependence.","authors":"Blake J. Bottesi, James T. Liu","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21754"},{"id":"2611.21787","title":"FLZ integrability in the 't Hooft model","summary":"We study the 't Hooft equation describing mesons in large-$N_c$ two-dimensional QCD with arbitrary unequal quark masses. Using its Fredholm formulation and the associated Baxter finite-difference equation, we investigate the relations between spectral determinants and canonical Baxter $Q$-functions. We derive special-point identities for the Baxter solutions by exploiting finite-rank commutator relations of the underlying integral operators. We also determine the spectral residues of the inhomogeneous solutions and obtain exact expressions for eigenfunction normalization factors in terms of values at distinguished complex points. These results establish the unit-residue property of the Baxter expressions entering the Fateev-Lukyanov-Zamolodchikov (FLZ) spectral-determinant identities. As a further application, we prove the nondegeneracy of the meson spectrum for arbitrary unequal quark masses. We further identify a broader class of integral operators exhibiting the same mechanism, which we refer to as FLZ integrability, and illustrate its applicability to the meson spectral problem in Ising field theory. Our approach reveals a connection between the analytic structure of Baxter equations and the theory of completely integrable Fredholm operators.","authors":"Alexey Litvinov, Egor Shestopalov, Nikita Slavnov","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-th","math-ph","math.MP"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21787"},{"id":"2611.22570","title":"Non-integrability of open strings via Pohlmeyer reduction","summary":"We study integrable and non-integrable boundary conditions for classical strings from the point of view of Pohlmeyer reduction. We first consider the integrable case of strings ending on Y=0 non-maximal giant gravitons. Pohlmeyer reduction of such strings leads to a sine-Gordon model, for which a criterion for boundary integrability was provided by Ghoshal and Zamolodchikov. We confirm that the Pohlmeyer field satisfies this condition. We then turn to the main focus of our work, which is open strings on AdS3. By evaluating the Ghoshal-Zamolodchikov criterion for the corresponding Pohlmeyer-reduced models, we are able to distinguish between integrable and non-integrable cases, in line with expectations from recent analytic and numerical studies. We conclude that Pohlmeyer reduction can provide a useful perspective on open-string non-integrability.","authors":"Divania Carelse, Laila Tribelhorn, Konstantinos Zoubos","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22570"},{"id":"2610.35818","title":"Holographic Approaches to Hadron Structure","summary":"We give an introduction to and an overview of holographic approaches to QCD and hadron structure. After an introduction to the string-theoretic foundations of holographic QCD, we review the predictions of top-down and bottom-up models for the phenomenology of mesons, baryons, and glueballs. We focus on the top-down model following from probe flavor branes in the Witten model of low-energy Yang-Mills theory and, in the more phenomenological approach of bottom-up models, mostly on minimal models based on five-dimensional anti-de Sitter space, which also implement global symmetries and their anomalies consistently, but allow the high-energy behavior to better match real QCD.","authors":"Luigi Cappiello, Anton Rebhan, Shigeki Sugimoto","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-ph","hep-th","nucl-th"],"topics":["particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.35818"},{"id":"2611.21368","title":"Astrophysical Constraints for Bosonic Dark Matter in and Around Quark Stars","summary":"We investigate the structure, stability, and mass-radius relations of compact stars composed of quark matter within the non-ideal MIT Bag Model mixed with self-interacting bosonic dark matter with a $\\phi^n$ self-interaction potential. By solving the coupled two-fluid Tolman-Oppenheimer-Volkoff equations for boson masses between 200 MeV and 1 GeV and self-interaction strengths with $n=4$ and $n=40$, we show that strong self-interactions and higher boson masses enhance the stability of compact star configurations at high central pressures, though they reduce maximum overall masses. Ultra-compact solutions with $C \\ge 1/3$ capable of mimicking black holes are significantly restricted compared to hadronic models, emerging only for strong self-interactions and dark matter fractions exceeding 90\\%. Depending on the parameters, the compact stars form either extended dark matter halos or compact cores: stronger self-interactions and higher boson masses suppress the radial extension to favor compact cores of dark matter. Matching our models against observational constraints highlights PSR J1614$-$2230 and XTE J1814$-$338 as potential window of opportunity for dark matter detection. Notably, XTE J1814$-$338 could confirm a dark sector, given its requirement for light boson masses ($m_b \\approx 200\\text{--}300\\text{ MeV}$) and strongly DM-dominated configurations.","authors":"J\\'ulia Tartera Barber\\`a, Sarah Louisa Pitz, J\\\"urgen Schaffner-Bielich, Laura Tolos","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["astro-ph.HE","hep-th","nucl-th"],"topics":["dark","particle"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21368"},{"id":"2402.10129","title":"Holographic covering and the fortuity of black holes","summary":"We propose a classification of BPS states in holographic CFTs into monotone and fortuitous, based on their behaviors in the large $N$ limit. Intuitively, monotone BPS states form infinite sequences with increasing rank $N$, while fortuitous ones exist within finite ranges of consecutive ranks. A precise definition is formulated using supercharge cohomology. We conjecture that under the AdS/CFT correspondence, monotone BPS states are dual to smooth horizonless geometries, and fortuitous ones are responsible for typical black hole microstates and give dominant contributions to the entropy. We present supporting evidence for our conjectures in the ${\\cal N}=4$ SYM and symmetric product orbifolds.","authors":"Chi-Ming Chang, Ying-Hsuan Lin","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.10129"},{"id":"2512.11754","title":"Modular Witten Diagrams and Quantum Extremality","summary":"We study entanglement entropy for ball-shaped regions in excited states of holographic conformal field theories. The excited states are prepared by the Euclidean path integral in the CFT with a source turned on for some double-trace operator, with a small, $O(1)$ amplitude $\\lambda$. On the gravity side, the double-trace operator deforms the bulk geometry as well as the entanglement structure of the state of bulk matter fields. By the quantum extremal surface formula, this leads to a deformation of the shape of the entanglement wedge, an effect which becomes manifest in the entanglement entropy at $O(\\lambda^2 G_N)$. On the CFT side, we explicitly calculate the entanglement entropy perturbatively in the source amplitude to $O(\\lambda^2)$, in terms of modular-flowed correlation functions of double-trace operators. We then evaluate these modular-flowed correlation functions using Witten diagrams. This calculation involves a Schwinger-Keldysh contour ordering prescription in the bulk, which we motivate using analytic continuation from Euclidean replica correlators. Focusing on a particular graviton-exchange diagram, we rewrite it in a form where it manifestly reproduces the canonical energy term present in the quantum Ryu-Takayanagi formula, including the shape deformation of the entanglement wedge due to backreaction and quantum effects.","authors":"Abhirup Bhattacharya, Onkar Parrikar","published":"2026-11-18","categories":["hep-th"],"topics":["qgravity"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.11754"}]}}};
